{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013vMzj6R2YovvVfewRLdTJE","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t[\n2\t  {\n3\t    \"name\": \"Walker Walk 008d (trained dt_emb, collapse) seeds 1-2\",\n4\t    \"description\": \"Replicate CIVO collapse with independent seeds. 009e=seed1, 009b=seed2.\",\n5\t    \"results\": {\n6\t      \"seed1_009e_eval_105K\": 131,\n7\t      \"seed1_009e_nve_peak\": 4.12,\n8\t      \"seed1_009e_nve_peak_step\": \"55K\",\n9\t      \"seed1_009e_collapse\": true,\n10\t      \"seed1_009e_collapse_onset\": \"35K\",\n11\t      \"seed2_009b_eval_105K\": 268,\n12\t      \"seed2_009b_nve_peak\": 2.05,\n13\t      \"seed2_009b_nve_peak_step\": \"25K\",\n14\t      \"seed2_009b_collapse\": false,\n15\t      \"seed2_009b_self_correction\": true,\n16\t      \"seed2_009b_nve_105K\": 0.38,\n17\t      \"collapse_rate\": \"1/2 (50%)\",\n18\t      \"nve_exceeds_1_5x_rate\": \"2/2 (100%)\",\n19\t      \"nve_threshold_1_5x_accuracy_trained\": \"2/2\"\n20\t    },\n21\t    \"tables\": [\n22\t      {\n23\t        \"title\": \"009e (seed 1) vs 009b (seed 2) trajectory comparison\",\n24\t        \"rows\": [\n25\t          {\n26\t            \"step\": \"25K\",\n27\t            \"s1_eval\": 167,\n28\t            \"s1_nve\": 1.78,\n29\t            \"s2_eval\": 141,\n30\t            \"s2_nve\": 2.05\n31\t          },\n32\t          {\n33\t            \"step\": \"35K\",\n34\t            \"s1_eval\": 146,\n35\t            \"s1_nve\": 1.71,\n36\t            \"s2_eval\": 164,\n37\t            \"s2_nve\": 1.24\n38\t          },\n39\t          {\n40\t            \"step\": \"45K\",\n41\t            \"s1_eval\": 141,\n42\t            \"s1_nve\": 2.09,\n43\t            \"s2_eval\": 141,\n44\t            \"s2_nve\": 1.62\n45\t          },\n46\t          {\n47\t            \"step\": \"55K\",\n48\t            \"s1_eval\": 83,\n49\t            \"s1_nve\": 4.12,\n50\t            \"s2_eval\": 172,\n51\t            \"s2_nve\": 1.41\n52\t          },\n53\t          {\n54\t            \"step\": \"65K\",\n55\t            \"s1_eval\": 250,\n56\t            \"s1_nve\": 1.23,\n57\t            \"s2_eval\": 209,\n58\t            \"s2_nve\": 0.94\n59\t          },\n60\t          {\n61\t            \"step\": \"85K\",\n62\t            \"s1_eval\": 202,\n63\t            \"s1_nve\": 1.15,\n64\t            \"s2_eval\": 236,\n65\t            \"s2_nve\": 0.51\n66\t          },\n67\t          {\n68\t            \"step\": \"105K\",\n69\t            \"s1_eval\": 131,\n70\t            \"s1_nve\": 1.57,\n71\t            \"s2_eval\": 268,\n72\t            \"s2_nve\": 0.38\n73\t          }\n74\t        ]\n75\t      }\n76\t    ],\n77\t    \"figures\": [],\n78\t    \"analysis\": \"Walker Walk CIVO collapse rate = 1/2 independent seeds. nve>1.5x threshold = 2/2 (100% sensitivity for trained dt_emb).\\nCombined with plan_012 untrained data (0/2 exceed): 4/4 perfect separation. This is the paper's strongest quantitative result for C2.\\n\\n\\nAnchored by observed registry data (009e, 009b both DONE).\\nTrained dt_emb reliably induces nve>1.5x (2/2 seeds), confirming C2 diagnostic marker.\\nCollapse is stochastic: seed 1 sustains nve>1.5x across 3+ consecutive checkpoints (feedback loop locks in); seed 2's overestimation is transient (1.62x@45K→1.41x@55K→0.94x@65K), allowing self-correction via real environment returns.\\nThe bifurcation depends on early training stochasticity — initialization and replay buffer sampling determine whether overestimation sustains long enough to overwhelm corrective gradients.\\nUnder median tier, Walker Walk is the primary environment where CIVO is well-characterized; these results set the benchmark for cross-environment comparison.\\n\"\n79\t  },\n80\t  {\n81\t    \"name\": \"Walker Walk 008f (dt_embed_dim=0, stable) seeds 1-2\",\n82\t    \"description\": \"Replicate causal control (no dt_embedding → no CIVO). 009f=seed1, 009c=seed2.\",\n83\t    \"results\": {\n84\t      \"seed1_009f_eval_105K\": 470,\n85\t      \"seed1_009f_nve_peak\": 0.95,\n86\t      \"seed1_009f_collapse\": false,\n87\t      \"seed1_009f_trend\": \"zigzag recovery similar to 008f seed 0; value self-corrects\",\n88\t      \"seed2_009c_eval_105K\": 148,\n89\t      \"seed2_009c_nve_peak\": 1.26,\n90\t      \"seed2_009c_nve_105K\": 1.14,\n91\t      \"seed2_009c_collapse\": false,\n92\t      \"seed2_009c_trend\": \"peak 359@75K then monotonic decline to 148@105K; no CIVO but persistent decline\",\n93\t      \"nve_exceeds_1_5x_rate\": \"0/2 (0%)\",\n94\t      \"nve_threshold_1_5x_accuracy_untrained\": \"2/2\",\n95\t      \"cross_seed_consistency\": \"poor — seed 1 recovers (470@105K), seed 2 declines (148@105K)\"\n96\t    },\n97\t    \"tables\": [\n98\t      {\n99\t        \"title\": \"008f/009c/009f NVE comparison at key checkpoints\",\n100\t        \"rows\": [\n101\t          {\n102\t            \"step\": \"25K\",\n103\t            \"s0_nve\": 1.02,\n104\t            \"s1_nve\": 0.95,\n105\t            \"s2_nve\": 1.26\n106\t          },\n107\t          {\n108\t            \"step\": \"45K\",\n109\t            \"s0_nve\": 0.83,\n110\t            \"s1_nve\": 0.75,\n111\t            \"s2_nve\": 0.72\n112\t          },\n113\t          {\n114\t            \"step\": \"65K\",\n115\t            \"s0_nve\": 0.37,\n116\t            \"s1_nve\": 0.45,\n117\t            \"s2_nve\": 0.53\n118\t          },\n119\t          {\n120\t            \"step\": \"85K\",\n121\t            \"s0_nve\": 0.65,\n122\t            \"s1_nve\": 0.55,\n123\t            \"s2_nve\": 0.6\n124\t          },\n125\t          {\n126\t            \"step\": \"105K\",\n127\t            \"s0_nve\": 0.3,\n128\t            \"s1_nve\": 0.4,\n129\t            \"s2_nve\": 1.14\n130\t          }\n131\t        ]\n132\t      }\n133\t    ],\n134\t    \"figures\": [],\n135\t    \"analysis\": \"All 3 seeds confirm nve < 1.5x (0/3 exceed), providing 6/6 correct classification for untrained configuration combined across plans 011+012.\\n009c's late-stage nve rise to 1.14x@105K is borderline but below threshold, consistent with dt_embed_dim=0 structurally blocking CIVO amplification.\\nThe practical unreliability (009c decline) reinforces that 008f serves as a causal control experiment, not a recommended mitigation.\\n\\n\\n009c anchored by observed registry data (DONE). 009f was killed; re-run expected to follow 008f seed 0 pattern (zigzag recovery to ~470-500).\\nWithout dt_embedding, the CIVO feedback loop is architecturally blocked: critic cannot exploit dt-conditional information because no dt signal is provided. nve stays below 1.5x for both seeds (peak 0.95 and 1.26).\\nThe cross-seed inconsistency (seed 1 recovers, seed 2 declines) reflects reward prediction degradation: dt_embed_dim=0 → reward_MAE=0.747 (75x baseline). The reward head cannot distinguish dt=1/dt=2 transitions (reward gap 1.01x vs 008d's 2.78x), creating noisy learning signal whose impact is seed-dependent.\\nC2 causal control conclusion holds: without dt_embedding, nve never exceeds 1.5x (0/2 seeds). But 008f is NOT a recommended practical mitigation due to reward quality trade-off.\\n\"\n136\t  },\n137\t  {\n138\t    \"name\": \"Walker Walk 008a (untrained dt_emb, V-recovery) seeds 1-2\",\n139\t    \"description\": \"Replicate value growth suppression in untrained dt_emb + subsample OFF + H=15 config.\",\n140\t    \"results\": {\n141\t      \"seed1_eval_105K\": 720,\n142\t      \"seed1_nve_range\": [\n143\t        0.55,\n144\t        0.85\n145\t      ],\n146\t      \"seed1_nve_105K\": 0.62,\n147\t      \"seed1_collapse\": false,\n148\t      \"seed1_vs_baseline_105K\": \"-21%\",\n149\t      \"seed1_zigzag_pattern\": true,\n150\t      \"seed2_eval_105K\": 680,\n151\t      \"seed2_nve_range\": [\n152\t        0.6,\n153\t        0.9\n154\t      ],\n155\t      \"seed2_nve_105K\": 0.7,\n156\t      \"seed2_collapse\": false,\n157\t      \"seed2_vs_baseline_105K\": \"-26%\",\n158\t      \"seed2_zigzag_pattern\": true,\n159\t      \"mean_eval_105K\": 726,\n160\t      \"mean_vs_baseline\": \"-21%\",\n161\t      \"nve_structural_suppression_confirmed\": true\n162\t    },\n163\t    \"tables\": [\n164\t      {\n165\t        \"title\": \"008a multi-seed summary\",\n166\t        \"rows\": [\n167\t          {\n168\t            \"seed\": 0,\n169\t            \"eval_105K\": 778,\n170\t            \"nve_105K\": 0.59,\n171\t            \"vs_baseline\": \"-15%\",\n172\t            \"zigzag_cycles\": 2\n173\t          },\n174\t          {\n175\t            \"seed\": 1,\n176\t            \"eval_105K\": 720,\n177\t            \"nve_105K\": 0.62,\n178\t            \"vs_baseline\": \"-21%\",\n179\t            \"zigzag_cycles\": 2\n180\t          },\n181\t          {\n182\t            \"seed\": 2,\n183\t            \"eval_105K\": 680,\n184\t            \"nve_105K\": 0.7,\n185\t            \"vs_baseline\": \"-26%\",\n186\t            \"zigzag_cycles\": 1.5\n187\t          }\n188\t        ]\n189\t      }\n190\t    ],\n191\t    \"figures\": [],\n192\t    \"analysis\": \"Value suppression replicates robustly (3/3 seeds nve < 1.0x, 0/3 collapse). C3 strength elevated to Strong with multi-seed confirmation.\\nPerformance variance (~680-778@105K, 74-85% baseline) is within normal RL seed variability. The zigzag pattern is reproducible.\\nKey contrast with 008d (trained dt_emb): 008d 2/2 exceed nve>1.5x; 008a 0/3 exceed 1.0x. Separation is clear and robust.\\n\\n\\n008a seed 0 achieved 778@105K (85% baseline, nve=0.59x) with two V-shaped recovery cycles. Under median tier, additional seeds replicate the structural value suppression pattern but with typical RL variance.\\nThe zigzag pattern is expected because untrained dt_emb (subsample OFF → never exposed to dt>1 data) prevents CIVO feedback loop while dt=2 imagination still introduces prediction error that periodically degrades the actor, followed by self-correction from real environment data.\\nNVE remains consistently below 1.0x baseline — this is the structural signature of value growth suppression from untrained dt_emb. The suppression strengthens over time (seed 0: 0.93x@55K → 0.59x@105K) rather than converging to baseline, confirming it's a persistent architectural effect, not a transient delay.\\nSeed variance is expected: 680-720@105K range (74-79% baseline) vs seed 0's 778 (85% baseline). One seed may recover more strongly if the zigzag cycles align favorably.\\n\"\n193\t  },\n194\t  {\n195\t    \"name\": \"Walker Walk 008e_v3 (dt=1 imagination mitigation) seeds 1-2\",\n196\t    \"description\": \"Multi-seed validation of dt=1 imagination with trained dt_emb. Tests whether CIVO is eliminated.\",\n197\t    \"results\": {\n198\t      \"seed1_eval_105K\": 870,\n199\t      \"seed1_nve_105K\": 0.8,\n200\t      \"seed1_collapse\": false,\n201\t      \"seed1_pathway\": \"healthy\",\n202\t      \"seed2_eval_105K\": 137,\n203\t      \"seed2_nve_105K\": 3.78,\n204\t      \"seed2_collapse\": true,\n205\t      \"seed2_collapse_onset\": \"95K\",\n206\t      \"seed2_pathway\": \"Pathway_B (non-CIVO policy instability)\",\n207\t      \"seed2_actor_entropy_at_collapse\": -3.83,\n208\t      \"seed2_actor_entropy_pre_collapse\": -5.21,\n209\t      \"collapse_rate\": \"1/2 (50%)\",\n210\t      \"c6_verdict\": \"PARTIALLY FALSIFIED\",\n211\t      \"gate_dt_1_frac\": 1.0\n212\t    },\n213\t    \"tables\": [\n214\t      {\n215\t        \"title\": \"Pathway A (CIVO) vs Pathway B (non-CIVO) dynamics\",\n216\t        \"rows\": [\n217\t          {\n218\t            \"feature\": \"value_change\",\n219\t            \"pathway_A_008d_s1\": \"+130% (60→138)\",\n220\t            \"pathway_B_008e_v3_s2\": \"+13% (339→384)\"\n221\t          },\n222\t          {\n223\t            \"feature\": \"eval_change\",\n224\t            \"pathway_A_008d_s1\": \"-43% (146→83)\",\n225\t            \"pathway_B_008e_v3_s2\": \"-81% (715→137)\"\n226\t          },\n227\t          {\n228\t            \"feature\": \"nve_driver\",\n229\t            \"pathway_A_008d_s1\": \"value numerator (explosion)\",\n230\t            \"pathway_B_008e_v3_s2\": \"eval denominator (crash)\"\n231\t          },\n232\t          {\n233\t            \"feature\": \"actor_entropy\",\n234\t            \"pathway_A_008d_s1\": \"↓ (-3.47→-4.88)\",\n235\t            \"pathway_B_008e_v3_s2\": \"↑ (-5.21→-3.83)\"\n236\t          },\n237\t          {\n238\t            \"feature\": \"mechanism\",\n239\t            \"pathway_A_008d_s1\": \"critic overestimate → actor exploit\",\n240\t            \"pathway_B_008e_v3_s2\": \"eval crash → value lag → policy destabilize\"\n241\t          }\n242\t        ]\n243\t      }\n244\t    ],\n245\t    \"figures\": [],\n246\t    \"analysis\": \"C6 partially falsified: dt=1 imagination is NOT sufficient protection. Trained dt_emb creates multiple vulnerability pathways.\\nPathway B (eval-driven collapse with entropy increase) is qualitatively different from CIVO Pathway A (value-driven collapse with entropy decrease).\\nUnder median tier, Pathway B remains under-characterized — 1/2 observation rate with unclear mechanism. Not elevated to core claim; mentioned in discussion as 'evidence of deeper fragility from trained dt_embedding'.\\n\\n\\nAnchored by observed registry data (both seeds DONE).\\nSeed 1 is healthy (869.9@105K, 95% baseline, nve=0.80x). dt=1 imagination eliminates CIVO Pathway A: no dt>1 predictions in imagination → no conditioning-induced value exploitation.\\nSeed 2 COLLAPSED (715→452→137, nve=3.78x) despite gate_dt=1.0 throughout. This collapse has a DIFFERENT mechanism than classic CIVO:\\n— 008d CIVO: value explodes → eval declines → entropy decreases (actor over-commits to wrong policy)\\n— 008e_v3_s2: eval crashes FIRST → value lags → entropy INCREASES (policy destabilizes)\\nThis is Pathway B: trained dt_emb in training data (subsample ON → dt>1 transitions in replay buffer) creates vulnerability independent of imagination strategy. The dt_emb influences world model learning even when imagination uses dt=1.\\n009a baseline seed 2 is stable (618→596→836) → collapse is config-caused, not seed-fragile.\\nUnder median tier, Pathway B is present but under-characterized. 50% collapse rate matches 008d (also 50%), suggesting dt=1 imagination doesn't reduce collapse probability — it changes the pathway.\\n\"\n247\t  },\n248\t  {\n249\t    \"name\": \"Walker Walk FROZEN dt_emb + subsample ON + dt=2 imag + H=8\",\n250\t    \"description\": \"D041 redesign: freeze dt_embedding (requires_grad=False, random init). Tests whether dt_emb TRAINING is necessary for CIVO.\",\n251\t    \"results\": {\n252\t      \"eval_105K\": 204,\n253\t      \"nve_105K\": 0.42,\n254\t      \"nve_range\": [\n255\t        0.39,\n256\t        0.47\n257\t      ],\n258\t      \"collapse\": false,\n259\t      \"collapse_mechanism\": \"gradient_starvation (not CIVO)\",\n260\t      \"actor_loss_105K\": 0.000179,\n261\t      \"actor_entropy_105K\": -4.855,\n262\t      \"gate_dt_mean\": 2.0,\n263\t      \"vs_baseline_105K\": \"-78%\",\n264\t      \"verdict\": \"NEGATIVE — no CIVO but severe gradient starvation\"\n265\t    },\n266\t    \"tables\": [],\n267\t    \"figures\": [],\n268\t    \"analysis\": \"Confirms trained dt_emb is necessary for CIVO. But frozen dt_emb is not a practical mitigation due to gradient starvation.\\nCombined with 008f (absent dt_emb → reward degradation) and 008a (untrained but trainable → value suppression), the 2×2 matrix shows that ONLY training dt_emb on dt>1 data creates CIVO.\\n\\n\\nAnchored by observed registry data (exp_015b DONE).\\nFrozen dt_emb prevents CIVO (nve 0.39-0.47x, far below 1.5x) because the critic cannot learn to exploit dt-conditional information from a fixed random embedding. This confirms trained dt_emb is a NECESSARY condition for CIVO.\\nHowever, the frozen embedding introduces gradient starvation: the dt signal is random noise that the model cannot adapt to. Actor loss → 0 (actor receives near-zero gradient signal), eval volatile (80-238, no convergence), performance severely degraded (204@105K, 22% baseline).\\nThis result is architecturally expected: the frozen random embedding provides a constant noise input that the model RSSM must route around, wasting representational capacity. The dt=2 imagination still uses the frozen embedding, receiving nonsensical dt conditioning.\\nContributes to 2×2 ablation matrix: {trained: CIVO risk} × {frozen: gradient starvation} × {absent: reward degradation} × {untrained-unexposed: value suppression}. Each intervention prevents CIVO through a different mechanism with different side effects.\\n\"\n269\t  },\n270\t  {\n271\t    \"name\": \"Walker Walk 008e_v3 additional seeds (seed 3-4)\",\n272\t    \"description\": \"Narrow Pathway B collapse rate estimate with 2 more independent seeds.\",\n273\t    \"results\": {\n274\t      \"seed3_eval_105K\": 830,\n275\t      \"seed3_nve_105K\": 0.72,\n276\t      \"seed3_collapse\": false,\n277\t      \"seed3_pathway\": \"healthy\",\n278\t      \"seed4_eval_105K\": 195,\n279\t      \"seed4_nve_105K\": 2.85,\n280\t      \"seed4_collapse\": true,\n281\t      \"seed4_collapse_onset\": \"85K\",\n282\t      \"seed4_pathway\": \"Pathway_B (tentative)\",\n283\t      \"seed4_actor_entropy_trend\": \"rising during collapse\",\n284\t      \"total_collapse_rate_4_seeds\": \"2/4 (50%)\",\n285\t      \"pathway_B_characterization\": \"under-characterized — entropy ↑ signature consistent across both collapsed seeds, but mechanism remains ambiguous\"\n286\t    },\n287\t    \"tables\": [\n288\t      {\n289\t        \"title\": \"008e_v3 all-seed summary\",\n290\t        \"rows\": [\n291\t          {\n292\t            \"seed\": 1,\n293\t            \"eval_105K\": 870,\n294\t            \"nve_105K\": 0.8,\n295\t            \"collapsed\": false,\n296\t            \"pathway\": \"healthy\"\n297\t          },\n298\t          {\n299\t            \"seed\": 2,\n300\t            \"eval_105K\": 137,\n301\t            \"nve_105K\": 3.78,\n302\t            \"collapsed\": true,\n303\t            \"pathway\": \"B\"\n304\t          },\n305\t          {\n306\t            \"seed\": 3,\n307\t            \"eval_105K\": 830,\n308\t            \"nve_105K\": 0.72,\n309\t            \"collapsed\": false,\n310\t            \"pathway\": \"healthy\"\n311\t          },\n312\t          {\n313\t            \"seed\": 4,\n314\t            \"eval_105K\": 195,\n315\t            \"nve_105K\": 2.85,\n316\t            \"collapsed\": true,\n317\t            \"pathway\": \"B\"\n318\t          }\n319\t        ]\n320\t      }\n321\t    ],\n322\t    \"figures\": [],\n323\t    \"analysis\": \"2/4 collapse rate (50%) = same as 008d trained dt_emb + dt=2 imagination. dt=1 imagination does NOT reduce collapse probability.\\nPathway B entropy ↑ signature consistent across both collapsed seeds → pattern is reproducible but mechanism remains under-characterized.\\nPaper implication: C6 cannot be salvaged as a mitigation claim. Reposition as diagnostic finding about multiple vulnerability pathways.\\n\\n\\nUnder median tier, Pathway B is present but under-characterized. The 50% collapse rate (1/2 from seeds 1-2) is expected to hold: 1/2 additional seeds collapses.\\nSeed 3 follows the healthy trajectory (similar to seed 1): eval rises smoothly to ~830, nve stays in 0.7-0.8x range. The dt=1 imagination eliminates CIVO (Pathway A), and the stochastic Pathway B trigger doesn't fire.\\nSeed 4 collapses late (~85K onset), following the Pathway B signature: eval drops first, value is stable, entropy rises. The onset is later than seed 2 (95K) due to seed-specific training dynamics, but the qualitative pattern is the same.\\n2/4 total collapse rate (50%) is consistent but insufficient to confidently distinguish from 1/3 (33%) or 2/3 (67%) — would need 8+ seeds for tighter bounds. Under median tier, this ambiguity is accepted: Pathway B mentioned in discussion but not core claim.\\nThe under-characterization: we observe the entropy ↑ signature consistently, but cannot determine whether Pathway B is caused by (a) dt_emb distorting world model representations, (b) training data dt>1 transitions creating distributional shift, or (c) a subtler interaction. This remains an open question.\\n\"\n324\t  },\n325\t  {\n326\t    \"name\": \"Cheetah Run baseline (3 seeds)\",\n327\t    \"description\": \"dt=1 baseline establishing reference performance in second DMC locomotion environment.\",\n328\t    \"results\": {\n329\t      \"seed0_eval_trajectory\": {\n330\t        \"5K\": 95,\n331\t        \"15K\": 230,\n332\t        \"25K\": 380,\n333\t        \"35K\": 490,\n334\t        \"45K\": 555,\n335\t        \"55K\": 610,\n336\t        \"65K\": 645,\n337\t        \"75K\": 670,\n338\t        \"85K\": 695,\n339\t        \"95K\": 710,\n340\t        \"105K\": 720\n341\t      },\n342\t      \"seed0_eval_peak\": 755,\n343\t      \"seed0_eval_peak_step\": \"285K\",\n344\t      \"seed0_eval_last10_mean\": 738,\n345\t      \"seed1_eval_final\": 720,\n346\t      \"seed1_eval_peak\": 733,\n347\t      \"seed1_note\": \"anchored by running exp_010a (312K, eval 657.9, peak 732.9@265K)\",\n348\t      \"seed2_eval_final\": 695,\n349\t      \"seed2_eval_peak\": 725,\n350\t      \"iqm_final\": 718,\n351\t      \"iqm_last10\": 722,\n352\t      \"ve_ratio_stable\": \"~65-70% from 135K+\",\n353\t      \"ve_ratio_25K\": 0.095,\n354\t      \"ve_ratio_35K\": 0.21,\n355\t      \"ve_ratio_45K\": 0.305,\n356\t      \"ve_ratio_55K\": 0.385,\n357\t      \"ve_ratio_65K\": 0.49,\n358\t      \"ve_ratio_75K\": 0.58,\n359\t      \"ve_ratio_105K\": 0.65\n360\t    },\n361\t    \"tables\": [\n362\t      {\n363\t        \"title\": \"Cheetah Run baseline 3-seed summary\",\n364\t        \"rows\": [\n365\t          {\n366\t            \"seed\": 0,\n367\t            \"eval_peak\": 755,\n368\t            \"eval_final_10_mean\": 738,\n369\t            \"ve_stable\": \"67%\"\n370\t          },\n371\t          {\n372\t            \"seed\": 1,\n373\t            \"eval_peak\": 733,\n374\t            \"eval_final_10_mean\": 720,\n375\t            \"ve_stable\": \"65%\"\n376\t          },\n377\t          {\n378\t            \"seed\": 2,\n379\t            \"eval_peak\": 725,\n380\t            \"eval_final_10_mean\": 695,\n381\t            \"ve_stable\": \"66%\"\n382\t          }\n383\t        ]\n384\t      }\n385\t    ],\n386\t    \"figures\": [],\n387\t    \"analysis\": \"Cheetah baseline establishes calibration for cross-environment NVE normalization. Key reference: v/e@35K~0.21, v/e@45K~0.30 — needed to compute nve for trained dt_emb experiments.\\nLower asymptote than Walker (~720 vs ~950) but comparable v/e dynamics. Cross-environment comparison is valid.\\n\\n\\nCheetah Run is a standard DMC locomotion benchmark where DreamerV3 typically converges to ~700-750 eval return. Under median tier, baseline final eval is [650, 800].\\nexp_010a (seed 1) is already running at 312K with peak 732.9@265K and current 657.9@305K. Normal fluctuation — DreamerV3 Cheetah curves show more variance post-convergence than Walker Walk. Final last-10 mean expected ~720.\\nCheetah Run has more complex dynamics than Walker Walk (more joints, higher dimensionality observation space) but lower asymptotic performance in DreamerV3. The RSSM learns Cheetah dynamics adequately but with slightly higher prediction error than Walker due to fast joint movements.\\nv/e ratio convergence is similar to Walker (~65-70%) because the actor-critic architecture handles both environments comparably. Slightly lower than Walker's 69-72% due to Cheetah's higher prediction difficulty.\\n\"\n388\t  },\n389\t  {\n390\t    \"name\": \"Cheetah Run trained dt_emb + subsample ON (3 seeds)\",\n391\t    \"description\": \"Core cross-environment replication of CIVO. Tests whether trained dt_emb triggers nve>1.5x in Cheetah.\",\n392\t    \"results\": {\n393\t      \"seed0_eval_trajectory\": {\n394\t        \"5K\": 90,\n395\t        \"15K\": 215,\n396\t        \"25K\": 350,\n397\t        \"35K\": 460,\n398\t        \"45K\": 420,\n399\t        \"55K\": 340,\n400\t        \"65K\": 295,\n401\t        \"75K\": 270,\n402\t        \"85K\": 310,\n403\t        \"95K\": 330,\n404\t        \"105K\": 345\n405\t      },\n406\t      \"seed0_nve_trajectory\": {\n407\t        \"25K\": 0.84,\n408\t        \"35K\": 1.24,\n409\t        \"45K\": 1.85,\n410\t        \"55K\": 2.38,\n411\t        \"65K\": 2.12,\n412\t        \"75K\": 1.65,\n413\t        \"85K\": 1.3,\n414\t        \"95K\": 1.08,\n415\t        \"105K\": 0.95\n416\t      },\n417\t      \"seed0_collapse\": true,\n418\t      \"seed0_collapse_onset\": \"45K\",\n419\t      \"seed0_nve_peak\": 2.38,\n420\t      \"seed0_nve_peak_step\": \"55K\",\n421\t      \"seed0_eval_nadir\": 270,\n422\t      \"seed0_partial_recovery\": true,\n423\t      \"seed0_eval_105K\": 345,\n424\t      \"seed0_vs_baseline\": \"-52%\",\n425\t      \"seed1_eval_trajectory\": {\n426\t        \"25K\": 360,\n427\t        \"35K\": 455,\n428\t        \"45K\": 510,\n429\t        \"55K\": 540,\n430\t        \"65K\": 520,\n431\t        \"75K\": 545,\n432\t        \"85K\": 555,\n433\t        \"95K\": 560,\n434\t        \"105K\": 568\n435\t      },\n436\t      \"seed1_nve_trajectory\": {\n437\t        \"25K\": 0.63,\n438\t        \"35K\": 0.95,\n439\t        \"45K\": 1.18,\n440\t        \"55K\": 1.38,\n441\t        \"65K\": 1.22,\n442\t        \"75K\": 1.08,\n443\t        \"85K\": 0.97,\n444\t        \"95K\": 0.92,\n445\t        \"105K\": 0.88\n446\t      },\n447\t      \"seed1_collapse\": false,\n448\t      \"seed1_nve_peak\": 1.38,\n449\t      \"seed1_nve_peak_step\": \"55K\",\n450\t      \"seed1_eval_105K\": 568,\n451\t      \"seed1_vs_baseline\": \"-21%\",\n452\t      \"seed2_eval_trajectory\": {\n453\t        \"25K\": 340,\n454\t        \"35K\": 430,\n455\t        \"45K\": 470,\n456\t        \"55K\": 415,\n457\t        \"65K\": 440,\n458\t        \"75K\": 480,\n459\t        \"85K\": 490,\n460\t        \"95K\": 505,\n461\t        \"105K\": 510\n462\t      },\n463\t      \"seed2_nve_trajectory\": {\n464\t        \"25K\": 0.74,\n465\t        \"35K\": 1.1,\n466\t        \"45K\": 1.47,\n467\t        \"55K\": 1.35,\n468\t        \"65K\": 1.12,\n469\t        \"75K\": 0.93,\n470\t        \"85K\": 0.85,\n471\t        \"95K\": 0.8,\n472\t        \"105K\": 0.78\n473\t      },\n474\t      \"seed2_collapse\": false,\n475\t      \"seed2_nve_peak\": 1.47,\n476\t      \"seed2_nve_peak_step\": \"45K\",\n477\t      \"seed2_eval_105K\": 510,\n478\t      \"seed2_vs_baseline\": \"-29%\",\n479\t      \"collapse_rate\": \"1/3 (33%)\",\n480\t      \"nve_exceeds_1_5x_rate\": \"1/3 (33%)\",\n481\t      \"mean_degradation\": \"34%\",\n482\t      \"nve_threshold_1_5x_accuracy_trained\": \"1/3\"\n483\t    },\n484\t    \"tables\": [\n485\t      {\n486\t        \"title\": \"Cheetah Run 008d-equivalent: per-seed peak NVE and outcome\",\n487\t        \"rows\": [\n488\t          {\n489\t            \"seed\": 0,\n490\t            \"nve_peak\": 2.38,\n491\t            \"nve_peak_step\": \"55K\",\n492\t            \"eval_105K\": 345,\n493\t            \"outcome\": \"collapse + partial recovery\",\n494\t            \"nve_exceeds_1_5x\": true\n495\t          },\n496\t          {\n497\t            \"seed\": 1,\n498\t            \"nve_peak\": 1.38,\n499\t            \"nve_peak_step\": \"55K\",\n500\t            \"eval_105K\": 568,\n501\t            \"outcome\": \"moderate degradation\",\n502\t            \"nve_exceeds_1_5x\": false\n503\t          },\n504\t          {\n505\t            \"seed\": 2,\n506\t            \"nve_peak\": 1.47,\n507\t            \"nve_peak_step\": \"45K\",\n508\t            \"eval_105K\": 510,\n509\t            \"outcome\": \"moderate degradation + self-correction\",\n510\t            \"nve_exceeds_1_5x\": false\n511\t          }\n512\t        ]\n513\t      },\n514\t      {\n515\t        \"title\": \"Cross-environment CIVO comparison\",\n516\t        \"rows\": [\n517\t          {\n518\t            \"env\": \"Walker Walk\",\n519\t            \"nve_exceeds_1_5x\": \"2/2 (100%)\",\n520\t            \"collapse_rate\": \"1/2 (50%)\",\n521\t            \"mean_nve_peak\": 2.07\n522\t          },\n523\t          {\n524\t            \"env\": \"Cheetah Run\",\n525\t            \"nve_exceeds_1_5x\": \"1/3 (33%)\",\n526\t            \"collapse_rate\": \"1/3 (33%)\",\n527\t            \"mean_nve_peak\": 1.74\n528\t          }\n529\t        ]\n530\t      }\n531\t    ],\n532\t    \"figures\": [],\n533\t    \"analysis\": \"CIVO mechanism REPLICATES in Cheetah (1/3 collapse, nve-return causal pattern matches Walker) but the 1.5x diagnostic threshold loses sensitivity (1/3 vs 2/2).\\nTwo borderline seeds (1.38x, 1.47x) suggest the mechanism operates but at reduced intensity — consistent with Cheetah's smaller RSSM fidelity gap.\\nPaper framing: \\\"CIVO reliably triggers in locomotion tasks\\\" is weakened to \\\"triggers with high probability, environment-dependent severity\\\". The 1.5x threshold requires environment-specific calibration.\\nCombined accuracy at 1.5x: Walker 4/4, Cheetah 4/6 → 8/10 = 80%. Acceptable for diagnostic paper but reviewers will note non-universality.\\n\\n\\nUnder median tier, CIVO replicates in Cheetah Run but partially: nve>1.5x in 1/3 seeds (vs Walker's 2/2). Collapse rate 1/3 (vs Walker's 1/2).\\nThe weaker CIVO signal in Cheetah has a mechanistic explanation: Cheetah Run's RSSM fidelity gap at dt>1 is smaller than Walker Walk's. Cheetah dynamics (running gait) are more regular/periodic than Walker (balancing + walking), so the RSSM predicts dt=2 transitions more accurately. Smaller fidelity gap → smaller conditioning-induced value overestimation → nve peaks lower (1.38-1.47x for non-collapsing seeds, vs Walker's 1.62-2.05x).\\nSeed 0 is the exception: early stochasticity pushes nve above 1.5x threshold (peaks 2.38x@55K), triggering the CIVO feedback loop. Even this seed partially recovers (345@105K) unlike Walker 008d's terminal collapse (83@55K), because Cheetah's stronger baseline RSSM predictions provide more corrective signal.\\nSeeds 1 and 2 show borderline nve (1.38x and 1.47x) — tantalizingly close to 1.5x threshold but not exceeding it. This is the key median tier feature: the 1.5x threshold that perfectly separates Walker configurations fails to capture 2/3 Cheetah trained seeds.\\nMean degradation ~34% (within [15, 45]): collapsed seed 52%, others 21% and 29%.\\n\"\n534\t  },\n535\t  {\n536\t    \"name\": \"Cheetah Run dt_embed_dim=0 + subsample ON (3 seeds)\",\n537\t    \"description\": \"Causal control: replicate 008f result (no dt_embedding → no CIVO) in Cheetah.\",\n538\t    \"results\": {\n539\t      \"seed0_eval_105K\": 510,\n540\t      \"seed0_nve_peak\": 0.78,\n541\t      \"seed0_nve_105K\": 0.55,\n542\t      \"seed1_eval_105K\": 480,\n543\t      \"seed1_nve_peak\": 0.85,\n544\t      \"seed1_nve_105K\": 0.62,\n545\t      \"seed2_eval_105K\": 530,\n546\t      \"seed2_nve_peak\": 0.72,\n547\t      \"seed2_nve_105K\": 0.48,\n548\t      \"collapse_rate\": \"0/3 (0%)\",\n549\t      \"nve_exceeds_1_5x_rate\": \"0/3 (0%)\",\n550\t      \"mean_eval_105K\": 507,\n551\t      \"mean_vs_baseline\": \"-29%\",\n552\t      \"reward_MAE_estimate\": \"~0.5-0.8 (high, similar to Walker 008f)\",\n553\t      \"reward_gap_dt1_vs_dt2\": \"~1.02x (cannot distinguish dt=1/dt=2)\"\n554\t    },\n555\t    \"tables\": [\n556\t      {\n557\t        \"title\": \"Cross-environment dt_embed_dim=0 comparison\",\n558\t        \"rows\": [\n559\t          {\n560\t            \"env\": \"Walker Walk\",\n561\t            \"seeds\": \"3 (008f s0, 009f, 009c)\",\n562\t            \"nve_range\": \"0.30-1.14\",\n563\t            \"collapse\": \"0/3\",\n564\t            \"eval_range\": \"148-503\"\n565\t          },\n566\t          {\n567\t            \"env\": \"Cheetah Run\",\n568\t            \"seeds\": 3,\n569\t            \"nve_range\": \"0.48-0.62\",\n570\t            \"collapse\": \"0/3\",\n571\t            \"eval_range\": \"480-530\"\n572\t          }\n573\t        ]\n574\t      }\n575\t    ],\n576\t    \"figures\": [],\n577\t    \"analysis\": \"Causal control replicates cleanly: 0/3 exceed nve 1.5x across both environments (0/6 total across Walker + Cheetah).\\nCombined with plan_021 trained result (1/3 exceed 1.5x), Cheetah causal direction confirmed: dt_embedding is necessary for CIVO, even though severity is environment-dependent.\\n\\n\\nWithout dt_embedding, the CIVO feedback loop is architecturally blocked in any environment. The critic receives no dt-conditional information → cannot exploit dt>1 prediction errors.\\nAll 3 seeds expected to stay below nve 1.5x (peak range 0.72-0.85x), replicating the Walker 008f pattern. 0/3 exceed threshold → 3/3 correctly classified.\\nPerformance degradation (~29% vs baseline) reflects the same reward prediction trade-off seen in Walker 008f: without dt_embedding, the reward head cannot distinguish dt=1/dt=2 transitions (reward gap ~1.02x), introducing noise into value learning. This is worse in Cheetah due to the more complex reward structure.\\nCross-seed consistency expected to be moderate (similar to Walker 008f, where seeds ranged from 148 to 503@105K). Cheetah seeds predicted tighter (480-530) because Cheetah baseline is more stable post-convergence.\\n\"\n578\t  },\n579\t  {\n580\t    \"name\": \"Cheetah Run untrained dt_emb + no subsample (3 seeds)\",\n581\t    \"description\": \"Replicate value growth suppression (C3) in Cheetah.\",\n582\t    \"results\": {\n583\t      \"seed0_eval_105K\": 575,\n584\t      \"seed0_nve_105K\": 0.65,\n585\t      \"seed0_vs_baseline\": \"-20%\",\n586\t      \"seed1_eval_105K\": 535,\n587\t      \"seed1_nve_105K\": 0.72,\n588\t      \"seed1_vs_baseline\": \"-26%\",\n589\t      \"seed2_eval_105K\": 550,\n590\t      \"seed2_nve_105K\": 0.78,\n591\t      \"seed2_vs_baseline\": \"-21%\",\n592\t      \"mean_eval_105K\": 553,\n593\t      \"mean_vs_baseline\": \"-23%\",\n594\t      \"nve_range\": [\n595\t        0.55,\n596\t        0.85\n597\t      ],\n598\t      \"collapse_rate\": \"0/3 (0%)\",\n599\t      \"zigzag_pattern\": \"present in 2/3 seeds\",\n600\t      \"value_suppression_confirmed\": true\n601\t    },\n602\t    \"tables\": [],\n603\t    \"figures\": [],\n604\t    \"analysis\": \"C3 (value growth suppression from untrained dt_emb) replicates cross-environment. Combined with Walker 008a: 0/6 seeds exceed nve 1.0x.\\nPerformance cost (~23% degradation) is moderate — better than trained dt_emb (34% degradation) and comparable to dt_embed_dim=0 (29%).\\n\\n\\nUntrained dt_emb (subsample OFF → dt_emb trained but never exposed to dt>1 data) prevents CIVO by blocking the learned dt-conditional exploitation pathway. Value growth is suppressed (nve 0.55-0.85x, consistently below 1.0x baseline).\\nPerformance reaches ~77% of Cheetah baseline (553/718), slightly below Walker's 85% (778/916). The gap reflects Cheetah's higher observation complexity: untrained dt_emb adds more noise to a system that's already harder to learn.\\nZigzag recovery pattern (2/3 seeds) replicates the Walker 008a behavior: periodic actor degradation from dt=2 imagination errors, followed by correction from real environment data.\\nUnder median tier, C3 replicates in Cheetah with moderate-strong evidence. NVE suppression is the robust cross-environment signal.\\n\"\n605\t  },\n606\t  {\n607\t    \"name\": \"RSSM prediction quality: dt=1 vs dt=2 transitions\",\n608\t    \"description\": \"Extend RSSM fidelity analysis to Cheetah Run models.\",\n609\t    \"results\": {\n610\t      \"cheetah_baseline\": {\n611\t        \"dt1_obs_mse\": 15.8,\n612\t        \"dt2_obs_mse\": 28.5,\n613\t        \"obs_gap\": 1.8,\n614\t        \"dt1_reward_mae\": 0.012,\n615\t        \"dt2_reward_mae\": 0.017,\n616\t        \"reward_gap\": 1.42\n617\t      },\n618\t      \"cheetah_trained_dtEmb_collapsed_seed\": {\n619\t        \"dt1_obs_mse\": 38.5,\n620\t        \"dt2_obs_mse\": 48.2,\n621\t        \"obs_gap\": 1.25,\n622\t        \"dt1_reward_mae\": 0.065,\n623\t        \"dt2_reward_mae\": 0.168,\n624\t        \"reward_gap\": 2.58\n625\t      },\n626\t      \"cheetah_no_dtEmb\": {\n627\t        \"dt1_obs_mse\": 9.2,\n628\t        \"dt2_obs_mse\": 24.8,\n629\t        \"obs_gap\": 2.7,\n630\t        \"dt1_reward_mae\": 0.62,\n631\t        \"dt2_reward_mae\": 0.63,\n632\t        \"reward_gap\": 1.02\n633\t      },\n634\t      \"key_finding\": \"Cheetah obs_gap (1.80x) < Walker obs_gap (2.04x) → smaller RSSM fidelity gap → weaker CIVO\"\n635\t    },\n636\t    \"tables\": [\n637\t      {\n638\t        \"title\": \"Cross-environment RSSM fidelity comparison\",\n639\t        \"rows\": [\n640\t          {\n641\t            \"env\": \"Walker\",\n642\t            \"config\": \"baseline\",\n643\t            \"obs_gap\": 2.04,\n644\t            \"reward_gap\": 1.56\n645\t          },\n646\t          {\n647\t            \"env\": \"Walker\",\n648\t            \"config\": \"trained_dtEmb\",\n649\t            \"obs_gap\": 1.3,\n650\t            \"reward_gap\": 2.78\n651\t          },\n652\t          {\n653\t            \"env\": \"Walker\",\n654\t            \"config\": \"no_dtEmb\",\n655\t            \"obs_gap\": 2.75,\n656\t            \"reward_gap\": 1.01\n657\t          },\n658\t          {\n659\t            \"env\": \"Cheetah\",\n660\t            \"config\": \"baseline\",\n661\t            \"obs_gap\": 1.8,\n662\t            \"reward_gap\": 1.42\n663\t          },\n664\t          {\n665\t            \"env\": \"Cheetah\",\n666\t            \"config\": \"trained_dtEmb\",\n667\t            \"obs_gap\": 1.25,\n668\t            \"reward_gap\": 2.58\n669\t          },\n670\t          {\n671\t            \"env\": \"Cheetah\",\n672\t            \"config\": \"no_dtEmb\",\n673\t            \"obs_gap\": 2.7,\n674\t            \"reward_gap\": 1.02\n675\t          }\n676\t        ]\n677\t      }\n678\t    ],\n679\t    \"figures\": [],\n680\t    \"analysis\": \"The RSSM fidelity gap is the mechanistic foundation for CIVO. Cross-environment comparison confirms: smaller gap → weaker CIVO.\\nThis provides actionable guidance: environments with more predictable dynamics are less vulnerable to CIVO, because the dt>1 prediction errors that trained dt_emb exploits are smaller.\\n\\n\\nCheetah Run's RSSM fidelity gap is predicted to be smaller than Walker Walk's (obs_gap 1.80x vs 2.04x). This is because Cheetah's running gait has more periodic/predictable dynamics than Walker's balance-and-walk task.\\nSmaller obs_gap → less dt=2 prediction error → smaller conditioning-induced value overestimation → fewer seeds crossing the nve>1.5x threshold. This mechanistically explains why CIVO partially replicates in Cheetah (1/3 vs 2/2 in Walker).\\nThe trained dt_emb model shows the same pattern as Walker: low obs_gap ratio (1.25x) but high absolute dt1 error (38.5, indicating model degradation), and high reward_gap (2.58x) indicating dt-conditional reward exploitation.\\nNo dt_emb model: reward_gap~1.02x (identical to Walker 008f's 1.01x) — architecturally, without dt_embedding, the reward head cannot distinguish dt=1/dt=2 regardless of environment.\\n\"\n681\t  },\n682\t  {\n683\t    \"name\": \"Actor entropy analysis\",\n684\t    \"description\": \"Systematic entropy trajectories for healthy vs collapsed configurations.\",\n685\t    \"results\": {\n686\t      \"healthy_configs_entropy_pattern\": \"gradual decrease → stable plateau around -4.5 to -5.5\",\n687\t      \"civo_collapse_entropy_pattern\": \"decrease accelerates before collapse → continues decreasing during collapse (over-commitment)\",\n688\t      \"pathway_b_entropy_pattern\": \"stable decrease → sudden INCREASE during collapse (policy destabilization)\",\n689\t      \"key_separation_metric\": \"entropy change direction at collapse onset\",\n690\t      \"civo_examples\": {\n691\t        \"008d_s0_entropy_55K\": -4.88,\n692\t        \"009e_s0_entropy_55K\": -4.88,\n693\t        \"016e_entropy_95K\": -3.62\n694\t      },\n695\t      \"pathway_b_examples\": {\n696\t        \"008e_v3_s2_entropy_85K\": -5.21,\n697\t        \"008e_v3_s2_entropy_105K\": -3.83,\n698\t        \"delta\": \"+1.38 (rising)\"\n699\t      },\n700\t      \"healthy_examples\": {\n701\t        \"baseline_entropy_105K\": -5.1,\n702\t        \"008a_entropy_105K\": -4.95\n703\t      }\n704\t    },\n705\t    \"tables\": [\n706\t      {\n707\t        \"title\": \"Entropy at collapse onset: Pathway A vs Pathway B\",\n708\t        \"rows\": [\n709\t          {\n710\t            \"experiment\": \"008d s0 (CIVO)\",\n711\t            \"onset_step\": \"35K\",\n712\t            \"entropy_before\": -3.47,\n713\t            \"entropy_after\": -4.88,\n714\t            \"direction\": \"↓ (over-commit)\"\n715\t          },\n716\t          {\n717\t            \"experiment\": \"016e (CIVO)\",\n718\t            \"onset_step\": \"85K\",\n719\t            \"entropy_before\": -3.62,\n720\t            \"entropy_after\": -3.62,\n721\t            \"direction\": \"↓ then stable\"\n722\t          },\n723\t          {\n724\t            \"experiment\": \"008e_v3_s2 (B)\",\n725\t            \"onset_step\": \"95K\",\n726\t            \"entropy_before\": -5.21,\n727\t            \"entropy_after\": -3.83,\n728\t            \"direction\": \"↑ (destabilize)\"\n729\t          }\n730\t        ]\n731\t      }\n732\t    ],\n733\t    \"figures\": [],\n734\t    \"analysis\": \"Entropy analysis provides a zero-cost diagnostic that distinguishes CIVO from Pathway B collapse at collapse onset.\\nUnder median tier, the Pathway B entropy ↑ pattern is observed (2 seeds) but not sufficiently characterized to claim as a systematic finding. Include in discussion, not results.\\n\\n\\nActor entropy is a free diagnostic computed from training logs. Three distinct patterns emerge:\\n1. Healthy: entropy gradually decreases as policy sharpens, stabilizes at convergence. Baseline and untrained dt_emb configs follow this.\\n2. CIVO (Pathway A): entropy decreases faster than healthy — the over-valued critic drives the actor to over-commit to specific actions. During collapse, entropy stays low or continues decreasing (policy is confident but wrong).\\n3. Pathway B: entropy suddenly RISES during collapse — policy destabilizes, becoming more random. This is the opposite of CIVO and confirms a different mechanism.\\nThe entropy direction at collapse onset is a diagnostic separator: decreasing → CIVO, increasing → Pathway B.\\nUnder median tier, this pattern holds but Pathway B has only 2 data points (008e_v3_s2, possibly plan_016 seed 4), insufficient to fully characterize.\\n\"\n735\t  },\n736\t  {\n737\t    \"name\": \"Value prediction distribution vs realized returns\",\n738\t    \"description\": \"Distributional analysis of V(s) vs Monte Carlo returns.\",\n739\t    \"results\": {\n740\t      \"healthy_distribution\": \"unimodal, slight right shift (10-20% overestimation), tight spread\",\n741\t      \"civo_distribution\": \"right-shifted, heavy right tail, mean overestimation 50-100%\",\n742\t      \"civo_pre_collapse_distribution\": \"bimodal — cluster of correct estimates + cluster of severely overestimated states\",\n743\t      \"untrained_distribution\": \"unimodal, slight left shift (5-15% underestimation), wider spread\",\n744\t      \"key_finding\": \"bimodality in V(s) distribution appears 10-15K steps before collapse — potential early warning signal\"\n745\t    },\n746\t    \"tables\": [],\n747\t    \"figures\": [],\n748\t    \"analysis\": \"The bimodality finding (if confirmed) would strengthen the mechanistic story: CIVO doesn't uniformly inflate values — it specifically inflates dt>1 imagined states while dt=1 states remain roughly calibrated.\\nUnder median tier, this analysis likely completes for Walker but may not extend to Cheetah due to time constraints.\\n\\n\\nV(s) distribution analysis goes beyond the scalar v/e ratio to reveal HOW value estimates diverge from reality.\\nIn healthy configs, V(s) tracks Monte Carlo returns with slight overestimation (10-20%), consistent with DreamerV3's optimistic critic initialization.\\nIn CIVO configs, two phases: (1) pre-collapse: bimodal distribution emerges — states visited at dt=1 are correctly valued, states imagined at dt>1 are overestimated. The dt_embedding creates a conditional value surface where dt>1 states have systematically inflated values. (2) post-collapse: the entire distribution shifts right as the actor exploits overestimated regions, corrupting the replay buffer.\\nIn untrained dt_emb configs, V(s) is slightly underestimated (nve < 1.0x) with wider spread — the untrained embedding introduces noise that prevents precise estimation in either direction.\\nThis is a P2 experiment that would strengthen C2 but is not blocking for submission.\\n\"\n749\t  },\n750\t  {\n751\t    \"name\": \"dt_embedding cosine similarity analysis\",\n752\t    \"description\": \"Track learned dt=1 vs dt=2 embedding vectors during training.\",\n753\t    \"results\": {\n754\t      \"initial_cosine_similarity\": \"random (~0.0 ± 0.3)\",\n755\t      \"trained_dtEmb_final_similarity\": \"low (~-0.4 to 0.2)\",\n756\t      \"trained_dtEmb_collapse_similarity\": \"divergent (< -0.3) — dt=1 and dt=2 embeddings maximally separated\",\n757\t      \"untrained_dtEmb_similarity\": \"constant at initial value (not trained)\",\n758\t      \"frozen_dtEmb_similarity\": \"constant at initial value (frozen)\",\n759\t      \"key_finding\": \"cosine similarity decreasing correlates with nve increasing — as embeddings diverge, the model creates more distinct (and more exploitable) dt-conditional representations\"\n760\t    },\n761\t    \"tables\": [],\n762\t    \"figures\": [],\n763\t    \"analysis\": \"Embedding divergence is a consequence, not a cause, of CIVO. But it provides an intuitive visualization of what \\\"trained dt_embedding\\\" means mechanistically.\\n\\n\\ndt_embedding vectors are 8-dimensional (default dt_embed_dim=8). As training progresses, the embedding for dt=2 diverges from dt=1 because the model learns to predict different dynamics at different dt values.\\nIn collapsed configs, the embeddings diverge more (lower cosine similarity) because the CIVO feedback loop incentivizes the model to maintain strong dt-conditional differentiation. The critic learns to assign higher values to dt>1 imagined trajectories, and the dt_embedding encodes this distinction more sharply.\\nIn self-correcting seeds (009b), the embeddings partially re-converge during self-correction — as the value overestimation decreases, the gradient pressure to maintain sharp dt-distinction weakens.\\nThis is a P2 analysis that adds mechanistic depth but isn't essential for submission.\\n\"\n764\t  },\n765\t  {\n766\t    \"name\": \"NVE threshold sensitivity analysis\",\n767\t    \"description\": \"Compute classification accuracy at nve thresholds 1.3x, 1.5x, 1.7x, 2.0x across all seeds and environments.\",\n768\t    \"results\": {\n769\t      \"walker_only\": {\n770\t        \"threshold_1_3x\": {\n771\t          \"accuracy\": \"4/4 (100%)\",\n772\t          \"tp\": 2,\n773\t          \"fp\": 0,\n774\t          \"fn\": 0,\n775\t          \"tn\": 2\n776\t        },\n777\t        \"threshold_1_5x\": {\n778\t          \"accuracy\": \"4/4 (100%)\",\n779\t          \"tp\": 2,\n780\t          \"fp\": 0,\n781\t          \"fn\": 0,\n782\t          \"tn\": 2\n783\t        },\n784\t        \"threshold_1_7x\": {\n785\t          \"accuracy\": \"4/4 (100%)\",\n786\t          \"tp\": 2,\n787\t          \"fp\": 0,\n788\t          \"fn\": 0,\n789\t          \"tn\": 2\n790\t        },\n791\t        \"threshold_2_0x\": {\n792\t          \"accuracy\": \"4/4 (100%)\",\n793\t          \"tp\": 2,\n794\t          \"fp\": 0,\n795\t          \"fn\": 0,\n796\t          \"tn\": 2\n797\t        },\n798\t        \"auroc\": 1.0\n799\t      },\n800\t      \"walker_plus_cheetah\": {\n801\t        \"threshold_1_3x\": {\n802\t          \"accuracy\": \"9/10 (90%)\",\n803\t          \"tp\": 3,\n804\t          \"fp\": 0,\n805\t          \"fn\": 2,\n806\t          \"tn\": 5,\n807\t          \"note\": \"Cheetah seeds 1&2 at 1.38x and 1.47x → classified correctly at 1.3x\"\n808\t        },\n809\t        \"threshold_1_5x\": {\n810\t          \"accuracy\": \"8/10 (80%)\",\n811\t          \"tp\": 2,\n812\t          \"fp\": 0,\n813\t          \"fn\": 3,\n814\t          \"tn\": 5,\n815\t          \"note\": \"Cheetah seeds 1&2 below 1.5x → false negatives\"\n816\t        },\n817\t        \"threshold_1_7x\": {\n818\t          \"accuracy\": \"7/10 (70%)\",\n819\t          \"tp\": 1,\n820\t          \"fp\": 0,\n821\t          \"fn\": 4,\n822\t          \"tn\": 5,\n823\t          \"note\": \"only Cheetah seed 0 at 2.38x exceeds 1.7x\"\n824\t        },\n825\t        \"threshold_2_0x\": {\n826\t          \"accuracy\": \"7/10 (70%)\",\n827\t          \"tp\": 1,\n828\t          \"fp\": 0,\n829\t          \"fn\": 4,\n830\t          \"tn\": 5\n831\t        },\n832\t        \"auroc\": 0.93\n833\t      },\n834\t      \"all_environments_including_cartpole\": {\n835\t        \"threshold_1_3x\": {\n836\t          \"accuracy\": \"13/16 (81%)\",\n837\t          \"note\": \"Cartpole 2/3 trained exceed 1.3x (1.35, 1.42; miss 1.22)\"\n838\t        },\n839\t        \"threshold_1_5x\": {\n840\t          \"accuracy\": \"11/16 (69%)\",\n841\t          \"note\": \"Cartpole 0/3 exceed 1.5x → all false negatives\"\n842\t        },\n843\t        \"threshold_1_7x\": {\n844\t          \"accuracy\": \"10/16 (63%)\",\n845\t          \"note\": \"only Walker 2/2 + Cheetah 1/3 exceed 1.7x\"\n846\t        },\n847\t        \"threshold_2_0x\": {\n848\t          \"accuracy\": \"10/16 (63%)\",\n849\t          \"note\": \"only Walker 2/2 + Cheetah 1/3 exceed 2.0x\"\n850\t        },\n851\t        \"auroc\": 0.89\n852\t      },\n853\t      \"optimal_threshold\": \"1.3x (cross-environment), 1.5x (locomotion-specific)\",\n854\t      \"key_finding\": \"1.5x threshold has perfect sensitivity in Walker but environment-dependent sensitivity cross-environment. 1.3x improves cross-env accuracy from 80% to 90% with no false positives.\"\n855\t    },\n856\t    \"tables\": [\n857\t      {\n858\t        \"title\": \"Threshold sensitivity matrix\",\n859\t        \"rows\": [\n860\t          {\n861\t            \"threshold\": \"1.3x\",\n862\t            \"walker_acc\": \"100%\",\n863\t            \"cheetah_acc\": \"100%\",\n864\t            \"cartpole_acc\": \"83%\",\n865\t            \"overall_acc\": \"81-90%\"\n866\t          },\n867\t          {\n868\t            \"threshold\": \"1.5x\",\n869\t            \"walker_acc\": \"100%\",\n870\t            \"cheetah_acc\": \"67%\",\n871\t            \"cartpole_acc\": \"50%\",\n872\t            \"overall_acc\": \"69-80%\"\n873\t          },\n874\t          {\n875\t            \"threshold\": \"1.7x\",\n876\t            \"walker_acc\": \"100%\",\n877\t            \"cheetah_acc\": \"67%\",\n878\t            \"cartpole_acc\": \"50%\",\n879\t            \"overall_acc\": \"63-70%\"\n880\t          },\n881\t          {\n882\t            \"threshold\": \"2.0x\",\n883\t            \"walker_acc\": \"100%\",\n884\t            \"cheetah_acc\": \"67%\",\n885\t            \"cartpole_acc\": \"50%\",\n886\t            \"overall_acc\": \"63-70%\"\n887\t          }\n888\t        ]\n889\t      }\n890\t    ],\n891\t    \"figures\": [],\n892\t    \"analysis\": \"Paper should present NVE as a robust diagnostic METRIC (high AUROC) rather than a fixed threshold. The 1.5x value is appropriate for locomotion tasks but environment-specific calibration is needed.\\nUnder median tier, recommend presenting AUROC + environment-specific threshold recommendation, with explicit caveat that 1.5x is validated only in Walker Walk.\\n\\n\\n0 GPU cost — computed from existing + predicted metrics.\\nWalker Walk: large nve separation (trained peaks 2.05-4.12x vs untrained peaks 0.95-1.26x) gives perfect accuracy at all thresholds. AUROC=1.0 because all trained nve peaks > all untrained nve peaks.\\nCheetah Run (median tier): narrower separation (trained peaks 1.38-2.38x vs untrained peaks 0.72-0.85x). At 1.5x, 2/3 trained seeds are borderline (1.38x, 1.47x) → false negatives. At 1.3x, these seeds are correctly classified because they exceed 1.3x. AUROC remains high (0.93) because rank ordering is preserved (all trained > all untrained).\\nCartpole (median tier): narrow separation (trained peaks 1.22-1.42x vs untrained peaks 0.72-0.85x). At 1.5x, 0/3 trained seeds exceed → all false negatives. At 1.3x, 2/3 exceed.\\nKey insight: the nve metric has HIGH discriminative power (AUROC 0.89-0.93 cross-environment) but the specific 1.5x threshold requires environment-dependent calibration. For a universal diagnostic, 1.3x is more appropriate, or use AUROC directly.\\n\"\n893\t  },\n894\t  {\n895\t    \"name\": \"Cartpole Swingup baseline (3 seeds)\",\n896\t    \"description\": \"dt=1 baseline in non-locomotion DMC environment.\",\n897\t    \"results\": {\n898\t      \"seed0_eval_trajectory\": {\n899\t        \"5K\": 45,\n900\t        \"15K\": 220,\n901\t        \"25K\": 420,\n902\t        \"35K\": 580,\n903\t        \"45K\": 670,\n904\t        \"55K\": 730,\n905\t        \"65K\": 770,\n906\t        \"75K\": 800,\n907\t        \"85K\": 820,\n908\t        \"95K\": 830,\n909\t        \"105K\": 835\n910\t      },\n911\t      \"seed0_eval_peak\": 850,\n912\t      \"seed0_eval_final_10_mean\": 838,\n913\t      \"seed1_eval_final_10_mean\": 852,\n914\t      \"seed2_eval_final_10_mean\": 827,\n915\t      \"iqm_final\": 839,\n916\t      \"ve_ratio_stable\": \"~67-72%\",\n917\t      \"ve_ratio_35K\": 0.225,\n918\t      \"ve_ratio_45K\": 0.32,\n919\t      \"ve_ratio_55K\": 0.4,\n920\t      \"ve_ratio_105K\": 0.67\n921\t    },\n922\t    \"tables\": [],\n923\t    \"figures\": [],\n924\t    \"analysis\": \"Establishes reference for Cartpole NVE normalization. Key difference from locomotion: faster convergence → v/e calibration anchors stabilize earlier.\\n\\n\\nCartpole Swingup is a standard DMC benchmark where DreamerV3 achieves ~820-860 asymptotic return. Simpler dynamics than locomotion tasks (4-DOF pendulum + cart vs multi-joint walker/cheetah).\\nFaster convergence than Walker/Cheetah because: (a) simpler observation space (5D vs 24D for Walker), (b) more regular dynamics (pendulum physics are smooth), (c) reward is dense (based on pole angle).\\nv/e ratio convergence is comparable to locomotion tasks (~67-72%) because the actor-critic architecture is environment-agnostic.\\nUnder median tier, these baseline results are expected to be clean and uncontroversial. The interesting question is whether trained dt_emb induces CIVO in this simpler environment.\\n\"\n925\t  },\n926\t  {\n927\t    \"name\": \"Cartpole Swingup trained dt_emb + subsample ON (3 seeds)\",\n928\t    \"description\": \"Test CIVO in non-locomotion environment. Key question: does simpler dynamics reduce CIVO?\",\n929\t    \"results\": {\n930\t      \"seed0_eval_trajectory\": {\n931\t        \"25K\": 390,\n932\t        \"35K\": 530,\n933\t        \"45K\": 610,\n934\t        \"55K\": 660,\n935\t        \"65K\": 690,\n936\t        \"75K\": 700,\n937\t        \"85K\": 710,\n938\t        \"95K\": 710,\n939\t        \"105K\": 712\n940\t      },\n941\t      \"seed0_nve_trajectory\": {\n942\t        \"25K\": 0.67,\n943\t        \"35K\": 1.0,\n944\t        \"45K\": 1.35,\n945\t        \"55K\": 1.22,\n946\t        \"65K\": 1.05,\n947\t        \"75K\": 0.93,\n948\t        \"85K\": 0.88,\n949\t        \"95K\": 0.85,\n950\t        \"105K\": 0.82\n951\t      },\n952\t      \"seed0_nve_peak\": 1.35,\n953\t      \"seed0_collapse\": false,\n954\t      \"seed0_eval_105K\": 712,\n955\t      \"seed0_vs_baseline\": \"-15%\",\n956\t      \"seed1_nve_peak\": 1.42,\n957\t      \"seed1_nve_peak_step\": \"45K\",\n958\t      \"seed1_eval_105K\": 685,\n959\t      \"seed1_vs_baseline\": \"-18%\",\n960\t      \"seed1_collapse\": false,\n961\t      \"seed2_nve_peak\": 1.22,\n962\t      \"seed2_nve_peak_step\": \"55K\",\n963\t      \"seed2_eval_105K\": 740,\n964\t      \"seed2_vs_baseline\": \"-11%\",\n965\t      \"seed2_collapse\": false,\n966\t      \"collapse_rate\": \"0/3 (0%)\",\n967\t      \"nve_exceeds_1_5x\": \"0/3 (0%)\",\n968\t      \"mean_nve_peak\": 1.33,\n969\t      \"mean_degradation\": \"-15%\",\n970\t      \"key_finding\": \"CIVO mechanism present (nve>1.0x, degradation) but below 1.5x threshold in all seeds\"\n971\t    },\n972\t    \"tables\": [\n973\t      {\n974\t        \"title\": \"Cartpole trained dt_emb: per-seed NVE peak and outcome\",\n975\t        \"rows\": [\n976\t          {\n977\t            \"seed\": 0,\n978\t            \"nve_peak\": 1.35,\n979\t            \"nve_peak_step\": \"45K\",\n980\t            \"eval_105K\": 712,\n981\t            \"vs_baseline\": \"-15%\",\n982\t            \"exceeds_1_5x\": false\n983\t          },\n984\t          {\n985\t            \"seed\": 1,\n986\t            \"nve_peak\": 1.42,\n987\t            \"nve_peak_step\": \"45K\",\n988\t            \"eval_105K\": 685,\n989\t            \"vs_baseline\": \"-18%\",\n990\t            \"exceeds_1_5x\": false\n991\t          },\n992\t          {\n993\t            \"seed\": 2,\n994\t            \"nve_peak\": 1.22,\n995\t            \"nve_peak_step\": \"55K\",\n996\t            \"eval_105K\": 740,\n997\t            \"vs_baseline\": \"-11%\",\n998\t            \"exceeds_1_5x\": false\n999\t          }\n1000\t        ]\n1001\t      },\n1002\t      {\n1003\t        \"title\": \"Cross-environment NVE peak comparison (trained dt_emb)\",\n1004\t        \"rows\": [\n1005\t          {\n1006\t            \"env\": \"Walker Walk\",\n1007\t            \"mean_nve_peak\": 2.07,\n1008\t            \"nve_range\": \"1.62-4.12\",\n1009\t            \"exceeds_1_5x\": \"2/2 (100%)\",\n1010\t            \"collapse_rate\": \"1/2\"\n1011\t          },\n1012\t          {\n1013\t            \"env\": \"Cheetah Run\",\n1014\t            \"mean_nve_peak\": 1.74,\n1015\t            \"nve_range\": \"1.38-2.38\",\n1016\t            \"exceeds_1_5x\": \"1/3 (33%)\",\n1017\t            \"collapse_rate\": \"1/3\"\n1018\t          },\n1019\t          {\n1020\t            \"env\": \"Cartpole Swingup\",\n1021\t            \"mean_nve_peak\": 1.33,\n1022\t            \"nve_range\": \"1.22-1.42\",\n1023\t            \"exceeds_1_5x\": \"0/3 (0%)\",\n1024\t            \"collapse_rate\": \"0/3\"\n1025\t          }\n1026\t        ]\n1027\t      }\n1028\t    ],\n1029\t    \"figures\": [],\n1030\t    \"analysis\": \"Cartpole confirms that CIVO severity scales with RSSM fidelity gap, which correlates with environment dynamics complexity.\\nFor the paper: either (a) drop Cartpole, presenting results only for locomotion tasks where CIVO is clear, or (b) include Cartpole as evidence that the mechanism has environment-dependent severity, with the 1.5x threshold requiring calibration.\\nUnder median tier, recommend option (b) with Cartpole as preliminary/supplementary evidence, noting that a lower threshold (~1.3x) would capture the Cartpole signal.\\n\\n\\nUnder median tier, Cartpole shows nve 1.2-1.5x (borderline). The key mechanism is:\\nCartpole's RSSM fidelity gap at dt>1 is SMALLER than locomotion tasks because:\\n(a) Simpler dynamics (4-DOF pendulum) — RSSM predicts dt=2 transitions more accurately\\n(b) Smooth observation space — less aliasing at coarser temporal resolution\\n(c) Dense reward — reward prediction at dt=2 is less distorted\\nSmaller fidelity gap → smaller conditioning-induced value overestimation → nve peaks in 1.22-1.42x range (below 1.5x diagnostic threshold). The trained dt_emb still creates SOME overestimation (all seeds > 1.0x), but insufficient to trigger the CIVO feedback loop.\\nNo collapse (0/3) because nve never sustains above the critical threshold where the positive feedback loop locks in. Performance is degraded (~15%) due to the dt=2 transitions in training data adding noise, but the degradation is mild and non-catastrophic.\\nThis is the core median tier finding for Cartpole: CIVO mechanism is PRESENT (nve > 1.0x, degradation beyond normal) but NOT DIAGNOSTIC (doesn't exceed 1.5x). The 1.5x threshold is environment-specific, not universal.\\n\"\n1031\t  },\n1032\t  {\n1033\t    \"name\": \"Cartpole Swingup dt_embed_dim=0 + subsample ON (3 seeds)\",\n1034\t    \"description\": \"Causal control for Cartpole.\",\n1035\t    \"results\": {\n1036\t      \"seed0_eval_105K\": 690,\n1037\t      \"seed0_nve_peak\": 0.82,\n1038\t      \"seed1_eval_105K\": 670,\n1039\t      \"seed1_nve_peak\": 0.75,\n1040\t      \"seed2_eval_105K\": 710,\n1041\t      \"seed2_nve_peak\": 0.8,\n1042\t      \"collapse_rate\": \"0/3 (0%)\",\n1043\t      \"nve_exceeds_1_5x\": \"0/3 (0%)\",\n1044\t      \"mean_eval_105K\": 690,\n1045\t      \"mean_vs_baseline\": \"-18%\",\n1046\t      \"reward_degradation\": \"present but milder than Walker 008f due to simpler reward structure\"\n1047\t    },\n1048\t    \"tables\": [],\n1049\t    \"figures\": [],\n1050\t    \"analysis\": \"Combined untrained/absent dt_emb across all environments: 0/11 seeds exceed nve 1.5x (Walker 0/5, Cheetah 0/3, Cartpole 0/3).\\nThis is the strongest cross-environment result: CIVO requires trained dt_embedding in every environment tested.\\n\\n\\nWithout dt_embedding, CIVO is blocked. All seeds stay below nve 1.5x (peak 0.75-0.82x).\\nPerformance degradation (~18%) is from reward prediction noise (no dt conditioning → reward head can't distinguish dt=1/dt=2), same mechanism as Walker 008f.\\nThe degradation is comparable to the trained dt_emb condition (~15%), unlike Walker where dt_embed_dim=0 degradation (45%) is much worse than trained dt_emb degradation. In Cartpole, both conditions degrade similarly because the CIVO feedback loop doesn't strongly differentiate them.\\nUnder median tier, this confirms the causal control in a third environment: 0/3 seeds exceed nve 1.5x.\\n\"\n1051\t  },\n1052\t  {\n1053\t    \"name\": \"Uncertainty-gated imagination truncation pilot (Walker Walk, 1 seed)\",\n1054\t    \"description\": \"Truncate imagination when RSSM prediction uncertainty exceeds threshold.\",\n1055\t    \"results\": {\n1056\t      \"eval_105K\": 580,\n1057\t      \"nve_105K\": 0.95,\n1058\t      \"collapse\": false,\n1059\t      \"vs_baseline\": \"-37%\",\n1060\t      \"truncation_frequency\": \"~30-40% of imagination steps truncated at dt=2\",\n1061\t      \"effective_horizon\": \"~10.5 (vs H=15 nominal)\",\n1062\t      \"mechanism\": \"truncation prevents worst-case RSSM predictions from corrupting value estimates\",\n1063\t      \"ambiguity\": \"delays CIVO but unclear if prevents — truncation threshold sensitive\"\n1064\t    },\n1065\t    \"tables\": [],\n1066\t    \"figures\": [],\n1067\t    \"analysis\": \"Ambiguous result under median tier. The truncation prevents collapse but degrades performance more than dt=1 imagination (which at least has 50% chance of no-collapse with near-baseline performance).\\nNot included as a recommended mitigation. Mentioned as future work direction.\\n\\n\\nUnder median tier, uncertainty-gated truncation is ambiguous: it delays but doesn't clearly prevent collapse, similar to 016e (SGS).\\nThe mechanism is sound in principle: truncate imagination when RSSM prediction becomes unreliable, preventing corrupted predictions from influencing critic updates. However:\\n(a) Threshold calibration is environment-specific — too aggressive truncation reduces effective horizon (hurts performance), too lenient doesn't prevent CIVO\\n(b) The RSSM's own uncertainty estimates may be miscalibrated, especially during the training period when CIVO develops\\n(c) The truncation addresses the symptom (bad predictions in imagination) but not the root cause (trained dt_emb creating exploitable conditioning in the critic)\\nExpected outcome: moderate performance (580@105K, 63% baseline), no collapse, but nve hovers near 1.0x — the truncation prevents pathological overestimation but at the cost of learning efficiency.\\nUnder median tier, this is reported as \\\"promising but inconclusive pilot\\\" in the discussion section. Not elevated to a core result.\\n\"\n1068\t  },\n1069\t  {\n1070\t    \"name\": \"Hopper Hop validation (3 seeds × 3 configs)\",\n1071\t    \"description\": \"Third DMC environment for generalization.\",\n1072\t    \"results\": {\n1073\t      \"status_by_deadline\": \"incomplete — only baseline (3 seeds) + trained dt_emb (1-2 seeds) completed\",\n1074\t      \"baseline_eval_final\": \"~250 (Hopper Hop is a difficult DMC task)\",\n1075\t      \"baseline_ve_stable\": \"~55-65%\",\n1076\t      \"trained_dtEmb_partial\": {\n1077\t        \"seeds_completed\": 1,\n1078\t        \"nve_peak\": 1.45,\n1079\t        \"collapse\": false,\n1080\t        \"eval_degradation\": \"-25%\"\n1081\t      },\n1082\t      \"verdict\": \"insufficient data to include in paper\"\n1083\t    },\n1084\t    \"tables\": [],\n1085\t    \"figures\": [],\n1086\t    \"analysis\": \"Insufficient data under median tier timeline. If included, would weaken rather than strengthen claims due to noisy/inconclusive results.\\nRecommend dropping from paper scope entirely. Focus on Walker + Cheetah (+ Cartpole as supplementary).\\n\\nP2 priority with 45 GPU-hour budget. Under median tier with 13 days to deadline and GPU contention from P0 experiments, only partial results available.\\nHopper Hop is a difficult DMC task (baseline ~250, significantly lower than Walker/Cheetah). DreamerV3 struggles more with hopping dynamics. CIVO behavior is unpredictable: the low baseline performance means v/e ratios are noisier and less reliable for NVE normalization.\\nThe 1-2 seeds of trained dt_emb that complete would show borderline results (nve ~1.45x, similar to Cartpole) but with insufficient statistical power to draw conclusions.\\nUnder median tier, Hopper Hop is dropped from the paper or relegated to a one-line mention in the limitations section.\\n\"\n1087\t  }\n1088\t]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"522f0308-de2f-49f8-9b4c-8bcbe609d7cd","uuid":"c0ad7abf-00ea-471b-b105-b66b8fa740ca","timestamp":"2026-05-16T12:26:09.986Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_9/projects/event-gated-adaptive-horizon/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260516T115400/median/experiment_data.json","content":"[\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Walker Walk 008d (trained dt_emb, collapse) seeds 1-2\",\n    \"description\": \"Replicate CIVO collapse with independent seeds. 009e=seed1, 009b=seed2.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"seed1_009e_eval_105K\": 131,\n      \"seed1_009e_nve_peak\": 4.12,\n      \"seed1_009e_nve_peak_step\": \"55K\",\n      \"seed1_009e_collapse\": true,\n      \"seed1_009e_collapse_onset\": \"35K\",\n      \"seed2_009b_eval_105K\": 268,\n      \"seed2_009b_nve_peak\": 2.05,\n      \"seed2_009b_nve_peak_step\": \"25K\",\n      \"seed2_009b_collapse\": false,\n      \"seed2_009b_self_correction\": true,\n      \"seed2_009b_nve_105K\": 0.38,\n      \"collapse_rate\": \"1/2 (50%)\",\n      \"nve_exceeds_1_5x_rate\": \"2/2 (100%)\",\n      \"nve_threshold_1_5x_accuracy_trained\": \"2/2\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"009e (seed 1) vs 009b (seed 2) trajectory comparison\",\n        \"rows\": [\n          {\n            \"step\": \"25K\",\n            \"s1_eval\": 167,\n            \"s1_nve\": 1.78,\n            \"s2_eval\": 141,\n            \"s2_nve\": 2.05\n          },\n          {\n            \"step\": \"35K\",\n            \"s1_eval\": 146,\n            \"s1_nve\": 1.71,\n            \"s2_eval\": 164,\n            \"s2_nve\": 1.24\n          },\n          {\n            \"step\": \"45K\",\n            \"s1_eval\": 141,\n            \"s1_nve\": 2.09,\n            \"s2_eval\": 141,\n            \"s2_nve\": 1.62\n          },\n          {\n            \"step\": \"55K\",\n            \"s1_eval\": 83,\n            \"s1_nve\": 4.12,\n            \"s2_eval\": 172,\n            \"s2_nve\": 1.41\n          },\n          {\n            \"step\": \"65K\",\n            \"s1_eval\": 250,\n            \"s1_nve\": 1.23,\n            \"s2_eval\": 209,\n            \"s2_nve\": 0.94\n          },\n          {\n            \"step\": \"85K\",\n            \"s1_eval\": 202,\n            \"s1_nve\": 1.15,\n            \"s2_eval\": 236,\n            \"s2_nve\": 0.51\n          },\n          {\n            \"step\": \"105K\",\n            \"s1_eval\": 131,\n            \"s1_nve\": 1.57,\n            \"s2_eval\": 268,\n            \"s2_nve\": 0.38\n          }\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Walker Walk CIVO collapse rate = 1/2 independent seeds. nve>1.5x threshold = 2/2 (100% sensitivity for trained dt_emb).\\nCombined with plan_012 untrained data (0/2 exceed): 4/4 perfect separation. This is the paper's strongest quantitative result for C2.\\n\\n\\nAnchored by observed registry data (009e, 009b both DONE).\\nTrained dt_emb reliably induces nve>1.5x (2/2 seeds), confirming C2 diagnostic marker.\\nCollapse is stochastic: seed 1 sustains nve>1.5x across 3+ consecutive checkpoints (feedback loop locks in); seed 2's overestimation is transient (1.62x@45K→1.41x@55K→0.94x@65K), allowing self-correction via real environment returns.\\nThe bifurcation depends on early training stochasticity — initialization and replay buffer sampling determine whether overestimation sustains long enough to overwhelm corrective gradients.\\nUnder median tier, Walker Walk is the primary environment where CIVO is well-characterized; these results set the benchmark for cross-environment comparison.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Walker Walk 008f (dt_embed_dim=0, stable) seeds 1-2\",\n    \"description\": \"Replicate causal control (no dt_embedding → no CIVO). 009f=seed1, 009c=seed2.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"seed1_009f_eval_105K\": 470,\n      \"seed1_009f_nve_peak\": 0.95,\n      \"seed1_009f_collapse\": false,\n      \"seed1_009f_trend\": \"zigzag recovery similar to 008f seed 0; value self-corrects\",\n      \"seed2_009c_eval_105K\": 148,\n      \"seed2_009c_nve_peak\": 1.26,\n      \"seed2_009c_nve_105K\": 1.14,\n      \"seed2_009c_collapse\": false,\n      \"seed2_009c_trend\": \"peak 359@75K then monotonic decline to 148@105K; no CIVO but persistent decline\",\n      \"nve_exceeds_1_5x_rate\": \"0/2 (0%)\",\n      \"nve_threshold_1_5x_accuracy_untrained\": \"2/2\",\n      \"cross_seed_consistency\": \"poor — seed 1 recovers (470@105K), seed 2 declines (148@105K)\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"008f/009c/009f NVE comparison at key checkpoints\",\n        \"rows\": [\n          {\n            \"step\": \"25K\",\n            \"s0_nve\": 1.02,\n            \"s1_nve\": 0.95,\n            \"s2_nve\": 1.26\n          },\n          {\n            \"step\": \"45K\",\n            \"s0_nve\": 0.83,\n            \"s1_nve\": 0.75,\n            \"s2_nve\": 0.72\n          },\n          {\n            \"step\": \"65K\",\n            \"s0_nve\": 0.37,\n            \"s1_nve\": 0.45,\n            \"s2_nve\": 0.53\n          },\n          {\n            \"step\": \"85K\",\n            \"s0_nve\": 0.65,\n            \"s1_nve\": 0.55,\n            \"s2_nve\": 0.6\n          },\n          {\n            \"step\": \"105K\",\n            \"s0_nve\": 0.3,\n            \"s1_nve\": 0.4,\n            \"s2_nve\": 1.14\n          }\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"All 3 seeds confirm nve < 1.5x (0/3 exceed), providing 6/6 correct classification for untrained configuration combined across plans 011+012.\\n009c's late-stage nve rise to 1.14x@105K is borderline but below threshold, consistent with dt_embed_dim=0 structurally blocking CIVO amplification.\\nThe practical unreliability (009c decline) reinforces that 008f serves as a causal control experiment, not a recommended mitigation.\\n\\n\\n009c anchored by observed registry data (DONE). 009f was killed; re-run expected to follow 008f seed 0 pattern (zigzag recovery to ~470-500).\\nWithout dt_embedding, the CIVO feedback loop is architecturally blocked: critic cannot exploit dt-conditional information because no dt signal is provided. nve stays below 1.5x for both seeds (peak 0.95 and 1.26).\\nThe cross-seed inconsistency (seed 1 recovers, seed 2 declines) reflects reward prediction degradation: dt_embed_dim=0 → reward_MAE=0.747 (75x baseline). The reward head cannot distinguish dt=1/dt=2 transitions (reward gap 1.01x vs 008d's 2.78x), creating noisy learning signal whose impact is seed-dependent.\\nC2 causal control conclusion holds: without dt_embedding, nve never exceeds 1.5x (0/2 seeds). But 008f is NOT a recommended practical mitigation due to reward quality trade-off.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Walker Walk 008a (untrained dt_emb, V-recovery) seeds 1-2\",\n    \"description\": \"Replicate value growth suppression in untrained dt_emb + subsample OFF + H=15 config.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"seed1_eval_105K\": 720,\n      \"seed1_nve_range\": [\n        0.55,\n        0.85\n      ],\n      \"seed1_nve_105K\": 0.62,\n      \"seed1_collapse\": false,\n      \"seed1_vs_baseline_105K\": \"-21%\",\n      \"seed1_zigzag_pattern\": true,\n      \"seed2_eval_105K\": 680,\n      \"seed2_nve_range\": [\n        0.6,\n        0.9\n      ],\n      \"seed2_nve_105K\": 0.7,\n      \"seed2_collapse\": false,\n      \"seed2_vs_baseline_105K\": \"-26%\",\n      \"seed2_zigzag_pattern\": true,\n      \"mean_eval_105K\": 726,\n      \"mean_vs_baseline\": \"-21%\",\n      \"nve_structural_suppression_confirmed\": true\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"008a multi-seed summary\",\n        \"rows\": [\n          {\n            \"seed\": 0,\n            \"eval_105K\": 778,\n            \"nve_105K\": 0.59,\n            \"vs_baseline\": \"-15%\",\n            \"zigzag_cycles\": 2\n          },\n          {\n            \"seed\": 1,\n            \"eval_105K\": 720,\n            \"nve_105K\": 0.62,\n            \"vs_baseline\": \"-21%\",\n            \"zigzag_cycles\": 2\n          },\n          {\n            \"seed\": 2,\n            \"eval_105K\": 680,\n            \"nve_105K\": 0.7,\n            \"vs_baseline\": \"-26%\",\n            \"zigzag_cycles\": 1.5\n          }\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Value suppression replicates robustly (3/3 seeds nve < 1.0x, 0/3 collapse). C3 strength elevated to Strong with multi-seed confirmation.\\nPerformance variance (~680-778@105K, 74-85% baseline) is within normal RL seed variability. The zigzag pattern is reproducible.\\nKey contrast with 008d (trained dt_emb): 008d 2/2 exceed nve>1.5x; 008a 0/3 exceed 1.0x. Separation is clear and robust.\\n\\n\\n008a seed 0 achieved 778@105K (85% baseline, nve=0.59x) with two V-shaped recovery cycles. Under median tier, additional seeds replicate the structural value suppression pattern but with typical RL variance.\\nThe zigzag pattern is expected because untrained dt_emb (subsample OFF → never exposed to dt>1 data) prevents CIVO feedback loop while dt=2 imagination still introduces prediction error that periodically degrades the actor, followed by self-correction from real environment data.\\nNVE remains consistently below 1.0x baseline — this is the structural signature of value growth suppression from untrained dt_emb. The suppression strengthens over time (seed 0: 0.93x@55K → 0.59x@105K) rather than converging to baseline, confirming it's a persistent architectural effect, not a transient delay.\\nSeed variance is expected: 680-720@105K range (74-79% baseline) vs seed 0's 778 (85% baseline). One seed may recover more strongly if the zigzag cycles align favorably.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Walker Walk 008e_v3 (dt=1 imagination mitigation) seeds 1-2\",\n    \"description\": \"Multi-seed validation of dt=1 imagination with trained dt_emb. Tests whether CIVO is eliminated.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"seed1_eval_105K\": 870,\n      \"seed1_nve_105K\": 0.8,\n      \"seed1_collapse\": false,\n      \"seed1_pathway\": \"healthy\",\n      \"seed2_eval_105K\": 137,\n      \"seed2_nve_105K\": 3.78,\n      \"seed2_collapse\": true,\n      \"seed2_collapse_onset\": \"95K\",\n      \"seed2_pathway\": \"Pathway_B (non-CIVO policy instability)\",\n      \"seed2_actor_entropy_at_collapse\": -3.83,\n      \"seed2_actor_entropy_pre_collapse\": -5.21,\n      \"collapse_rate\": \"1/2 (50%)\",\n      \"c6_verdict\": \"PARTIALLY FALSIFIED\",\n      \"gate_dt_1_frac\": 1.0\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Pathway A (CIVO) vs Pathway B (non-CIVO) dynamics\",\n        \"rows\": [\n          {\n            \"feature\": \"value_change\",\n            \"pathway_A_008d_s1\": \"+130% (60→138)\",\n            \"pathway_B_008e_v3_s2\": \"+13% (339→384)\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"feature\": \"eval_change\",\n            \"pathway_A_008d_s1\": \"-43% (146→83)\",\n            \"pathway_B_008e_v3_s2\": \"-81% (715→137)\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"feature\": \"nve_driver\",\n            \"pathway_A_008d_s1\": \"value numerator (explosion)\",\n            \"pathway_B_008e_v3_s2\": \"eval denominator (crash)\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"feature\": \"actor_entropy\",\n            \"pathway_A_008d_s1\": \"↓ (-3.47→-4.88)\",\n            \"pathway_B_008e_v3_s2\": \"↑ (-5.21→-3.83)\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"feature\": \"mechanism\",\n            \"pathway_A_008d_s1\": \"critic overestimate → actor exploit\",\n            \"pathway_B_008e_v3_s2\": \"eval crash → value lag → policy destabilize\"\n          }\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"C6 partially falsified: dt=1 imagination is NOT sufficient protection. Trained dt_emb creates multiple vulnerability pathways.\\nPathway B (eval-driven collapse with entropy increase) is qualitatively different from CIVO Pathway A (value-driven collapse with entropy decrease).\\nUnder median tier, Pathway B remains under-characterized — 1/2 observation rate with unclear mechanism. Not elevated to core claim; mentioned in discussion as 'evidence of deeper fragility from trained dt_embedding'.\\n\\n\\nAnchored by observed registry data (both seeds DONE).\\nSeed 1 is healthy (869.9@105K, 95% baseline, nve=0.80x). dt=1 imagination eliminates CIVO Pathway A: no dt>1 predictions in imagination → no conditioning-induced value exploitation.\\nSeed 2 COLLAPSED (715→452→137, nve=3.78x) despite gate_dt=1.0 throughout. This collapse has a DIFFERENT mechanism than classic CIVO:\\n— 008d CIVO: value explodes → eval declines → entropy decreases (actor over-commits to wrong policy)\\n— 008e_v3_s2: eval crashes FIRST → value lags → entropy INCREASES (policy destabilizes)\\nThis is Pathway B: trained dt_emb in training data (subsample ON → dt>1 transitions in replay buffer) creates vulnerability independent of imagination strategy. The dt_emb influences world model learning even when imagination uses dt=1.\\n009a baseline seed 2 is stable (618→596→836) → collapse is config-caused, not seed-fragile.\\nUnder median tier, Pathway B is present but under-characterized. 50% collapse rate matches 008d (also 50%), suggesting dt=1 imagination doesn't reduce collapse probability — it changes the pathway.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Walker Walk FROZEN dt_emb + subsample ON + dt=2 imag + H=8\",\n    \"description\": \"D041 redesign: freeze dt_embedding (requires_grad=False, random init). Tests whether dt_emb TRAINING is necessary for CIVO.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"eval_105K\": 204,\n      \"nve_105K\": 0.42,\n      \"nve_range\": [\n        0.39,\n        0.47\n      ],\n      \"collapse\": false,\n      \"collapse_mechanism\": \"gradient_starvation (not CIVO)\",\n      \"actor_loss_105K\": 0.000179,\n      \"actor_entropy_105K\": -4.855,\n      \"gate_dt_mean\": 2.0,\n      \"vs_baseline_105K\": \"-78%\",\n      \"verdict\": \"NEGATIVE — no CIVO but severe gradient starvation\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Confirms trained dt_emb is necessary for CIVO. But frozen dt_emb is not a practical mitigation due to gradient starvation.\\nCombined with 008f (absent dt_emb → reward degradation) and 008a (untrained but trainable → value suppression), the 2×2 matrix shows that ONLY training dt_emb on dt>1 data creates CIVO.\\n\\n\\nAnchored by observed registry data (exp_015b DONE).\\nFrozen dt_emb prevents CIVO (nve 0.39-0.47x, far below 1.5x) because the critic cannot learn to exploit dt-conditional information from a fixed random embedding. This confirms trained dt_emb is a NECESSARY condition for CIVO.\\nHowever, the frozen embedding introduces gradient starvation: the dt signal is random noise that the model cannot adapt to. Actor loss → 0 (actor receives near-zero gradient signal), eval volatile (80-238, no convergence), performance severely degraded (204@105K, 22% baseline).\\nThis result is architecturally expected: the frozen random embedding provides a constant noise input that the model RSSM must route around, wasting representational capacity. The dt=2 imagination still uses the frozen embedding, receiving nonsensical dt conditioning.\\nContributes to 2×2 ablation matrix: {trained: CIVO risk} × {frozen: gradient starvation} × {absent: reward degradation} × {untrained-unexposed: value suppression}. Each intervention prevents CIVO through a different mechanism with different side effects.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Walker Walk 008e_v3 additional seeds (seed 3-4)\",\n    \"description\": \"Narrow Pathway B collapse rate estimate with 2 more independent seeds.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"seed3_eval_105K\": 830,\n      \"seed3_nve_105K\": 0.72,\n      \"seed3_collapse\": false,\n      \"seed3_pathway\": \"healthy\",\n      \"seed4_eval_105K\": 195,\n      \"seed4_nve_105K\": 2.85,\n      \"seed4_collapse\": true,\n      \"seed4_collapse_onset\": \"85K\",\n      \"seed4_pathway\": \"Pathway_B (tentative)\",\n      \"seed4_actor_entropy_trend\": \"rising during collapse\",\n      \"total_collapse_rate_4_seeds\": \"2/4 (50%)\",\n      \"pathway_B_characterization\": \"under-characterized — entropy ↑ signature consistent across both collapsed seeds, but mechanism remains ambiguous\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"008e_v3 all-seed summary\",\n        \"rows\": [\n          {\n            \"seed\": 1,\n            \"eval_105K\": 870,\n            \"nve_105K\": 0.8,\n            \"collapsed\": false,\n            \"pathway\": \"healthy\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"seed\": 2,\n            \"eval_105K\": 137,\n            \"nve_105K\": 3.78,\n            \"collapsed\": true,\n            \"pathway\": \"B\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"seed\": 3,\n            \"eval_105K\": 830,\n            \"nve_105K\": 0.72,\n            \"collapsed\": false,\n            \"pathway\": \"healthy\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"seed\": 4,\n            \"eval_105K\": 195,\n            \"nve_105K\": 2.85,\n            \"collapsed\": true,\n            \"pathway\": \"B\"\n          }\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"2/4 collapse rate (50%) = same as 008d trained dt_emb + dt=2 imagination. dt=1 imagination does NOT reduce collapse probability.\\nPathway B entropy ↑ signature consistent across both collapsed seeds → pattern is reproducible but mechanism remains under-characterized.\\nPaper implication: C6 cannot be salvaged as a mitigation claim. Reposition as diagnostic finding about multiple vulnerability pathways.\\n\\n\\nUnder median tier, Pathway B is present but under-characterized. The 50% collapse rate (1/2 from seeds 1-2) is expected to hold: 1/2 additional seeds collapses.\\nSeed 3 follows the healthy trajectory (similar to seed 1): eval rises smoothly to ~830, nve stays in 0.7-0.8x range. The dt=1 imagination eliminates CIVO (Pathway A), and the stochastic Pathway B trigger doesn't fire.\\nSeed 4 collapses late (~85K onset), following the Pathway B signature: eval drops first, value is stable, entropy rises. The onset is later than seed 2 (95K) due to seed-specific training dynamics, but the qualitative pattern is the same.\\n2/4 total collapse rate (50%) is consistent but insufficient to confidently distinguish from 1/3 (33%) or 2/3 (67%) — would need 8+ seeds for tighter bounds. Under median tier, this ambiguity is accepted: Pathway B mentioned in discussion but not core claim.\\nThe under-characterization: we observe the entropy ↑ signature consistently, but cannot determine whether Pathway B is caused by (a) dt_emb distorting world model representations, (b) training data dt>1 transitions creating distributional shift, or (c) a subtler interaction. This remains an open question.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Cheetah Run baseline (3 seeds)\",\n    \"description\": \"dt=1 baseline establishing reference performance in second DMC locomotion environment.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"seed0_eval_trajectory\": {\n        \"5K\": 95,\n        \"15K\": 230,\n        \"25K\": 380,\n        \"35K\": 490,\n        \"45K\": 555,\n        \"55K\": 610,\n        \"65K\": 645,\n        \"75K\": 670,\n        \"85K\": 695,\n        \"95K\": 710,\n        \"105K\": 720\n      },\n      \"seed0_eval_peak\": 755,\n      \"seed0_eval_peak_step\": \"285K\",\n      \"seed0_eval_last10_mean\": 738,\n      \"seed1_eval_final\": 720,\n      \"seed1_eval_peak\": 733,\n      \"seed1_note\": \"anchored by running exp_010a (312K, eval 657.9, peak 732.9@265K)\",\n      \"seed2_eval_final\": 695,\n      \"seed2_eval_peak\": 725,\n      \"iqm_final\": 718,\n      \"iqm_last10\": 722,\n      \"ve_ratio_stable\": \"~65-70% from 135K+\",\n      \"ve_ratio_25K\": 0.095,\n      \"ve_ratio_35K\": 0.21,\n      \"ve_ratio_45K\": 0.305,\n      \"ve_ratio_55K\": 0.385,\n      \"ve_ratio_65K\": 0.49,\n      \"ve_ratio_75K\": 0.58,\n      \"ve_ratio_105K\": 0.65\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Cheetah Run baseline 3-seed summary\",\n        \"rows\": [\n          {\n            \"seed\": 0,\n            \"eval_peak\": 755,\n            \"eval_final_10_mean\": 738,\n            \"ve_stable\": \"67%\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"seed\": 1,\n            \"eval_peak\": 733,\n            \"eval_final_10_mean\": 720,\n            \"ve_stable\": \"65%\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"seed\": 2,\n            \"eval_peak\": 725,\n            \"eval_final_10_mean\": 695,\n            \"ve_stable\": \"66%\"\n          }\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Cheetah baseline establishes calibration for cross-environment NVE normalization. Key reference: v/e@35K~0.21, v/e@45K~0.30 — needed to compute nve for trained dt_emb experiments.\\nLower asymptote than Walker (~720 vs ~950) but comparable v/e dynamics. Cross-environment comparison is valid.\\n\\n\\nCheetah Run is a standard DMC locomotion benchmark where DreamerV3 typically converges to ~700-750 eval return. Under median tier, baseline final eval is [650, 800].\\nexp_010a (seed 1) is already running at 312K with peak 732.9@265K and current 657.9@305K. Normal fluctuation — DreamerV3 Cheetah curves show more variance post-convergence than Walker Walk. Final last-10 mean expected ~720.\\nCheetah Run has more complex dynamics than Walker Walk (more joints, higher dimensionality observation space) but lower asymptotic performance in DreamerV3. The RSSM learns Cheetah dynamics adequately but with slightly higher prediction error than Walker due to fast joint movements.\\nv/e ratio convergence is similar to Walker (~65-70%) because the actor-critic architecture handles both environments comparably. Slightly lower than Walker's 69-72% due to Cheetah's higher prediction difficulty.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Cheetah Run trained dt_emb + subsample ON (3 seeds)\",\n    \"description\": \"Core cross-environment replication of CIVO. Tests whether trained dt_emb triggers nve>1.5x in Cheetah.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"seed0_eval_trajectory\": {\n        \"5K\": 90,\n        \"15K\": 215,\n        \"25K\": 350,\n        \"35K\": 460,\n        \"45K\": 420,\n        \"55K\": 340,\n        \"65K\": 295,\n        \"75K\": 270,\n        \"85K\": 310,\n        \"95K\": 330,\n        \"105K\": 345\n      },\n      \"seed0_nve_trajectory\": {\n        \"25K\": 0.84,\n        \"35K\": 1.24,\n        \"45K\": 1.85,\n        \"55K\": 2.38,\n        \"65K\": 2.12,\n        \"75K\": 1.65,\n        \"85K\": 1.3,\n        \"95K\": 1.08,\n        \"105K\": 0.95\n      },\n      \"seed0_collapse\": true,\n      \"seed0_collapse_onset\": \"45K\",\n      \"seed0_nve_peak\": 2.38,\n      \"seed0_nve_peak_step\": \"55K\",\n      \"seed0_eval_nadir\": 270,\n      \"seed0_partial_recovery\": true,\n      \"seed0_eval_105K\": 345,\n      \"seed0_vs_baseline\": \"-52%\",\n      \"seed1_eval_trajectory\": {\n        \"25K\": 360,\n        \"35K\": 455,\n        \"45K\": 510,\n        \"55K\": 540,\n        \"65K\": 520,\n        \"75K\": 545,\n        \"85K\": 555,\n        \"95K\": 560,\n        \"105K\": 568\n      },\n      \"seed1_nve_trajectory\": {\n        \"25K\": 0.63,\n        \"35K\": 0.95,\n        \"45K\": 1.18,\n        \"55K\": 1.38,\n        \"65K\": 1.22,\n        \"75K\": 1.08,\n        \"85K\": 0.97,\n        \"95K\": 0.92,\n        \"105K\": 0.88\n      },\n      \"seed1_collapse\": false,\n      \"seed1_nve_peak\": 1.38,\n      \"seed1_nve_peak_step\": \"55K\",\n      \"seed1_eval_105K\": 568,\n      \"seed1_vs_baseline\": \"-21%\",\n      \"seed2_eval_trajectory\": {\n        \"25K\": 340,\n        \"35K\": 430,\n        \"45K\": 470,\n        \"55K\": 415,\n        \"65K\": 440,\n        \"75K\": 480,\n        \"85K\": 490,\n        \"95K\": 505,\n        \"105K\": 510\n      },\n      \"seed2_nve_trajectory\": {\n        \"25K\": 0.74,\n        \"35K\": 1.1,\n        \"45K\": 1.47,\n        \"55K\": 1.35,\n        \"65K\": 1.12,\n        \"75K\": 0.93,\n        \"85K\": 0.85,\n        \"95K\": 0.8,\n        \"105K\": 0.78\n      },\n      \"seed2_collapse\": false,\n      \"seed2_nve_peak\": 1.47,\n      \"seed2_nve_peak_step\": \"45K\",\n      \"seed2_eval_105K\": 510,\n      \"seed2_vs_baseline\": \"-29%\",\n      \"collapse_rate\": \"1/3 (33%)\",\n      \"nve_exceeds_1_5x_rate\": \"1/3 (33%)\",\n      \"mean_degradation\": \"34%\",\n      \"nve_threshold_1_5x_accuracy_trained\": \"1/3\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Cheetah Run 008d-equivalent: per-seed peak NVE and outcome\",\n        \"rows\": [\n          {\n            \"seed\": 0,\n            \"nve_peak\": 2.38,\n            \"nve_peak_step\": \"55K\",\n            \"eval_105K\": 345,\n            \"outcome\": \"collapse + partial recovery\",\n            \"nve_exceeds_1_5x\": true\n          },\n          {\n            \"seed\": 1,\n            \"nve_peak\": 1.38,\n            \"nve_peak_step\": \"55K\",\n            \"eval_105K\": 568,\n            \"outcome\": \"moderate degradation\",\n            \"nve_exceeds_1_5x\": false\n          },\n          {\n            \"seed\": 2,\n            \"nve_peak\": 1.47,\n            \"nve_peak_step\": \"45K\",\n            \"eval_105K\": 510,\n            \"outcome\": \"moderate degradation + self-correction\",\n            \"nve_exceeds_1_5x\": false\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Cross-environment CIVO comparison\",\n        \"rows\": [\n          {\n            \"env\": \"Walker Walk\",\n            \"nve_exceeds_1_5x\": \"2/2 (100%)\",\n            \"collapse_rate\": \"1/2 (50%)\",\n            \"mean_nve_peak\": 2.07\n          },\n          {\n            \"env\": \"Cheetah Run\",\n            \"nve_exceeds_1_5x\": \"1/3 (33%)\",\n            \"collapse_rate\": \"1/3 (33%)\",\n            \"mean_nve_peak\": 1.74\n          }\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"CIVO mechanism REPLICATES in Cheetah (1/3 collapse, nve-return causal pattern matches Walker) but the 1.5x diagnostic threshold loses sensitivity (1/3 vs 2/2).\\nTwo borderline seeds (1.38x, 1.47x) suggest the mechanism operates but at reduced intensity — consistent with Cheetah's smaller RSSM fidelity gap.\\nPaper framing: \\\"CIVO reliably triggers in locomotion tasks\\\" is weakened to \\\"triggers with high probability, environment-dependent severity\\\". The 1.5x threshold requires environment-specific calibration.\\nCombined accuracy at 1.5x: Walker 4/4, Cheetah 4/6 → 8/10 = 80%. Acceptable for diagnostic paper but reviewers will note non-universality.\\n\\n\\nUnder median tier, CIVO replicates in Cheetah Run but partially: nve>1.5x in 1/3 seeds (vs Walker's 2/2). Collapse rate 1/3 (vs Walker's 1/2).\\nThe weaker CIVO signal in Cheetah has a mechanistic explanation: Cheetah Run's RSSM fidelity gap at dt>1 is smaller than Walker Walk's. Cheetah dynamics (running gait) are more regular/periodic than Walker (balancing + walking), so the RSSM predicts dt=2 transitions more accurately. Smaller fidelity gap → smaller conditioning-induced value overestimation → nve peaks lower (1.38-1.47x for non-collapsing seeds, vs Walker's 1.62-2.05x).\\nSeed 0 is the exception: early stochasticity pushes nve above 1.5x threshold (peaks 2.38x@55K), triggering the CIVO feedback loop. Even this seed partially recovers (345@105K) unlike Walker 008d's terminal collapse (83@55K), because Cheetah's stronger baseline RSSM predictions provide more corrective signal.\\nSeeds 1 and 2 show borderline nve (1.38x and 1.47x) — tantalizingly close to 1.5x threshold but not exceeding it. This is the key median tier feature: the 1.5x threshold that perfectly separates Walker configurations fails to capture 2/3 Cheetah trained seeds.\\nMean degradation ~34% (within [15, 45]): collapsed seed 52%, others 21% and 29%.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Cheetah Run dt_embed_dim=0 + subsample ON (3 seeds)\",\n    \"description\": \"Causal control: replicate 008f result (no dt_embedding → no CIVO) in Cheetah.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"seed0_eval_105K\": 510,\n      \"seed0_nve_peak\": 0.78,\n      \"seed0_nve_105K\": 0.55,\n      \"seed1_eval_105K\": 480,\n      \"seed1_nve_peak\": 0.85,\n      \"seed1_nve_105K\": 0.62,\n      \"seed2_eval_105K\": 530,\n      \"seed2_nve_peak\": 0.72,\n      \"seed2_nve_105K\": 0.48,\n      \"collapse_rate\": \"0/3 (0%)\",\n      \"nve_exceeds_1_5x_rate\": \"0/3 (0%)\",\n      \"mean_eval_105K\": 507,\n      \"mean_vs_baseline\": \"-29%\",\n      \"reward_MAE_estimate\": \"~0.5-0.8 (high, similar to Walker 008f)\",\n      \"reward_gap_dt1_vs_dt2\": \"~1.02x (cannot distinguish dt=1/dt=2)\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Cross-environment dt_embed_dim=0 comparison\",\n        \"rows\": [\n          {\n            \"env\": \"Walker Walk\",\n            \"seeds\": \"3 (008f s0, 009f, 009c)\",\n            \"nve_range\": \"0.30-1.14\",\n            \"collapse\": \"0/3\",\n            \"eval_range\": \"148-503\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"env\": \"Cheetah Run\",\n            \"seeds\": 3,\n            \"nve_range\": \"0.48-0.62\",\n            \"collapse\": \"0/3\",\n            \"eval_range\": \"480-530\"\n          }\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Causal control replicates cleanly: 0/3 exceed nve 1.5x across both environments (0/6 total across Walker + Cheetah).\\nCombined with plan_021 trained result (1/3 exceed 1.5x), Cheetah causal direction confirmed: dt_embedding is necessary for CIVO, even though severity is environment-dependent.\\n\\n\\nWithout dt_embedding, the CIVO feedback loop is architecturally blocked in any environment. The critic receives no dt-conditional information → cannot exploit dt>1 prediction errors.\\nAll 3 seeds expected to stay below nve 1.5x (peak range 0.72-0.85x), replicating the Walker 008f pattern. 0/3 exceed threshold → 3/3 correctly classified.\\nPerformance degradation (~29% vs baseline) reflects the same reward prediction trade-off seen in Walker 008f: without dt_embedding, the reward head cannot distinguish dt=1/dt=2 transitions (reward gap ~1.02x), introducing noise into value learning. This is worse in Cheetah due to the more complex reward structure.\\nCross-seed consistency expected to be moderate (similar to Walker 008f, where seeds ranged from 148 to 503@105K). Cheetah seeds predicted tighter (480-530) because Cheetah baseline is more stable post-convergence.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Cheetah Run untrained dt_emb + no subsample (3 seeds)\",\n    \"description\": \"Replicate value growth suppression (C3) in Cheetah.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"seed0_eval_105K\": 575,\n      \"seed0_nve_105K\": 0.65,\n      \"seed0_vs_baseline\": \"-20%\",\n      \"seed1_eval_105K\": 535,\n      \"seed1_nve_105K\": 0.72,\n      \"seed1_vs_baseline\": \"-26%\",\n      \"seed2_eval_105K\": 550,\n      \"seed2_nve_105K\": 0.78,\n      \"seed2_vs_baseline\": \"-21%\",\n      \"mean_eval_105K\": 553,\n      \"mean_vs_baseline\": \"-23%\",\n      \"nve_range\": [\n        0.55,\n        0.85\n      ],\n      \"collapse_rate\": \"0/3 (0%)\",\n      \"zigzag_pattern\": \"present in 2/3 seeds\",\n      \"value_suppression_confirmed\": true\n    },\n    \"tables\": [],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"C3 (value growth suppression from untrained dt_emb) replicates cross-environment. Combined with Walker 008a: 0/6 seeds exceed nve 1.0x.\\nPerformance cost (~23% degradation) is moderate — better than trained dt_emb (34% degradation) and comparable to dt_embed_dim=0 (29%).\\n\\n\\nUntrained dt_emb (subsample OFF → dt_emb trained but never exposed to dt>1 data) prevents CIVO by blocking the learned dt-conditional exploitation pathway. Value growth is suppressed (nve 0.55-0.85x, consistently below 1.0x baseline).\\nPerformance reaches ~77% of Cheetah baseline (553/718), slightly below Walker's 85% (778/916). The gap reflects Cheetah's higher observation complexity: untrained dt_emb adds more noise to a system that's already harder to learn.\\nZigzag recovery pattern (2/3 seeds) replicates the Walker 008a behavior: periodic actor degradation from dt=2 imagination errors, followed by correction from real environment data.\\nUnder median tier, C3 replicates in Cheetah with moderate-strong evidence. NVE suppression is the robust cross-environment signal.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"RSSM prediction quality: dt=1 vs dt=2 transitions\",\n    \"description\": \"Extend RSSM fidelity analysis to Cheetah Run models.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"cheetah_baseline\": {\n        \"dt1_obs_mse\": 15.8,\n        \"dt2_obs_mse\": 28.5,\n        \"obs_gap\": 1.8,\n        \"dt1_reward_mae\": 0.012,\n        \"dt2_reward_mae\": 0.017,\n        \"reward_gap\": 1.42\n      },\n      \"cheetah_trained_dtEmb_collapsed_seed\": {\n        \"dt1_obs_mse\": 38.5,\n        \"dt2_obs_mse\": 48.2,\n        \"obs_gap\": 1.25,\n        \"dt1_reward_mae\": 0.065,\n        \"dt2_reward_mae\": 0.168,\n        \"reward_gap\": 2.58\n      },\n      \"cheetah_no_dtEmb\": {\n        \"dt1_obs_mse\": 9.2,\n        \"dt2_obs_mse\": 24.8,\n        \"obs_gap\": 2.7,\n        \"dt1_reward_mae\": 0.62,\n        \"dt2_reward_mae\": 0.63,\n        \"reward_gap\": 1.02\n      },\n      \"key_finding\": \"Cheetah obs_gap (1.80x) < Walker obs_gap (2.04x) → smaller RSSM fidelity gap → weaker CIVO\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Cross-environment RSSM fidelity comparison\",\n        \"rows\": [\n          {\n            \"env\": \"Walker\",\n            \"config\": \"baseline\",\n            \"obs_gap\": 2.04,\n            \"reward_gap\": 1.56\n          },\n          {\n            \"env\": \"Walker\",\n            \"config\": \"trained_dtEmb\",\n            \"obs_gap\": 1.3,\n            \"reward_gap\": 2.78\n          },\n          {\n            \"env\": \"Walker\",\n            \"config\": \"no_dtEmb\",\n            \"obs_gap\": 2.75,\n            \"reward_gap\": 1.01\n          },\n          {\n            \"env\": \"Cheetah\",\n            \"config\": \"baseline\",\n            \"obs_gap\": 1.8,\n            \"reward_gap\": 1.42\n          },\n          {\n            \"env\": \"Cheetah\",\n            \"config\": \"trained_dtEmb\",\n            \"obs_gap\": 1.25,\n            \"reward_gap\": 2.58\n          },\n          {\n            \"env\": \"Cheetah\",\n            \"config\": \"no_dtEmb\",\n            \"obs_gap\": 2.7,\n            \"reward_gap\": 1.02\n          }\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The RSSM fidelity gap is the mechanistic foundation for CIVO. Cross-environment comparison confirms: smaller gap → weaker CIVO.\\nThis provides actionable guidance: environments with more predictable dynamics are less vulnerable to CIVO, because the dt>1 prediction errors that trained dt_emb exploits are smaller.\\n\\n\\nCheetah Run's RSSM fidelity gap is predicted to be smaller than Walker Walk's (obs_gap 1.80x vs 2.04x). This is because Cheetah's running gait has more periodic/predictable dynamics than Walker's balance-and-walk task.\\nSmaller obs_gap → less dt=2 prediction error → smaller conditioning-induced value overestimation → fewer seeds crossing the nve>1.5x threshold. This mechanistically explains why CIVO partially replicates in Cheetah (1/3 vs 2/2 in Walker).\\nThe trained dt_emb model shows the same pattern as Walker: low obs_gap ratio (1.25x) but high absolute dt1 error (38.5, indicating model degradation), and high reward_gap (2.58x) indicating dt-conditional reward exploitation.\\nNo dt_emb model: reward_gap~1.02x (identical to Walker 008f's 1.01x) — architecturally, without dt_embedding, the reward head cannot distinguish dt=1/dt=2 regardless of environment.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Actor entropy analysis\",\n    \"description\": \"Systematic entropy trajectories for healthy vs collapsed configurations.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"healthy_configs_entropy_pattern\": \"gradual decrease → stable plateau around -4.5 to -5.5\",\n      \"civo_collapse_entropy_pattern\": \"decrease accelerates before collapse → continues decreasing during collapse (over-commitment)\",\n      \"pathway_b_entropy_pattern\": \"stable decrease → sudden INCREASE during collapse (policy destabilization)\",\n      \"key_separation_metric\": \"entropy change direction at collapse onset\",\n      \"civo_examples\": {\n        \"008d_s0_entropy_55K\": -4.88,\n        \"009e_s0_entropy_55K\": -4.88,\n        \"016e_entropy_95K\": -3.62\n      },\n      \"pathway_b_examples\": {\n        \"008e_v3_s2_entropy_85K\": -5.21,\n        \"008e_v3_s2_entropy_105K\": -3.83,\n        \"delta\": \"+1.38 (rising)\"\n      },\n      \"healthy_examples\": {\n        \"baseline_entropy_105K\": -5.1,\n        \"008a_entropy_105K\": -4.95\n      }\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Entropy at collapse onset: Pathway A vs Pathway B\",\n        \"rows\": [\n          {\n            \"experiment\": \"008d s0 (CIVO)\",\n            \"onset_step\": \"35K\",\n            \"entropy_before\": -3.47,\n            \"entropy_after\": -4.88,\n            \"direction\": \"↓ (over-commit)\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"experiment\": \"016e (CIVO)\",\n            \"onset_step\": \"85K\",\n            \"entropy_before\": -3.62,\n            \"entropy_after\": -3.62,\n            \"direction\": \"↓ then stable\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"experiment\": \"008e_v3_s2 (B)\",\n            \"onset_step\": \"95K\",\n            \"entropy_before\": -5.21,\n            \"entropy_after\": -3.83,\n            \"direction\": \"↑ (destabilize)\"\n          }\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Entropy analysis provides a zero-cost diagnostic that distinguishes CIVO from Pathway B collapse at collapse onset.\\nUnder median tier, the Pathway B entropy ↑ pattern is observed (2 seeds) but not sufficiently characterized to claim as a systematic finding. Include in discussion, not results.\\n\\n\\nActor entropy is a free diagnostic computed from training logs. Three distinct patterns emerge:\\n1. Healthy: entropy gradually decreases as policy sharpens, stabilizes at convergence. Baseline and untrained dt_emb configs follow this.\\n2. CIVO (Pathway A): entropy decreases faster than healthy — the over-valued critic drives the actor to over-commit to specific actions. During collapse, entropy stays low or continues decreasing (policy is confident but wrong).\\n3. Pathway B: entropy suddenly RISES during collapse — policy destabilizes, becoming more random. This is the opposite of CIVO and confirms a different mechanism.\\nThe entropy direction at collapse onset is a diagnostic separator: decreasing → CIVO, increasing → Pathway B.\\nUnder median tier, this pattern holds but Pathway B has only 2 data points (008e_v3_s2, possibly plan_016 seed 4), insufficient to fully characterize.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Value prediction distribution vs realized returns\",\n    \"description\": \"Distributional analysis of V(s) vs Monte Carlo returns.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"healthy_distribution\": \"unimodal, slight right shift (10-20% overestimation), tight spread\",\n      \"civo_distribution\": \"right-shifted, heavy right tail, mean overestimation 50-100%\",\n      \"civo_pre_collapse_distribution\": \"bimodal — cluster of correct estimates + cluster of severely overestimated states\",\n      \"untrained_distribution\": \"unimodal, slight left shift (5-15% underestimation), wider spread\",\n      \"key_finding\": \"bimodality in V(s) distribution appears 10-15K steps before collapse — potential early warning signal\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The bimodality finding (if confirmed) would strengthen the mechanistic story: CIVO doesn't uniformly inflate values — it specifically inflates dt>1 imagined states while dt=1 states remain roughly calibrated.\\nUnder median tier, this analysis likely completes for Walker but may not extend to Cheetah due to time constraints.\\n\\n\\nV(s) distribution analysis goes beyond the scalar v/e ratio to reveal HOW value estimates diverge from reality.\\nIn healthy configs, V(s) tracks Monte Carlo returns with slight overestimation (10-20%), consistent with DreamerV3's optimistic critic initialization.\\nIn CIVO configs, two phases: (1) pre-collapse: bimodal distribution emerges — states visited at dt=1 are correctly valued, states imagined at dt>1 are overestimated. The dt_embedding creates a conditional value surface where dt>1 states have systematically inflated values. (2) post-collapse: the entire distribution shifts right as the actor exploits overestimated regions, corrupting the replay buffer.\\nIn untrained dt_emb configs, V(s) is slightly underestimated (nve < 1.0x) with wider spread — the untrained embedding introduces noise that prevents precise estimation in either direction.\\nThis is a P2 experiment that would strengthen C2 but is not blocking for submission.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"dt_embedding cosine similarity analysis\",\n    \"description\": \"Track learned dt=1 vs dt=2 embedding vectors during training.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"initial_cosine_similarity\": \"random (~0.0 ± 0.3)\",\n      \"trained_dtEmb_final_similarity\": \"low (~-0.4 to 0.2)\",\n      \"trained_dtEmb_collapse_similarity\": \"divergent (< -0.3) — dt=1 and dt=2 embeddings maximally separated\",\n      \"untrained_dtEmb_similarity\": \"constant at initial value (not trained)\",\n      \"frozen_dtEmb_similarity\": \"constant at initial value (frozen)\",\n      \"key_finding\": \"cosine similarity decreasing correlates with nve increasing — as embeddings diverge, the model creates more distinct (and more exploitable) dt-conditional representations\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Embedding divergence is a consequence, not a cause, of CIVO. But it provides an intuitive visualization of what \\\"trained dt_embedding\\\" means mechanistically.\\n\\n\\ndt_embedding vectors are 8-dimensional (default dt_embed_dim=8). As training progresses, the embedding for dt=2 diverges from dt=1 because the model learns to predict different dynamics at different dt values.\\nIn collapsed configs, the embeddings diverge more (lower cosine similarity) because the CIVO feedback loop incentivizes the model to maintain strong dt-conditional differentiation. The critic learns to assign higher values to dt>1 imagined trajectories, and the dt_embedding encodes this distinction more sharply.\\nIn self-correcting seeds (009b), the embeddings partially re-converge during self-correction — as the value overestimation decreases, the gradient pressure to maintain sharp dt-distinction weakens.\\nThis is a P2 analysis that adds mechanistic depth but isn't essential for submission.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"NVE threshold sensitivity analysis\",\n    \"description\": \"Compute classification accuracy at nve thresholds 1.3x, 1.5x, 1.7x, 2.0x across all seeds and environments.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"walker_only\": {\n        \"threshold_1_3x\": {\n          \"accuracy\": \"4/4 (100%)\",\n          \"tp\": 2,\n          \"fp\": 0,\n          \"fn\": 0,\n          \"tn\": 2\n        },\n        \"threshold_1_5x\": {\n          \"accuracy\": \"4/4 (100%)\",\n          \"tp\": 2,\n          \"fp\": 0,\n          \"fn\": 0,\n          \"tn\": 2\n        },\n        \"threshold_1_7x\": {\n          \"accuracy\": \"4/4 (100%)\",\n          \"tp\": 2,\n          \"fp\": 0,\n          \"fn\": 0,\n          \"tn\": 2\n        },\n        \"threshold_2_0x\": {\n          \"accuracy\": \"4/4 (100%)\",\n          \"tp\": 2,\n          \"fp\": 0,\n          \"fn\": 0,\n          \"tn\": 2\n        },\n        \"auroc\": 1.0\n      },\n      \"walker_plus_cheetah\": {\n        \"threshold_1_3x\": {\n          \"accuracy\": \"9/10 (90%)\",\n          \"tp\": 3,\n          \"fp\": 0,\n          \"fn\": 2,\n          \"tn\": 5,\n          \"note\": \"Cheetah seeds 1&2 at 1.38x and 1.47x → classified correctly at 1.3x\"\n        },\n        \"threshold_1_5x\": {\n          \"accuracy\": \"8/10 (80%)\",\n          \"tp\": 2,\n          \"fp\": 0,\n          \"fn\": 3,\n          \"tn\": 5,\n          \"note\": \"Cheetah seeds 1&2 below 1.5x → false negatives\"\n        },\n        \"threshold_1_7x\": {\n          \"accuracy\": \"7/10 (70%)\",\n          \"tp\": 1,\n          \"fp\": 0,\n          \"fn\": 4,\n          \"tn\": 5,\n          \"note\": \"only Cheetah seed 0 at 2.38x exceeds 1.7x\"\n        },\n        \"threshold_2_0x\": {\n          \"accuracy\": \"7/10 (70%)\",\n          \"tp\": 1,\n          \"fp\": 0,\n          \"fn\": 4,\n          \"tn\": 5\n        },\n        \"auroc\": 0.93\n      },\n      \"all_environments_including_cartpole\": {\n        \"threshold_1_3x\": {\n          \"accuracy\": \"13/16 (81%)\",\n          \"note\": \"Cartpole 2/3 trained exceed 1.3x (1.35, 1.42; miss 1.22)\"\n        },\n        \"threshold_1_5x\": {\n          \"accuracy\": \"11/16 (69%)\",\n          \"note\": \"Cartpole 0/3 exceed 1.5x → all false negatives\"\n        },\n        \"threshold_1_7x\": {\n          \"accuracy\": \"10/16 (63%)\",\n          \"note\": \"only Walker 2/2 + Cheetah 1/3 exceed 1.7x\"\n        },\n        \"threshold_2_0x\": {\n          \"accuracy\": \"10/16 (63%)\",\n          \"note\": \"only Walker 2/2 + Cheetah 1/3 exceed 2.0x\"\n        },\n        \"auroc\": 0.89\n      },\n      \"optimal_threshold\": \"1.3x (cross-environment), 1.5x (locomotion-specific)\",\n      \"key_finding\": \"1.5x threshold has perfect sensitivity in Walker but environment-dependent sensitivity cross-environment. 1.3x improves cross-env accuracy from 80% to 90% with no false positives.\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Threshold sensitivity matrix\",\n        \"rows\": [\n          {\n            \"threshold\": \"1.3x\",\n            \"walker_acc\": \"100%\",\n            \"cheetah_acc\": \"100%\",\n            \"cartpole_acc\": \"83%\",\n            \"overall_acc\": \"81-90%\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"threshold\": \"1.5x\",\n            \"walker_acc\": \"100%\",\n            \"cheetah_acc\": \"67%\",\n            \"cartpole_acc\": \"50%\",\n            \"overall_acc\": \"69-80%\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"threshold\": \"1.7x\",\n            \"walker_acc\": \"100%\",\n            \"cheetah_acc\": \"67%\",\n            \"cartpole_acc\": \"50%\",\n            \"overall_acc\": \"63-70%\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"threshold\": \"2.0x\",\n            \"walker_acc\": \"100%\",\n            \"cheetah_acc\": \"67%\",\n            \"cartpole_acc\": \"50%\",\n            \"overall_acc\": \"63-70%\"\n          }\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Paper should present NVE as a robust diagnostic METRIC (high AUROC) rather than a fixed threshold. The 1.5x value is appropriate for locomotion tasks but environment-specific calibration is needed.\\nUnder median tier, recommend presenting AUROC + environment-specific threshold recommendation, with explicit caveat that 1.5x is validated only in Walker Walk.\\n\\n\\n0 GPU cost — computed from existing + predicted metrics.\\nWalker Walk: large nve separation (trained peaks 2.05-4.12x vs untrained peaks 0.95-1.26x) gives perfect accuracy at all thresholds. AUROC=1.0 because all trained nve peaks > all untrained nve peaks.\\nCheetah Run (median tier): narrower separation (trained peaks 1.38-2.38x vs untrained peaks 0.72-0.85x). At 1.5x, 2/3 trained seeds are borderline (1.38x, 1.47x) → false negatives. At 1.3x, these seeds are correctly classified because they exceed 1.3x. AUROC remains high (0.93) because rank ordering is preserved (all trained > all untrained).\\nCartpole (median tier): narrow separation (trained peaks 1.22-1.42x vs untrained peaks 0.72-0.85x). At 1.5x, 0/3 trained seeds exceed → all false negatives. At 1.3x, 2/3 exceed.\\nKey insight: the nve metric has HIGH discriminative power (AUROC 0.89-0.93 cross-environment) but the specific 1.5x threshold requires environment-dependent calibration. For a universal diagnostic, 1.3x is more appropriate, or use AUROC directly.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Cartpole Swingup baseline (3 seeds)\",\n    \"description\": \"dt=1 baseline in non-locomotion DMC environment.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"seed0_eval_trajectory\": {\n        \"5K\": 45,\n        \"15K\": 220,\n        \"25K\": 420,\n        \"35K\": 580,\n        \"45K\": 670,\n        \"55K\": 730,\n        \"65K\": 770,\n        \"75K\": 800,\n        \"85K\": 820,\n        \"95K\": 830,\n        \"105K\": 835\n      },\n      \"seed0_eval_peak\": 850,\n      \"seed0_eval_final_10_mean\": 838,\n      \"seed1_eval_final_10_mean\": 852,\n      \"seed2_eval_final_10_mean\": 827,\n      \"iqm_final\": 839,\n      \"ve_ratio_stable\": \"~67-72%\",\n      \"ve_ratio_35K\": 0.225,\n      \"ve_ratio_45K\": 0.32,\n      \"ve_ratio_55K\": 0.4,\n      \"ve_ratio_105K\": 0.67\n    },\n    \"tables\": [],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Establishes reference for Cartpole NVE normalization. Key difference from locomotion: faster convergence → v/e calibration anchors stabilize earlier.\\n\\n\\nCartpole Swingup is a standard DMC benchmark where DreamerV3 achieves ~820-860 asymptotic return. Simpler dynamics than locomotion tasks (4-DOF pendulum + cart vs multi-joint walker/cheetah).\\nFaster convergence than Walker/Cheetah because: (a) simpler observation space (5D vs 24D for Walker), (b) more regular dynamics (pendulum physics are smooth), (c) reward is dense (based on pole angle).\\nv/e ratio convergence is comparable to locomotion tasks (~67-72%) because the actor-critic architecture is environment-agnostic.\\nUnder median tier, these baseline results are expected to be clean and uncontroversial. The interesting question is whether trained dt_emb induces CIVO in this simpler environment.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Cartpole Swingup trained dt_emb + subsample ON (3 seeds)\",\n    \"description\": \"Test CIVO in non-locomotion environment. Key question: does simpler dynamics reduce CIVO?\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"seed0_eval_trajectory\": {\n        \"25K\": 390,\n        \"35K\": 530,\n        \"45K\": 610,\n        \"55K\": 660,\n        \"65K\": 690,\n        \"75K\": 700,\n        \"85K\": 710,\n        \"95K\": 710,\n        \"105K\": 712\n      },\n      \"seed0_nve_trajectory\": {\n        \"25K\": 0.67,\n        \"35K\": 1.0,\n        \"45K\": 1.35,\n        \"55K\": 1.22,\n        \"65K\": 1.05,\n        \"75K\": 0.93,\n        \"85K\": 0.88,\n        \"95K\": 0.85,\n        \"105K\": 0.82\n      },\n      \"seed0_nve_peak\": 1.35,\n      \"seed0_collapse\": false,\n      \"seed0_eval_105K\": 712,\n      \"seed0_vs_baseline\": \"-15%\",\n      \"seed1_nve_peak\": 1.42,\n      \"seed1_nve_peak_step\": \"45K\",\n      \"seed1_eval_105K\": 685,\n      \"seed1_vs_baseline\": \"-18%\",\n      \"seed1_collapse\": false,\n      \"seed2_nve_peak\": 1.22,\n      \"seed2_nve_peak_step\": \"55K\",\n      \"seed2_eval_105K\": 740,\n      \"seed2_vs_baseline\": \"-11%\",\n      \"seed2_collapse\": false,\n      \"collapse_rate\": \"0/3 (0%)\",\n      \"nve_exceeds_1_5x\": \"0/3 (0%)\",\n      \"mean_nve_peak\": 1.33,\n      \"mean_degradation\": \"-15%\",\n      \"key_finding\": \"CIVO mechanism present (nve>1.0x, degradation) but below 1.5x threshold in all seeds\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Cartpole trained dt_emb: per-seed NVE peak and outcome\",\n        \"rows\": [\n          {\n            \"seed\": 0,\n            \"nve_peak\": 1.35,\n            \"nve_peak_step\": \"45K\",\n            \"eval_105K\": 712,\n            \"vs_baseline\": \"-15%\",\n            \"exceeds_1_5x\": false\n          },\n          {\n            \"seed\": 1,\n            \"nve_peak\": 1.42,\n            \"nve_peak_step\": \"45K\",\n            \"eval_105K\": 685,\n            \"vs_baseline\": \"-18%\",\n            \"exceeds_1_5x\": false\n          },\n          {\n            \"seed\": 2,\n            \"nve_peak\": 1.22,\n            \"nve_peak_step\": \"55K\",\n            \"eval_105K\": 740,\n            \"vs_baseline\": \"-11%\",\n            \"exceeds_1_5x\": false\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Cross-environment NVE peak comparison (trained dt_emb)\",\n        \"rows\": [\n          {\n            \"env\": \"Walker Walk\",\n            \"mean_nve_peak\": 2.07,\n            \"nve_range\": \"1.62-4.12\",\n            \"exceeds_1_5x\": \"2/2 (100%)\",\n            \"collapse_rate\": \"1/2\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"env\": \"Cheetah Run\",\n            \"mean_nve_peak\": 1.74,\n            \"nve_range\": \"1.38-2.38\",\n            \"exceeds_1_5x\": \"1/3 (33%)\",\n            \"collapse_rate\": \"1/3\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"env\": \"Cartpole Swingup\",\n            \"mean_nve_peak\": 1.33,\n            \"nve_range\": \"1.22-1.42\",\n            \"exceeds_1_5x\": \"0/3 (0%)\",\n            \"collapse_rate\": \"0/3\"\n          }\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Cartpole confirms that CIVO severity scales with RSSM fidelity gap, which correlates with environment dynamics complexity.\\nFor the paper: either (a) drop Cartpole, presenting results only for locomotion tasks where CIVO is clear, or (b) include Cartpole as evidence that the mechanism has environment-dependent severity, with the 1.5x threshold requiring calibration.\\nUnder median tier, recommend option (b) with Cartpole as preliminary/supplementary evidence, noting that a lower threshold (~1.3x) would capture the Cartpole signal.\\n\\n\\nUnder median tier, Cartpole shows nve 1.2-1.5x (borderline). The key mechanism is:\\nCartpole's RSSM fidelity gap at dt>1 is SMALLER than locomotion tasks because:\\n(a) Simpler dynamics (4-DOF pendulum) — RSSM predicts dt=2 transitions more accurately\\n(b) Smooth observation space — less aliasing at coarser temporal resolution\\n(c) Dense reward — reward prediction at dt=2 is less distorted\\nSmaller fidelity gap → smaller conditioning-induced value overestimation → nve peaks in 1.22-1.42x range (below 1.5x diagnostic threshold). The trained dt_emb still creates SOME overestimation (all seeds > 1.0x), but insufficient to trigger the CIVO feedback loop.\\nNo collapse (0/3) because nve never sustains above the critical threshold where the positive feedback loop locks in. Performance is degraded (~15%) due to the dt=2 transitions in training data adding noise, but the degradation is mild and non-catastrophic.\\nThis is the core median tier finding for Cartpole: CIVO mechanism is PRESENT (nve > 1.0x, degradation beyond normal) but NOT DIAGNOSTIC (doesn't exceed 1.5x). The 1.5x threshold is environment-specific, not universal.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Cartpole Swingup dt_embed_dim=0 + subsample ON (3 seeds)\",\n    \"description\": \"Causal control for Cartpole.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"seed0_eval_105K\": 690,\n      \"seed0_nve_peak\": 0.82,\n      \"seed1_eval_105K\": 670,\n      \"seed1_nve_peak\": 0.75,\n      \"seed2_eval_105K\": 710,\n      \"seed2_nve_peak\": 0.8,\n      \"collapse_rate\": \"0/3 (0%)\",\n      \"nve_exceeds_1_5x\": \"0/3 (0%)\",\n      \"mean_eval_105K\": 690,\n      \"mean_vs_baseline\": \"-18%\",\n      \"reward_degradation\": \"present but milder than Walker 008f due to simpler reward structure\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Combined untrained/absent dt_emb across all environments: 0/11 seeds exceed nve 1.5x (Walker 0/5, Cheetah 0/3, Cartpole 0/3).\\nThis is the strongest cross-environment result: CIVO requires trained dt_embedding in every environment tested.\\n\\n\\nWithout dt_embedding, CIVO is blocked. All seeds stay below nve 1.5x (peak 0.75-0.82x).\\nPerformance degradation (~18%) is from reward prediction noise (no dt conditioning → reward head can't distinguish dt=1/dt=2), same mechanism as Walker 008f.\\nThe degradation is comparable to the trained dt_emb condition (~15%), unlike Walker where dt_embed_dim=0 degradation (45%) is much worse than trained dt_emb degradation. In Cartpole, both conditions degrade similarly because the CIVO feedback loop doesn't strongly differentiate them.\\nUnder median tier, this confirms the causal control in a third environment: 0/3 seeds exceed nve 1.5x.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Uncertainty-gated imagination truncation pilot (Walker Walk, 1 seed)\",\n    \"description\": \"Truncate imagination when RSSM prediction uncertainty exceeds threshold.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"eval_105K\": 580,\n      \"nve_105K\": 0.95,\n      \"collapse\": false,\n      \"vs_baseline\": \"-37%\",\n      \"truncation_frequency\": \"~30-40% of imagination steps truncated at dt=2\",\n      \"effective_horizon\": \"~10.5 (vs H=15 nominal)\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"truncation prevents worst-case RSSM predictions from corrupting value estimates\",\n      \"ambiguity\": \"delays CIVO but unclear if prevents — truncation threshold sensitive\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Ambiguous result under median tier. The truncation prevents collapse but degrades performance more than dt=1 imagination (which at least has 50% chance of no-collapse with near-baseline performance).\\nNot included as a recommended mitigation. Mentioned as future work direction.\\n\\n\\nUnder median tier, uncertainty-gated truncation is ambiguous: it delays but doesn't clearly prevent collapse, similar to 016e (SGS).\\nThe mechanism is sound in principle: truncate imagination when RSSM prediction becomes unreliable, preventing corrupted predictions from influencing critic updates. However:\\n(a) Threshold calibration is environment-specific — too aggressive truncation reduces effective horizon (hurts performance), too lenient doesn't prevent CIVO\\n(b) The RSSM's own uncertainty estimates may be miscalibrated, especially during the training period when CIVO develops\\n(c) The truncation addresses the symptom (bad predictions in imagination) but not the root cause (trained dt_emb creating exploitable conditioning in the critic)\\nExpected outcome: moderate performance (580@105K, 63% baseline), no collapse, but nve hovers near 1.0x — the truncation prevents pathological overestimation but at the cost of learning efficiency.\\nUnder median tier, this is reported as \\\"promising but inconclusive pilot\\\" in the discussion section. Not elevated to a core result.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Hopper Hop validation (3 seeds × 3 configs)\",\n    \"description\": \"Third DMC environment for generalization.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"status_by_deadline\": \"incomplete — only baseline (3 seeds) + trained dt_emb (1-2 seeds) completed\",\n      \"baseline_eval_final\": \"~250 (Hopper Hop is a difficult DMC task)\",\n      \"baseline_ve_stable\": \"~55-65%\",\n      \"trained_dtEmb_partial\": {\n        \"seeds_completed\": 1,\n        \"nve_peak\": 1.45,\n        \"collapse\": false,\n        \"eval_degradation\": \"-25%\"\n      },\n      \"verdict\": \"insufficient data to include in paper\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Insufficient data under median tier timeline. If included, would weaken rather than strengthen claims due to noisy/inconclusive results.\\nRecommend dropping from paper scope entirely. Focus on Walker + Cheetah (+ Cartpole as supplementary).\\n\\nP2 priority with 45 GPU-hour budget. Under median tier with 13 days to deadline and GPU contention from P0 experiments, only partial results available.\\nHopper Hop is a difficult DMC task (baseline ~250, significantly lower than Walker/Cheetah). DreamerV3 struggles more with hopping dynamics. CIVO behavior is unpredictable: the low baseline performance means v/e ratios are noisier and less reliable for NVE normalization.\\nThe 1-2 seeds of trained dt_emb that complete would show borderline results (nve ~1.45x, similar to Cartpole) but with insufficient statistical power to draw conclusions.\\nUnder median tier, Hopper Hop is dropped from the paper or relegated to a one-line mention in the limitations section.\\n\"\n  }\n]","numLines":1088,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1088}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017PnEF3b6Vz84iYPkLH66Hq","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Paper Framing: Diagnostic Analysis of Temporal Abstraction in World-Model RL\n2\t\n3\t## Title Candidates\n4\t\n5\t1. **\"Why Temporal Abstraction Fails in DreamerV3: A Diagnostic Analysis of Variable-Rate Imagination\"**\n6\t2. ~~**\"The Delusional Critic Problem in DreamerV3's Variable-Rate Imagination\"**~~ (deprecated — \"delusional critic\" conflicts with Lu et al. 2018)\n7\t3. **\"Diagnosing Value Overestimation from Temporal Abstraction in DreamerV3\"** ← preferred after D037 terminology decision\n8\t4. **\"Conditioning-Induced Value Overestimation in Variable-Rate World-Model Imagination\"**\n9\t\n10\t**⚠️ D037 术语决定**: \"delusional critic\" → **\"conditioning-induced value overestimation\"** (简称 CIVO)。Lu et al. 2018 的 delusional bias 指 policy-class constraints 导致不可实现的 Q 值，本文机制是 model imprecision + learned conditioning amplification，概念不同。Dream 模拟三档审稿一致指出术语误导。全文替换：\"delusional critic\" → \"conditioning-induced value overestimation\"，可 cite Lu et al. 2018 as analogy but must explicitly differentiate。\n11\t\n12\t**⚠️ D037 TAWM 对比（关键 novelty 升级）**: TAWM (Nhu et al., ICML 2025) 在 TD-MPC2 (MPC setting) 中成功使用 dt-conditioning。本文发现 dt-conditioning 在 imagination-based actor-critic 中失败 → **novelty 从 \"known failure class 的 specific instance\" 升级为 \"dt-conditioning succeeds in MPC but fails in imagination — here's the architecture-specific vulnerability\"**。TAWM 是强化 novelty 的最关键参考文献。\n13\t\n14\t## Venue\n15\t\n16\t**CoRL 2026** — Deadline: 2026-05-29\n17\t\n18\t## Narrative Positioning\n19\t\n20\tThis is a **diagnostic analysis paper**, not a method paper. We do not propose a new algorithm. Instead, we provide a systematic, controlled investigation answering: *Why does temporal abstraction fail in world-model RL?*\n21\t\n22\tThe contribution is a **strongly supported causal chain** from mechanism to failure mode, backed by a factorial experiment design that isolates each factor, plus a **practical mitigation guideline** (restrict imagination to dt=1 during multi-dt training → 95% baseline recovery). Diagnostic papers are valued when they (a) address a widely-attempted but poorly-understood failure, (b) provide actionable insight for future method design, and (c) are methodologically rigorous.\n23\t\n24\tWe scope all claims to **DreamerV3 and its RSSM world model**. Generalizability to other latent-dynamics architectures is discussed but not claimed.\n25\t\n26\t---\n27\t\n28\t## Core Claims\n29\t\n30\t### C1: Variable-rate imagination in DreamerV3 consistently degrades performance\n31\t\n32\tTemporal abstraction via variable dt—whether learned or fixed—degrades asymptotic performance relative to the dt=1 baseline across all tested configurations.\n33\t\n34\t| Evidence | Experiment | Key Metric |\n35\t|----------|-----------|------------|\n36\t| Baseline stability | exp_001_baseline (dt=1) | Stable convergence ~950 eval return |\n37\t| Fixed dt=2 collapse | exp_006_fixed_dt2 (dt=2, subsample ON, H=15) | Peak 485@35K → collapse to ~135@65K |\n38\t| Fixed dt=4 severe degradation | exp_007_fixed_dt4 (dt=4) | Peak 176.9@25K, 144.4@45K (33% of baseline@45K=443) |\n39\t| Learned gate collapse | exp_005 (learned gate) | Peak 541@55K → 99@145K |\n40\t\n41\t**Strength**: Strong. Three independent dt>1 configurations all show degradation. No positive result exists for variable-rate imagination in our setup.\n42\t\n43\t### C2: Trained dt_embedding reliably triggers pathological value overestimation (>1.5x), creating high collapse risk\n44\t\n45\t**D032 update: nve>1.5x threshold has perfect separating power (4/4); collapse itself is stochastic.**\n46\t\n47\tThe trained dt_embedding reliably induces pathological value overestimation (normalized v/e >1.5x baseline) across all tested seeds, but whether this overestimation progresses to catastrophic performance collapse is stochastic:\n48\t\n49\t- **WITH trained dt_emb**: 2/2 seeds exceed nve>1.5x at 35K+ (008d seed 1: sustained 1.71x@35K→2.09x@45K→4.12x@55K → collapse; 009b seed 2: 1.62x@45K → self-correction to 0.38x@105K)\n50\t- **WITHOUT dt_emb (008f)**: 0/2 seeds exceed nve>1.5x at 35K+ (seed 1 peak 1.02x@25K; seed 2 peak 1.26x@25K)\n51\t- **1.5x threshold (35K+)**: 4/4 perfect classification — reliable diagnostic marker separating trained vs untrained dt_emb\n52\t\n53\t**⚠️ 时间限定**: nve>1.5x 诊断适用于 **35K+ steps**（此时 baseline v/e 已充分收敛至 >0.24，比率稳定可靠）。25K 处 baseline v/e 仅 0.087，微小绝对差异可被放大为大比率（如 009b@25K nve=2.05x 和 008e_v3@25K nve=1.93x 均为 false positive）。论文中报告 threshold 时必须标注此限定。\n54\t\n55\t**Sustained vs transient overestimation**: The key difference between collapse (008d seed 1) and self-correction (009b seed 2) is the **temporal persistence** of nve>1.5x. Seed 1 maintained nve>1.5x across 3 consecutive checkpoints at 35K+ (1.71x@35K→2.09x@45K→4.12x@55K, accelerating), locking the positive feedback loop; seed 2 exceeded 1.5x at 45K (1.62x) but dropped to 1.41x@55K and continued declining, allowing self-correction. This suggests a \"point of no return\" where sustained overestimation becomes irreversible, with early training stochasticity (initialization, data sampling order) determining which path is taken (cf. Henderson et al. 2018, Bjorck et al. 2022, Qiao et al. 2023).\n56\t\n57\tThe trained dt_embedding enables precise dt prediction in imagination, but RSSM state prediction quality at dt>1 is insufficient to support this precision. The result is conditioning-induced value overestimation (CIVO) that drives actor learning toward incorrect policies. Without dt_embedding conditioning, the overestimation feedback loop is blocked entirely (nve never exceeds 1.5x).\n58\t\n59\t| Evidence | Experiment | Key Metric |\n60\t|----------|-----------|------------|\n61\t| Untrained dt_emb, no collapse | exp_008c (dt=2, subsample OFF, untrained dt_emb, H=8) | eval plateau ~242@55K; v/e: @35K=21.8% (0.90x) → @55K=29.1% (0.72x) → @65K=32.8% (0.58x) (declining normalized v/e, ~7x slower than trained) |\n62\t| Trained dt_emb, collapse | exp_008d (dt=2, subsample ON, trained dt_emb, H=8) | eval declining 167→146→141 @25K→35K→45K; v/e: @25K=15.5% (1.78x) → @35K=41.2% (1.71x) → @45K=69.3% (2.09x) (accelerating overestimation) |\n63\t| Both trained dt_emb → accelerating v/e | exp_006 (H=15) vs exp_008d (H=8) | 006: v/e @35K=31.1% (1.29x)→@45K=53.3% (1.61x)→@55K=93.3% (2.31x); 008d: v/e @35K=41.2% (1.71x)→@45K=69.3% (2.09x) — both show accelerating normalized v/e leading to collapse |\n64\t| Baseline v/e calibration (overshoot reference) | exp_001_baseline | v/e@35K=24.1%, @45K=33.1%, @55K=40.4%. Overshoot ratio: 008d@35K=1.7x, @45K=2.1x; 006@45K=1.6x, @55K=2.3x (all >1.5x = pathological) |\n65\t| **D032: Trained dt_emb seed 2 — pathological but self-correcting** | exp_009b (008d config, seed 2) | eval 39→141→164→141→172→209→197→236→279→268@105K (**no collapse**, rising). nve: **2.05x@25K**→1.24x→**1.62x@45K**→1.41x→0.94x→0.66x→0.51x→0.43x→**0.38x@105K**. Transient nve>1.5x with self-correction. Value peaked 110.9@65K → self-corrected to 67.3@105K while eval improved. |\n66\t| **1.5x threshold separating power** | 008d+009b vs 008f+009c | WITH trained dt_emb: 2/2 seeds exceed nve>1.5x (008d peak 2.09x sustained, 009b peak 2.05x transient). WITHOUT dt_emb: 0/2 seeds exceed nve>1.5x (008f peak 1.02x, 009c peak 1.26x). **4/4 perfect classification.** |\n67\t| **Causal control: dt_embed_dim=0 (direction confirmed, seed-dependent)** | exp_008f seed 1 + exp_009c seed 2 | **Seed 1**: eval 197→207→194→173→503@105K (zigzag recovery); nve 1.02x→0.73x→0.83x→0.97x→0.30x. **Seed 2**: eval 169→211→239→359→227→191→148@105K (decline after 75K); nve 1.26x→0.79x→0.72x→0.33x→0.60x→0.90x→1.14x. Both vs 008d@45K nve=2.09x: onset delayed 60K+ steps, severity reduced ~2x. **009c extending to 200K to observe if nve crosses 1.5x.** |\n68\t| **Reward SGS = no-op (reward gradient = 0 by architecture)** | exp_016e (reward_dt_emb_detach=True, subsample ON, H=8, seed 1) | Peak eval 326.7@75K → collapse 31.7@95K, NVE=10.42x. 梯度验证揭示 reward loss 对 dt_emb.weight 贡献为 0——reward_dt_emb_detach 是 no-op。016e 与 008d 的差异（onset timing, peak height）归因于随机变异。**dt_emb 的 93% 梯度来自 observation decoders（orientations/velocity/height），7% 来自 KL dynamics，reward=0。** |\n69\t| RSSM fidelity analysis | baseline / 008d / 008f | See RSSM Fidelity Table below |\n70\t\n71\t**⚠️ 008f 定位：因果控制实验，非推荐方案。** 008f 去掉 dt_embedding 后 reward_MAE=0.747（baseline 的 75x），reward head 无法区分 dt=1/dt=2（reward gap 仅 1.01x vs 008d 的 2.78x）。**D030 更新**：008f seed 2 (009c) 在 105K 达到 nve=1.14x（borderline），seed 1 为 0.30x——protection 不 robust。但 008f 两个 seed 的 v/e 增长速度均显著慢于 008d（008d@45K=2.09x vs 008f seed 2@105K=1.14x），确认 dt_embedding 是 primary amplifier。**C2 因果方向成立，但 \"eliminates\" 改为 \"significantly delays and reduces\"。推荐方案仍是 008e_v3（C6）**。\n72\t\n73\t**RSSM Fidelity Table（论文中必须同时报告绝对误差和比率）：**\n74\t\n75\t| Model | dt1 obs_MSE | dt2 obs_MSE | obs gap | dt1 reward_MAE | dt2 reward_MAE | reward gap |\n76\t|-------|------------|------------|---------|---------------|---------------|------------|\n77\t| baseline (exp_001) | 13.49 | 27.50 | **2.04x** | 0.0102 | 0.0159 | 1.56x |\n78\t| 008d (trained dt_emb, collapsed) | 41.31 | 53.67 | **1.30x** | 0.0568 | 0.1579 | **2.78x** |\n79\t| 008f (no dt_emb, stable) | 8.01 | 22.04 | **2.75x** | 0.7469 | 0.7526 | 1.01x |\n80\t\n81\t**解读要点**：008d 的低 obs gap ratio (1.30x) 具有误导性——其 dt=1 绝对误差本身已是 baseline 的 3x（模型已崩塌）。论文中必须并排呈现绝对误差和比率，避免审稿人被比率误导。\n82\t\n83\t**dt_emb Gradient Decomposition (D061)**\n84\t\n85\t梯度验证（torch.autograd.grad analysis, commit 5fc8e49）揭示 dt_emb.weight 的梯度来源分布：\n86\t\n87\t| Source | Grad Norm | Fraction |\n88\t|--------|-----------|----------|\n89\t| Observation decoders (orientations, velocity, height) | 1.505 | 93% |\n90\t| KL dynamics | 0.119 | 7% |\n91\t| Cont head | ~0 (included in decoder total) | <1% |\n92\t| **Reward head** | **0.000** | **0%** |\n93\t| **Total** | **1.624** | **100%** |\n94\t\n95\t这意味着：(1) 016e 的 reward_dt_emb_detach 是 no-op——detach 了一个不存在的梯度通路；(2) dt_emb 的训练几乎完全由 observation reconstruction loss 驱动；(3) Full-SGS（exp_018）移除 observation decoder 梯度后仅保留 7% KL 梯度，是真正的 gradient-level intervention。\n96\t\n97\t**Strength**: Moderate-strong (D032 update: causal direction + diagnostic marker confirmed; collapse stochastic). The **nve>1.5x threshold** achieves 4/4 perfect separation: all trained dt_emb seeds (008d, 009b) exceed 1.5x; no untrained seeds (008f, 009c) exceed 1.5x. This is the paper's strongest quantitative result for C2. **Collapse** is stochastic (1/2 trained seeds): 008d seed 1 shows sustained nve>1.5x → collapse; 009b seed 2 shows transient nve>1.5x → self-correction. The **causal direction** is unambiguous across all 4 data points. Pending: 009e (008d seed 1) to determine collapse probability (1/3 vs 2/3); 009f (008f seed 1) for third untrained seed; 009c extension to 200K.\n98\t\n99\t### C3: Untrained dt_embedding prevents optimistic drift, substantively protecting performance\n100\t\n101\tWhen dt_embedding is untrained (random fixed), the value function cannot exploit precise dt information, preventing the optimistic value drift seen in trained variants. This value growth suppression substantively protects performance: exp_008a demonstrates two complete zigzag recovery cycles over 105K steps (peak @105K=778, 85% of baseline 916), with normalized v/e consistently suppressed at 0.59-0.93x baseline. The trained dt_embedding variant (exp_006) collapses catastrophically as its v/e ratio accelerates far past baseline levels (2.3x baseline@55K).\n102\t\n103\t| Evidence | Experiment | Key Metric |\n104\t|----------|-----------|------------|\n105\t| Two V-shaped recoveries (105K) | exp_008a (dt=2, subsample OFF, untrained dt_emb, H=15) | 501→563→488→608→470→584→572→778; two complete recovery cycles over 105K steps. @65K=608 (-1.3% baseline), @105K=778 (-15.1% baseline, 85% of 916). No collapse. |\n106\t| Sustained v/e suppression | exp_008a | v/e: 37.6%@55K → 37.2%@65K → 53.6%@75K → 47.5%@85K → 49.7%@95K → 38.6%@105K. Normalized v/e: 0.93x→0.66x→0.75x→0.68x→0.79x→0.59x baseline. Consistently below 1.0x, no acceleration. |\n107\t| Trained dt_emb: accelerating collapse | exp_006 (dt=2, subsample ON, trained dt_emb, H=15) | 485→477→360→collapse; v/e: 53.3%@45K (1.61x)→93.3%@55K (2.31x) → immediate collapse |\n108\t| v/e rate comparison (H=8 controlled) | exp_008c vs exp_008d | 008c (untrained): @35K=21.8% (0.90x)→@65K=32.8% (0.58x) (normalized declining); 008d (trained): @25K=15.5% (1.78x)→@35K=41.2% (1.71x) (normalized >1.5x = pathological). ~7x slower overestimation rate |\n109\t| Baseline v/e calibration | exp_001_baseline | v/e: 8.7%@25K → 24.1%@35K → 40.4%@55K → 56.3%@65K → 65.6%@105K (stable 69-72% from 135K+). 008a normalized v/e: 0.93x@55K → 0.66x@65K → 0.59x@105K (suppressed, diverging further below baseline); 008d: 1.7x@35K → 2.1x@45K (optimistic drift) |\n110\t\n111\t**Strength**: Strong (B+ confirmed with 105K long-run data). Two complete zigzag recovery cycles (501→608→470→778) with normalized v/e consistently 0.59-0.93x baseline across 105K steps. This is structural value growth suppression, not a transient delay. Contrasts sharply with exp_006's accelerating v/e (1.6x→2.3x baseline → collapse). The mechanism is clear: untrained dt_embedding prevents the value function from exploiting precise (but wrong) dt>1 predictions, suppressing the optimistic value drift that drives the CIVO feedback loop in trained variants.\n112\t\n113\t**Diagnostic metric: v/e overshoot ratio** = experiment v/e / baseline v/e at same training step. >1.5x = pathological warning. Trained dt_emb variants: 008d@35K=1.7x, @45K=2.1x; 006@45K=1.6x, @55K=2.3x. Untrained: 008a normalized v/e range 0.59-0.93x over 55K-105K (consistently below baseline = healthy, diverging further from baseline over time).\n114\t\n115\t**105K long-run confirmed**: exp_008a ran to 112K steps. Normalized v/e at 105K=0.59x baseline (lowest point), confirming value growth suppression is structural, not a transient delay. The suppression strengthens over time rather than converging to baseline levels.\n116\t\n117\t### C4: Longer dt amplifies the problem\n118\t\n119\tIncreasing dt from 2 to 4 worsens degradation, consistent with RSSM prediction error compounding over longer temporal gaps.\n120\t\n121\t| Evidence | Experiment | Key Metric |\n122\t|----------|-----------|------------|\n123\t| dt=2 degradation | exp_006_fixed_dt2 | Peak 485, collapse to ~135 |\n124\t| dt=4 severe degradation | exp_007_fixed_dt4 | Peak 176.9@25K, 144.4@45K (33% of baseline@45K=443) |\n125\t| Monotonic relationship | dt=1 (~950) > dt=2 (peak 485@35K) > dt=4 (peak 177@25K) | Clear monotonic decline |\n126\t\n127\t**Strength**: Moderate. Only two dt values tested (2 and 4). The monotonic trend is clear but a finer grid (dt=3, dt=6) would strengthen this claim. Sufficient for a supporting claim but not a standalone finding.\n128\t\n129\t### C5: Learning signal reduction from shorter effective horizons compounds degradation but is not the root cause\n130\t\n131\tSubsampling with dt>1 reduces the effective number of learning transitions per episode. This contributes to degradation but is neither necessary nor sufficient for collapse.\n132\t\n133\t| Evidence | Experiment | Key Metric |\n134\t|----------|-----------|------------|\n135\t| Subsample OFF, still degraded | exp_008c (subsample OFF, untrained dt_emb, H=8) | eval ~242 vs baseline ~950 |\n136\t| Subsample OFF, no collapse | exp_008a (subsample OFF, untrained dt_emb, H=15) | eval 778@105K (85% baseline), no collapse through 112K steps |\n137\t| Subsample ON, collapse | exp_008d (subsample ON, trained dt_emb, H=8) | eval 146, value overestimation |\n138\t| Horizon effect | exp_008c (H=8) vs exp_008a (H=15) | H=15 significantly outperforms H=8, both subsample OFF |\n139\t| Subsample ON, trained dt_emb, dt=1 imag, no collapse | exp_008e_v3 (subsample ON, trained dt_emb, dt=1 imag, H=15) | eval 382@45K (86% baseline); normalized v/e 0.88x@45K (normal). Even with trained dt_emb and subsample ON, restricting imagination to dt=1 prevents collapse |\n140\t\n141\t**Strength**: Moderate. The 008c vs 008a comparison shows horizon length matters independently. exp_008e_v3 confirms subsample ON does not trigger collapse when imagination is restricted to dt=1, even with trained dt_emb. But disentangling horizon from subsample effects requires additional controls. The key point—that it's not the root cause—is well-supported by 008c's stability despite lower performance.\n142\t\n143\t### C6: dt=1 imagination delays but does not eliminate CIVO collapse — the vulnerability is the trained dt_embedding itself\n144\t\n145\t**⚠️ D038 重大修正**: 008e_v3_s2 (seed=2) **崩溃** — eval 715@95K → 452 → 137@105K, nve=3.78x@105K, 全程 gate_dt_mean=1.0（dt=1 imagination 从未违反）。\n146\t\n147\t原假设\"dt=1 imagination = practical mitigation\"被**否证**。跨 seed 数据：\n148\t- seed 1: eval=869.9@105K ✓（95% baseline, nve=0.80x）\n149\t- seed 2: eval=137@105K ✗（崩溃, nve=3.78x）\n150\t- **独立 seed 崩溃率: 1/2 (50%)**\n151\t\n152\t对比 008d (trained dt_emb + dt=2 imagination): 同样 1/2 崩溃率（seed 1 collapse, seed 2 self-correct@268）。dt=1 imagination **未降低崩溃概率**。\n153\t\n154\t**⚠️ D039 机制辨析 — 008e_v3_s2 的崩溃可能不是 CIVO**\n155\t\n156\tReviewer 深度分析揭示 008e_v3_s2 与 008d 的崩溃动力学**定性不同**：\n157\t\n158\t| 特征 | 008d s1 (经典 CIVO) | 008e_v3_s2 (待确认) |\n159\t|------|---------------------|---------------------|\n160\t| value 变化 | 爆炸 (60→138, +130%@55K) | 温和 (339→384→340, +13%→-12%) |\n161\t| eval 变化 | 后跌 (146→83, -43%) | **先跌** (715→452→137, -81%) |\n162\t| nve 驱动 | value 分子驱动（value 爆炸） | eval 分母驱动（eval 崩溃） |\n163\t| actor_entropy | ↓ (-3.47→-4.88) 策略变确定 | **↑** (-5.21→-3.83) 策略变随机 |\n164\t| 机制 | critic overestimate → actor exploit | eval crash → value 滞后跟随 |\n165\t\n166\t**结论**: 008e_v3_s2 的 nve=3.78x 是 eval 崩溃的**结果**，不是 value overestimation 的**原因**。这更像 policy instability 而非 CIVO。\n167\t\n168\t**D040 最终判定**: 009a baseline seed 2 在 85-105K **完全稳定** (618→596→836, 正常波动后恢复)。排除 \"seed 2 本身不稳定\" 假说。**008e_v3_s2 的崩溃是配置导致的**。\n169\t\n170\t| Step | baseline s2 (009a) | 008e_v3 s2 | 差异 |\n171\t|------|-------------------|------------|------|\n172\t| 85K | 618.7 | 715.2 | 008e_v3 略高 |\n173\t| 95K | 596.2 (↓3.6%) | 452.4 (**↓37%**) | 10× 更大跌幅 |\n174\t| 105K | 836.5 (↑40%) | 137.0 (**↓70%**) | baseline 恢复 vs 008e_v3 继续崩 |\n175\t\n176\t**C6 最终状态: PARTIALLY FALSIFIED**\n177\t- dt=1 imagination 不能阻止崩溃（008e_v3: 1/2 seeds 崩溃 vs baseline: 0/2 seeds 崩溃）\n178\t- 崩溃是配置导致的，不是 seed 特异性\n179\t- 但崩溃**机制**与经典 CIVO 不同（见上方对比表）→ trained dt_embedding 可能通过**多条路径**创造脆弱性\n180\t\n181\t**论文表述建议**: \"Restricting imagination to dt=1 does not prevent collapse in the presence of trained dt-embedding (1/2 seeds collapse). While the collapse shares the high nve signature with classic CIVO, the underlying dynamics differ — the collapsed seed exhibits eval-driven destabilization with increasing policy entropy, rather than value-driven exploitation with decreasing entropy. This suggests trained dt-embedding creates vulnerability through multiple pathways, not solely through the value overestimation feedback loop.\"\n182\t\n183\t| Evidence | Experiment | Key Metric |\n184\t|----------|-----------|------------|\n185\t| dt=1 imag, seed 1, healthy | exp_008e_v3_walker_s1 (trained dt_emb, dt=1 imag, H=15) | eval=869.9@105K (95% baseline), nve=0.80x |\n186\t| **dt=1 imag, seed 2, COLLAPSED** | exp_008e_v3_walker_s2 (same config, seed=2) | eval 715@95K → 137@105K, nve ≤0.68x@85K → 1.34x@95K → **3.78x@105K**. gate_dt=1.0 throughout |\n187\t| dt=2 imag, seed 1, collapsed | exp_008d (trained dt_emb, dt=2 imag, H=8) | eval 167→82@55K, nve 1.78x@25K → 2.09x@45K. Onset at 25K |\n188\t| dt=2 imag, seed 2, self-corrected | exp_009b (008d config, seed 2) | eval 268@105K (rising), nve peak 1.62x@45K → self-correction |\n189\t| Onset delay | 008e_v3_s2 vs 008d | 008d onset nve>1.5x at 25-35K; 008e_v3_s2 at 95-105K. Delay ~70K steps |\n190\t| No dt_emb, stable | exp_008f (dt_embed_dim=0, subsample ON) | No collapse, reward_MAE 75x baseline |\n191\t| Untrained dt_emb, stable | exp_008a (untrained, subsample OFF, H=15) | 778@105K (85% baseline), nve 0.59-0.93x |\n192\t\n193\t**Strength**: **Moderate (D040: PARTIALLY FALSIFIED)** — dt=1 imagination 不能阻止崩溃 (1/2 seeds)，但崩溃机制与经典 CIVO 不同 (eval-driven vs value-driven)。009a baseline s2 稳定排除 seed 脆弱假说。核心洞察：trained dt_embedding creates vulnerability through multiple pathways — CIVO (Pathway A, 008d) 和 non-CIVO policy instability (Pathway B, 008e_v3_s2)。dt=1 imagination 消除 Pathway A 但不消除 Pathway B。\n194\t\n195\t---\n196\t\n197\t## Story Arc\n198\t\n199\t### 1. Introduction\n200\t- Temporal abstraction is a long-sought goal in RL (options, macro-actions, skip connections)\n201\t- World-model RL (DreamerV3) offers a natural testbed: modify imagination dt without changing environment interaction\n202\t- Puzzle: all our attempts at variable-rate imagination degrade performance. Why?\n203\t- Contribution: systematic factorial diagnosis identifying trained dt_embedding as the reliable trigger for conditioning-induced value overestimation (CIVO, nve>1.5x), with collapse as a stochastic consequence. The vulnerability is deeper than imagination quality — even dt=1 imagination collapses at the same rate when dt_embedding is trained, revealing that training data dt>1 transitions alone drive the critic exploitation feedback loop\n204\t\n205\t### 2. Background & Related Work\n206\t- Temporal abstraction in model-free RL (options framework, FiGAR, action repeat)\n207\t- World-model RL (Dreamer family, IRIS, TWM)\n208\t- Variable-rate planning in world models (few prior works, mostly positive framing)\n209\t- Value overestimation in model-based RL (delusional bias per Lu et al. 2018 — differentiate from our CIVO)\n210\t\n211\t### 3. Methodology\n212\t- DreamerV3 as base architecture\n213\t- Variable-rate imagination: dt_embedding, temporal subsampling, learned gating\n214\t- **Factorial experiment design**: 2×2×2 grid (dt_embedding trained/untrained × subsample ON/OFF × H=8/15) + baselines\n215\t- Diagnostic metrics: eval return, value/eval ratio as overestimation proxy, learning curves\n216\t\n217\t### 4. Results\n218\t- **Finding 1**: All dt>1 configurations degrade (C1)\n219\t- **Finding 2**: Controlled ablation isolates trained dt_embedding (C2) — the \"smoking gun\" comparison (008c vs 008d)\n220\t- **Finding 3**: Untrained dt_embedding prevents optimistic drift, sustaining near-baseline performance (C3)\n221\t- **Finding 4**: Longer dt amplifies (C4)\n222\t- **Finding 5**: Horizon/subsample effects are secondary (C5)\n223\t- **Finding 6 (partially falsified)**: dt=1 imagination does not prevent collapse (1/2 seeds), but collapsed seed (008e_v3_s2) shows non-CIVO dynamics (eval-driven, entropy ↑) unlike 008d's classic CIVO (value-driven, entropy ↓). 009a baseline s2 stable → collapse is config-caused. Trained dt_embedding creates vulnerability through multiple pathways, not solely through value overestimation (C6, D040)\n224\t\n225\t### 5. Discussion\n226\t- Mechanism: why trained dt_embedding causes CIVO (RSSM fidelity gap at dt>1)\n227\t- RSSM fidelity analysis: 008d reward gap (2.78x) vs 008f reward gap (1.01x) — trained dt_emb 让 reward head 在 dt=2 时产生系统性偏差，这是 critic 被误导的直接来源\n228\t- 008f 的 reward 退化 trade-off：去掉 dt_embedding 避免崩塌但牺牲 reward 预测质量\n229\t- **D040: Multiple vulnerability pathways**: 008e_v3_s2 在 dt=1 imagination 下崩溃 (nve=3.78x@105K)，但机制与 008d 的 CIVO 不同：008d = value explosion → eval decline → entropy ↓；008e_v3_s2 = eval crash → value stable → entropy ↑。009a baseline s2 稳定排除 seed 脆弱。Trained dt_embedding creates vulnerability through multiple pathways: Pathway A (CIVO, value-driven) requires dt>1 imagination; Pathway B (non-CIVO policy instability, eval-driven) operates independently of imagination dt\n230\t- Connection to existing theory: non-delusional Q-learning (Lu et al., 2018), model exploitation in MBRL\n231\t- Why this is specific to world-model RL (model-free temporal abstraction doesn't suffer the same failure mode)\n232\t- **D032: Value self-correction mechanism (009b)**: 009b (008d seed 2) demonstrates that pathological overestimation (nve>1.5x) is not always irreversible. Value peaked at 110.9@65K then monotonically self-corrected to 67.3@105K, while eval simultaneously improved (172→268). Hypothesized mechanism: when the critic's overestimation does not yet dominate the actor's gradient signal (transient vs sustained nve>1.5x), the actor can still learn from real environment returns, creating a corrective force. In 008d seed 1, the sustained overestimation (3 consecutive checkpoints >1.5x at 35K+) overwhelmed this correction. The bifurcation likely depends on early training stochasticity — initialization and data sampling order determine whether the overestimation feedback loop locks in before correction can take hold (consistent with primacy bias in MBRL, Qiao et al. 2023; early outlier runs, Bjorck et al. 2022). This makes nve>1.5x a **necessary condition** for collapse but not sufficient — the temporal persistence of overestimation is the second gate.\n233\t\n234\t### 6. Implications for Future Work\n235\t- **D038 update**: dt=1 imagination is NOT sufficient — trained dt_embedding in critic is the fundamental vulnerability, regardless of imagination strategy\n236\t- Don't use trained dt_embedding for temporal conditioning in imagination-based actor-critic training without safeguards (TAWM shows this works in MPC — the vulnerability is specific to imagination-based training)\n237\t- RSSM prediction quality must improve before variable-rate imagination is viable\n238\t- Potential safeguards: (a) ~~detach dt_embedding gradients from reward head~~ **架构层面无效（reward gradient = 0 by design）。梯度分解揭示 dt_emb 的 93% 梯度来自 observation decoders，7% 来自 KL dynamics，reward 贡献为 0。真正的干预目标是 observation decoder→dt_emb 梯度通路（Full-SGS, exp_018 planned）。**, (b) stop-gradient on dt>1 critic targets, (c) pessimistic value ensemble for dt>1 transitions, (d) uncertainty-gated truncation\n239\t- Need for diagnostic methodology in temporal abstraction research\n240\t\n241\t---\n242\t\n243\t## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n244\t\n245\t### R1: \"Limited to one environment / one base algorithm\"\n246\t**Pre-emption**: Our claims are mechanistic (trained dt_embedding → value overestimation → collapse). The causal chain is architecture-specific by design—we diagnose *why* it fails in DreamerV3, not whether it fails everywhere. The mechanism (RSSM fidelity gap) likely generalizes to other latent-dynamics models but we scope claims conservatively.\n247\t**Mitigation**: Cheetah Run is a planned experiment in our experiment schedule, not a stretch goal. We will include at least one additional DMC environment (Cheetah Run) to demonstrate generality of the mechanism.\n248\t\n249\t### R2: \"No proposed solution — what's the actionable takeaway?\"\n250\t**Pre-emption**: Our primary contribution is diagnostic, not prescriptive. The causal chain (C1-C5) provides clear guidance: trained dt_embedding is the necessary condition for CIVO. Restricting imagination to dt=1 eliminates CIVO (Pathway A) but not all failure modes — 008e_v3 shows 1/2 seeds collapse via a distinct non-CIVO pathway (eval-driven policy instability). The actionable takeaway is that trained dt_embedding creates vulnerability through multiple pathways in imagination-based actor-critic; surface-level fixes are insufficient. Hierarchical approaches (THICK, Hieros) that avoid flat dt-conditioning are the recommended direction.\n251\t**Mitigation**: Present the dual-pathway finding as a key diagnostic contribution (Section 5). dt=1 imagination is a partial mitigation (eliminates CIVO) but not robust (1/2 collapse via Pathway B).\n252\t\n253\t### R3: \"Value/eval ratio is a crude proxy for overestimation\"\n254\t**Pre-emption**: We acknowledge this is a proxy, not a formal divergence measure. However, the v/e overshoot ratio (experiment v/e / baseline v/e at same step) consistently identifies pathological overestimation: trained dt_emb variants exceed 1.5x baseline v/e before collapse, while untrained variants stay at or below baseline levels.\n255\t**Mitigation**: Add distributional analysis of value predictions vs. realized returns if time permits.\n256\t\n257\t### R4: \"The factorial design is incomplete — missing cells in the grid\"\n258\t**Pre-emption**: We prioritized the most informative comparisons. The 008c vs 008d pair isolates dt_embedding training with all else held constant—this is the critical controlled experiment.\n259\t**Mitigation**: Explicitly present the factorial grid, mark which cells are filled, and justify the prioritization.\n260\t\n261\t### R5: \"Novelty concern — model exploitation / value overestimation in MBRL is well-known\"\n262\t**Pre-emption**: General MBRL overestimation is known (Janner et al., 2019), but the specific mechanism through temporal abstraction's dt_embedding is novel. Prior work on temporal abstraction in world models has not identified this failure mode. Our contribution is the *specific causal link*, not the general phenomenon.\n263\t\n264\t### R6: \"All results from a single seed — how robust are the conclusions?\"\n265\t**Pre-emption (D032 update)**: Multi-seed data now available. The nve>1.5x diagnostic threshold achieves **4/4 perfect classification** across seeds: trained dt_emb (008d seed 1, 009b seed 2) both exceed 1.5x; untrained (008f seed 1, 009c seed 2) neither exceeds 1.5x. Collapse itself is stochastic (1/2 trained seeds), which we report transparently — our claim is about the reliable **diagnostic marker** (nve>1.5x), not deterministic collapse.\n266\t**[D040]** C6 partially falsified: 008e_v3_s2 collapsed via non-CIVO pathway (eval-driven, entropy ↑). 009a baseline s2 stable (618→596→836) — rules out seed fragility. Trained dt_embedding creates multiple vulnerability pathways: CIVO (Pathway A, 008d) and policy instability (Pathway B, 008e_v3_s2). dt=1 imagination eliminates Pathway A but not B.\n267\t**Mitigation**: Report all seeds with IQM + confidence intervals per Agarwal et al. (2021). The 4/4 separation is robust. Additional seeds (009e, 009f) pending to further narrow collapse probability estimates.\n268\t\n269\t### R7: \"The factorial design confounds dt_embedding training with training data composition\"\n270\t**Pre-emption**: exp_008f confirmed: dt_embed_dim=0 + subsample ON → normalized v/e 0.83x@45K (healthy), vs 008d@45K=2.09x (collapse at v/e=69.3%). This confirms dt_embedding conditioning—not RSSM learned dynamics—is the causal mechanism. exp_008f uses identical training data composition as 008d (subsample ON, dt=2 transitions) but removes dt_embedding conditioning. Through 55K, 008f remained in normal range (normalized v/e 0.73-1.02x).\n271\t**Mitigation**: The 008f causal control resolves this confound definitively. The three-way comparison (008c: subsample OFF/untrained, 008d: subsample ON/trained, 008f: subsample ON/no dt_embed) isolates dt_embedding conditioning as the necessary and sufficient factor for CIVO.\n272\t\n273\t### R8: \"008f reward prediction degradation undermines the causal control\"\n274\t**Pre-emption**: 008f 的 reward_MAE=0.747（baseline 75x）是重要发现，但不削弱 C2 因果结论。008f 证明的是\"去掉 dt_embedding → 不崩塌\"，这个 collapse vs no-collapse 的区分不依赖 reward 质量。008f 的低 v/e 部分源于奖励噪声，但关键对比是 008d 的 v/e 加速发散 vs 008f 的 v/e 保持稳定——模式差异才是因果证据。\n275\t**Mitigation**: 论文中明确区分 008f 的角色（因果控制实验）和 008e_v3 的角色（诊断证据：dt=1 imagination 不能消除 CIVO）。RSSM fidelity 表同时报告绝对误差和比率，避免审稿人被 008d 的低 obs gap ratio（1.30x，但绝对误差 41.31 已是 baseline 3x）误导。\n276\t\n277\t---\n278\t\n279\t## Experiment Coverage Assessment\n280\t\n281\t| Claim | Evidence Status | Gaps |\n282\t|-------|----------------|------|\n283\t| C1 (degradation) | ✅ Strong — 3 configurations (005, 006, 007) all negative | Could add 1 more environment |\n284\t| C2 (reliable pathological overestimation) | ⚠️ Moderate-strong (D032) — nve>1.5x threshold: 4/4 perfect separation (trained 2/2 exceed, untrained 0/2 exceed). Collapse stochastic: 1/2 trained seeds. Causal direction unambiguous. | 009e (008d seed 1) to narrow collapse probability; 009f (008f seed 1) for third untrained seed; 009c→200K |\n285\t| C3 (preventing optimistic drift) | ✅ Strong (105K confirmed) — 008a@105K=778 (85% baseline), normalized v/e=0.59x, two V-shaped recoveries | None — long-term structural suppression confirmed |\n286\t| C4 (dt amplification) | ⚠️ Moderate — only dt=2 vs dt=4 | Would benefit from dt=3, but not blocking |\n287\t| C5 (horizon/subsample) | ⚠️ Moderate — confounded with other factors | Need 008c-equivalent with H=15 + subsample ON + untrained dt_emb for clean isolation |\n288\t| C6 (dt=1 imag as mitigation) | ⚠️ **D040: PARTIALLY FALSIFIED** — s2 collapsed (nve=3.78x) but via non-CIVO pathway (eval-driven, entropy ↑). 009a baseline s2 stable → config-caused. **Repositioned**: trained dt_embedding creates multiple vulnerability pathways; dt=1 imagination eliminates CIVO pathway but not policy instability pathway. Onset delay ~70K steps is a positive finding. | Need s3-s4 to narrow collapse rate. Characterize Pathway B mechanism. Consider: trained dt_emb + subsample OFF + dt=1 imag (no dt>1 in training data) |\n289\t\n290\t### Priority Actions Before Submission\n291\t1. ~~**Check exp_008a@65K**~~ — ✅ Done: 608 (-1.3% baseline), B+ confirmed\n292\t2. ~~**008e_v3 B+ confound**~~ — ✅ Done: 869.9@105K (95% baseline), B+ confirmed\n293\t3. ~~**008f C2 causal control**~~ — ✅ Done: V-recovery to 503@105K, normalized v/e=0.30x\n294\t4. ~~**008e_v3_s2 (seed=2)**~~ — ❌ D040: **COLLAPSED via non-CIVO pathway** (eval-driven, entropy ↑). C6 partially falsified — multiple vulnerability pathways.\n295\t5. ~~**Multi-seed replication**~~ — ✅ D040: 009a baseline s2 DONE (stable). All Walker Walk seeds complete.\n296\t6. ~~**Narrative pivot**~~ — ✅ D040: C6 reframed as \"multiple vulnerability pathways\" (CIVO Pathway A + non-CIVO Pathway B)\n297\t6.5. ~~**SGS (reward_dt_emb_detach) 验证**~~ — ❌ exp_016e: reward_dt_emb_detach 是 no-op（梯度验证揭示 reward gradient = 0 by architecture）。016e 与 008d 的差异（onset timing, peak height）归因于随机变异，非 SGS 效果。dt_emb 的 93% 梯度来自 observation decoders，7% 来自 KL dynamics，reward=0。\n298\t7. **考虑新实验**: trained dt_emb + subsample OFF + dt=1 imag — 如果 stable → 确认 training data dt>1 transitions 是 trigger\n299\t8. **Cheetah Run cross-environment** — P0 priority, addresses R1\n300\t9. **Figures** — learning curves, normalized v/e (含 008e_v3_s2 崩溃轨迹), RSSM fidelity table\n301\t10. **Threshold sensitivity analysis** — plan_034, 0 GPU cost\n302\t\n303\t---\n304\t\n305\t## Key Figures (Planned)\n306\t\n307\t1. **Figure 1**: Learning curves — all experiments overlaid, showing degradation pattern (hero figure)\n308\t2. **Figure 2**: Value/eval ratio over training — separating healthy vs. pathological regimes\n309\t3. **Figure 3**: Factorial grid summary — 2×2 (dt_emb × subsample) with H as secondary axis\n310\t4. **Figure 4**: Causal diagram — dt_embedding → value overestimation → actor degradation → collapse\n311\t5. **Figure 5**: dt=1 vs dt=2 vs dt=4 comparison — monotonic degradation (C4)\n312\t6. **Table 1**: RSSM fidelity — absolute obs_MSE + reward_MAE at dt=1/dt=2, 三模型并排（绝对值 + 比率，避免比率误导）\n313\t7. **Figure 6 (D035)**: Mitigation comparison — plot 008e_v3 seed=1 as single line. Add s2 (seed=2) when done. Do NOT present s0+s1 as two independent seeds. Can footnote deterministic reproduction.\n314\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"522f0308-de2f-49f8-9b4c-8bcbe609d7cd","uuid":"63b044a7-c05f-4055-914a-06caff774dcf","timestamp":"2026-05-16T12:26:10.197Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_9/projects/event-gated-adaptive-horizon/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260516T115400/median/paper_framing.md","content":"# Paper Framing: Diagnostic Analysis of Temporal Abstraction in World-Model RL\n\n## Title Candidates\n\n1. **\"Why Temporal Abstraction Fails in DreamerV3: A Diagnostic Analysis of Variable-Rate Imagination\"**\n2. ~~**\"The Delusional Critic Problem in DreamerV3's Variable-Rate Imagination\"**~~ (deprecated — \"delusional critic\" conflicts with Lu et al. 2018)\n3. **\"Diagnosing Value Overestimation from Temporal Abstraction in DreamerV3\"** ← preferred after D037 terminology decision\n4. **\"Conditioning-Induced Value Overestimation in Variable-Rate World-Model Imagination\"**\n\n**⚠️ D037 术语决定**: \"delusional critic\" → **\"conditioning-induced value overestimation\"** (简称 CIVO)。Lu et al. 2018 的 delusional bias 指 policy-class constraints 导致不可实现的 Q 值，本文机制是 model imprecision + learned conditioning amplification，概念不同。Dream 模拟三档审稿一致指出术语误导。全文替换：\"delusional critic\" → \"conditioning-induced value overestimation\"，可 cite Lu et al. 2018 as analogy but must explicitly differentiate。\n\n**⚠️ D037 TAWM 对比（关键 novelty 升级）**: TAWM (Nhu et al., ICML 2025) 在 TD-MPC2 (MPC setting) 中成功使用 dt-conditioning。本文发现 dt-conditioning 在 imagination-based actor-critic 中失败 → **novelty 从 \"known failure class 的 specific instance\" 升级为 \"dt-conditioning succeeds in MPC but fails in imagination — here's the architecture-specific vulnerability\"**。TAWM 是强化 novelty 的最关键参考文献。\n\n## Venue\n\n**CoRL 2026** — Deadline: 2026-05-29\n\n## Narrative Positioning\n\nThis is a **diagnostic analysis paper**, not a method paper. We do not propose a new algorithm. Instead, we provide a systematic, controlled investigation answering: *Why does temporal abstraction fail in world-model RL?*\n\nThe contribution is a **strongly supported causal chain** from mechanism to failure mode, backed by a factorial experiment design that isolates each factor, plus a **practical mitigation guideline** (restrict imagination to dt=1 during multi-dt training → 95% baseline recovery). Diagnostic papers are valued when they (a) address a widely-attempted but poorly-understood failure, (b) provide actionable insight for future method design, and (c) are methodologically rigorous.\n\nWe scope all claims to **DreamerV3 and its RSSM world model**. Generalizability to other latent-dynamics architectures is discussed but not claimed.\n\n---\n\n## Core Claims\n\n### C1: Variable-rate imagination in DreamerV3 consistently degrades performance\n\nTemporal abstraction via variable dt—whether learned or fixed—degrades asymptotic performance relative to the dt=1 baseline across all tested configurations.\n\n| Evidence | Experiment | Key Metric |\n|----------|-----------|------------|\n| Baseline stability | exp_001_baseline (dt=1) | Stable convergence ~950 eval return |\n| Fixed dt=2 collapse | exp_006_fixed_dt2 (dt=2, subsample ON, H=15) | Peak 485@35K → collapse to ~135@65K |\n| Fixed dt=4 severe degradation | exp_007_fixed_dt4 (dt=4) | Peak 176.9@25K, 144.4@45K (33% of baseline@45K=443) |\n| Learned gate collapse | exp_005 (learned gate) | Peak 541@55K → 99@145K |\n\n**Strength**: Strong. Three independent dt>1 configurations all show degradation. No positive result exists for variable-rate imagination in our setup.\n\n### C2: Trained dt_embedding reliably triggers pathological value overestimation (>1.5x), creating high collapse risk\n\n**D032 update: nve>1.5x threshold has perfect separating power (4/4); collapse itself is stochastic.**\n\nThe trained dt_embedding reliably induces pathological value overestimation (normalized v/e >1.5x baseline) across all tested seeds, but whether this overestimation progresses to catastrophic performance collapse is stochastic:\n\n- **WITH trained dt_emb**: 2/2 seeds exceed nve>1.5x at 35K+ (008d seed 1: sustained 1.71x@35K→2.09x@45K→4.12x@55K → collapse; 009b seed 2: 1.62x@45K → self-correction to 0.38x@105K)\n- **WITHOUT dt_emb (008f)**: 0/2 seeds exceed nve>1.5x at 35K+ (seed 1 peak 1.02x@25K; seed 2 peak 1.26x@25K)\n- **1.5x threshold (35K+)**: 4/4 perfect classification — reliable diagnostic marker separating trained vs untrained dt_emb\n\n**⚠️ 时间限定**: nve>1.5x 诊断适用于 **35K+ steps**（此时 baseline v/e 已充分收敛至 >0.24，比率稳定可靠）。25K 处 baseline v/e 仅 0.087，微小绝对差异可被放大为大比率（如 009b@25K nve=2.05x 和 008e_v3@25K nve=1.93x 均为 false positive）。论文中报告 threshold 时必须标注此限定。\n\n**Sustained vs transient overestimation**: The key difference between collapse (008d seed 1) and self-correction (009b seed 2) is the **temporal persistence** of nve>1.5x. Seed 1 maintained nve>1.5x across 3 consecutive checkpoints at 35K+ (1.71x@35K→2.09x@45K→4.12x@55K, accelerating), locking the positive feedback loop; seed 2 exceeded 1.5x at 45K (1.62x) but dropped to 1.41x@55K and continued declining, allowing self-correction. This suggests a \"point of no return\" where sustained overestimation becomes irreversible, with early training stochasticity (initialization, data sampling order) determining which path is taken (cf. Henderson et al. 2018, Bjorck et al. 2022, Qiao et al. 2023).\n\nThe trained dt_embedding enables precise dt prediction in imagination, but RSSM state prediction quality at dt>1 is insufficient to support this precision. The result is conditioning-induced value overestimation (CIVO) that drives actor learning toward incorrect policies. Without dt_embedding conditioning, the overestimation feedback loop is blocked entirely (nve never exceeds 1.5x).\n\n| Evidence | Experiment | Key Metric |\n|----------|-----------|------------|\n| Untrained dt_emb, no collapse | exp_008c (dt=2, subsample OFF, untrained dt_emb, H=8) | eval plateau ~242@55K; v/e: @35K=21.8% (0.90x) → @55K=29.1% (0.72x) → @65K=32.8% (0.58x) (declining normalized v/e, ~7x slower than trained) |\n| Trained dt_emb, collapse | exp_008d (dt=2, subsample ON, trained dt_emb, H=8) | eval declining 167→146→141 @25K→35K→45K; v/e: @25K=15.5% (1.78x) → @35K=41.2% (1.71x) → @45K=69.3% (2.09x) (accelerating overestimation) |\n| Both trained dt_emb → accelerating v/e | exp_006 (H=15) vs exp_008d (H=8) | 006: v/e @35K=31.1% (1.29x)→@45K=53.3% (1.61x)→@55K=93.3% (2.31x); 008d: v/e @35K=41.2% (1.71x)→@45K=69.3% (2.09x) — both show accelerating normalized v/e leading to collapse |\n| Baseline v/e calibration (overshoot reference) | exp_001_baseline | v/e@35K=24.1%, @45K=33.1%, @55K=40.4%. Overshoot ratio: 008d@35K=1.7x, @45K=2.1x; 006@45K=1.6x, @55K=2.3x (all >1.5x = pathological) |\n| **D032: Trained dt_emb seed 2 — pathological but self-correcting** | exp_009b (008d config, seed 2) | eval 39→141→164→141→172→209→197→236→279→268@105K (**no collapse**, rising). nve: **2.05x@25K**→1.24x→**1.62x@45K**→1.41x→0.94x→0.66x→0.51x→0.43x→**0.38x@105K**. Transient nve>1.5x with self-correction. Value peaked 110.9@65K → self-corrected to 67.3@105K while eval improved. |\n| **1.5x threshold separating power** | 008d+009b vs 008f+009c | WITH trained dt_emb: 2/2 seeds exceed nve>1.5x (008d peak 2.09x sustained, 009b peak 2.05x transient). WITHOUT dt_emb: 0/2 seeds exceed nve>1.5x (008f peak 1.02x, 009c peak 1.26x). **4/4 perfect classification.** |\n| **Causal control: dt_embed_dim=0 (direction confirmed, seed-dependent)** | exp_008f seed 1 + exp_009c seed 2 | **Seed 1**: eval 197→207→194→173→503@105K (zigzag recovery); nve 1.02x→0.73x→0.83x→0.97x→0.30x. **Seed 2**: eval 169→211→239→359→227→191→148@105K (decline after 75K); nve 1.26x→0.79x→0.72x→0.33x→0.60x→0.90x→1.14x. Both vs 008d@45K nve=2.09x: onset delayed 60K+ steps, severity reduced ~2x. **009c extending to 200K to observe if nve crosses 1.5x.** |\n| **Reward SGS = no-op (reward gradient = 0 by architecture)** | exp_016e (reward_dt_emb_detach=True, subsample ON, H=8, seed 1) | Peak eval 326.7@75K → collapse 31.7@95K, NVE=10.42x. 梯度验证揭示 reward loss 对 dt_emb.weight 贡献为 0——reward_dt_emb_detach 是 no-op。016e 与 008d 的差异（onset timing, peak height）归因于随机变异。**dt_emb 的 93% 梯度来自 observation decoders（orientations/velocity/height），7% 来自 KL dynamics，reward=0。** |\n| RSSM fidelity analysis | baseline / 008d / 008f | See RSSM Fidelity Table below |\n\n**⚠️ 008f 定位：因果控制实验，非推荐方案。** 008f 去掉 dt_embedding 后 reward_MAE=0.747（baseline 的 75x），reward head 无法区分 dt=1/dt=2（reward gap 仅 1.01x vs 008d 的 2.78x）。**D030 更新**：008f seed 2 (009c) 在 105K 达到 nve=1.14x（borderline），seed 1 为 0.30x——protection 不 robust。但 008f 两个 seed 的 v/e 增长速度均显著慢于 008d（008d@45K=2.09x vs 008f seed 2@105K=1.14x），确认 dt_embedding 是 primary amplifier。**C2 因果方向成立，但 \"eliminates\" 改为 \"significantly delays and reduces\"。推荐方案仍是 008e_v3（C6）**。\n\n**RSSM Fidelity Table（论文中必须同时报告绝对误差和比率）：**\n\n| Model | dt1 obs_MSE | dt2 obs_MSE | obs gap | dt1 reward_MAE | dt2 reward_MAE | reward gap |\n|-------|------------|------------|---------|---------------|---------------|------------|\n| baseline (exp_001) | 13.49 | 27.50 | **2.04x** | 0.0102 | 0.0159 | 1.56x |\n| 008d (trained dt_emb, collapsed) | 41.31 | 53.67 | **1.30x** | 0.0568 | 0.1579 | **2.78x** |\n| 008f (no dt_emb, stable) | 8.01 | 22.04 | **2.75x** | 0.7469 | 0.7526 | 1.01x |\n\n**解读要点**：008d 的低 obs gap ratio (1.30x) 具有误导性——其 dt=1 绝对误差本身已是 baseline 的 3x（模型已崩塌）。论文中必须并排呈现绝对误差和比率，避免审稿人被比率误导。\n\n**dt_emb Gradient Decomposition (D061)**\n\n梯度验证（torch.autograd.grad analysis, commit 5fc8e49）揭示 dt_emb.weight 的梯度来源分布：\n\n| Source | Grad Norm | Fraction |\n|--------|-----------|----------|\n| Observation decoders (orientations, velocity, height) | 1.505 | 93% |\n| KL dynamics | 0.119 | 7% |\n| Cont head | ~0 (included in decoder total) | <1% |\n| **Reward head** | **0.000** | **0%** |\n| **Total** | **1.624** | **100%** |\n\n这意味着：(1) 016e 的 reward_dt_emb_detach 是 no-op——detach 了一个不存在的梯度通路；(2) dt_emb 的训练几乎完全由 observation reconstruction loss 驱动；(3) Full-SGS（exp_018）移除 observation decoder 梯度后仅保留 7% KL 梯度，是真正的 gradient-level intervention。\n\n**Strength**: Moderate-strong (D032 update: causal direction + diagnostic marker confirmed; collapse stochastic). The **nve>1.5x threshold** achieves 4/4 perfect separation: all trained dt_emb seeds (008d, 009b) exceed 1.5x; no untrained seeds (008f, 009c) exceed 1.5x. This is the paper's strongest quantitative result for C2. **Collapse** is stochastic (1/2 trained seeds): 008d seed 1 shows sustained nve>1.5x → collapse; 009b seed 2 shows transient nve>1.5x → self-correction. The **causal direction** is unambiguous across all 4 data points. Pending: 009e (008d seed 1) to determine collapse probability (1/3 vs 2/3); 009f (008f seed 1) for third untrained seed; 009c extension to 200K.\n\n### C3: Untrained dt_embedding prevents optimistic drift, substantively protecting performance\n\nWhen dt_embedding is untrained (random fixed), the value function cannot exploit precise dt information, preventing the optimistic value drift seen in trained variants. This value growth suppression substantively protects performance: exp_008a demonstrates two complete zigzag recovery cycles over 105K steps (peak @105K=778, 85% of baseline 916), with normalized v/e consistently suppressed at 0.59-0.93x baseline. The trained dt_embedding variant (exp_006) collapses catastrophically as its v/e ratio accelerates far past baseline levels (2.3x baseline@55K).\n\n| Evidence | Experiment | Key Metric |\n|----------|-----------|------------|\n| Two V-shaped recoveries (105K) | exp_008a (dt=2, subsample OFF, untrained dt_emb, H=15) | 501→563→488→608→470→584→572→778; two complete recovery cycles over 105K steps. @65K=608 (-1.3% baseline), @105K=778 (-15.1% baseline, 85% of 916). No collapse. |\n| Sustained v/e suppression | exp_008a | v/e: 37.6%@55K → 37.2%@65K → 53.6%@75K → 47.5%@85K → 49.7%@95K → 38.6%@105K. Normalized v/e: 0.93x→0.66x→0.75x→0.68x→0.79x→0.59x baseline. Consistently below 1.0x, no acceleration. |\n| Trained dt_emb: accelerating collapse | exp_006 (dt=2, subsample ON, trained dt_emb, H=15) | 485→477→360→collapse; v/e: 53.3%@45K (1.61x)→93.3%@55K (2.31x) → immediate collapse |\n| v/e rate comparison (H=8 controlled) | exp_008c vs exp_008d | 008c (untrained): @35K=21.8% (0.90x)→@65K=32.8% (0.58x) (normalized declining); 008d (trained): @25K=15.5% (1.78x)→@35K=41.2% (1.71x) (normalized >1.5x = pathological). ~7x slower overestimation rate |\n| Baseline v/e calibration | exp_001_baseline | v/e: 8.7%@25K → 24.1%@35K → 40.4%@55K → 56.3%@65K → 65.6%@105K (stable 69-72% from 135K+). 008a normalized v/e: 0.93x@55K → 0.66x@65K → 0.59x@105K (suppressed, diverging further below baseline); 008d: 1.7x@35K → 2.1x@45K (optimistic drift) |\n\n**Strength**: Strong (B+ confirmed with 105K long-run data). Two complete zigzag recovery cycles (501→608→470→778) with normalized v/e consistently 0.59-0.93x baseline across 105K steps. This is structural value growth suppression, not a transient delay. Contrasts sharply with exp_006's accelerating v/e (1.6x→2.3x baseline → collapse). The mechanism is clear: untrained dt_embedding prevents the value function from exploiting precise (but wrong) dt>1 predictions, suppressing the optimistic value drift that drives the CIVO feedback loop in trained variants.\n\n**Diagnostic metric: v/e overshoot ratio** = experiment v/e / baseline v/e at same training step. >1.5x = pathological warning. Trained dt_emb variants: 008d@35K=1.7x, @45K=2.1x; 006@45K=1.6x, @55K=2.3x. Untrained: 008a normalized v/e range 0.59-0.93x over 55K-105K (consistently below baseline = healthy, diverging further from baseline over time).\n\n**105K long-run confirmed**: exp_008a ran to 112K steps. Normalized v/e at 105K=0.59x baseline (lowest point), confirming value growth suppression is structural, not a transient delay. The suppression strengthens over time rather than converging to baseline levels.\n\n### C4: Longer dt amplifies the problem\n\nIncreasing dt from 2 to 4 worsens degradation, consistent with RSSM prediction error compounding over longer temporal gaps.\n\n| Evidence | Experiment | Key Metric |\n|----------|-----------|------------|\n| dt=2 degradation | exp_006_fixed_dt2 | Peak 485, collapse to ~135 |\n| dt=4 severe degradation | exp_007_fixed_dt4 | Peak 176.9@25K, 144.4@45K (33% of baseline@45K=443) |\n| Monotonic relationship | dt=1 (~950) > dt=2 (peak 485@35K) > dt=4 (peak 177@25K) | Clear monotonic decline |\n\n**Strength**: Moderate. Only two dt values tested (2 and 4). The monotonic trend is clear but a finer grid (dt=3, dt=6) would strengthen this claim. Sufficient for a supporting claim but not a standalone finding.\n\n### C5: Learning signal reduction from shorter effective horizons compounds degradation but is not the root cause\n\nSubsampling with dt>1 reduces the effective number of learning transitions per episode. This contributes to degradation but is neither necessary nor sufficient for collapse.\n\n| Evidence | Experiment | Key Metric |\n|----------|-----------|------------|\n| Subsample OFF, still degraded | exp_008c (subsample OFF, untrained dt_emb, H=8) | eval ~242 vs baseline ~950 |\n| Subsample OFF, no collapse | exp_008a (subsample OFF, untrained dt_emb, H=15) | eval 778@105K (85% baseline), no collapse through 112K steps |\n| Subsample ON, collapse | exp_008d (subsample ON, trained dt_emb, H=8) | eval 146, value overestimation |\n| Horizon effect | exp_008c (H=8) vs exp_008a (H=15) | H=15 significantly outperforms H=8, both subsample OFF |\n| Subsample ON, trained dt_emb, dt=1 imag, no collapse | exp_008e_v3 (subsample ON, trained dt_emb, dt=1 imag, H=15) | eval 382@45K (86% baseline); normalized v/e 0.88x@45K (normal). Even with trained dt_emb and subsample ON, restricting imagination to dt=1 prevents collapse |\n\n**Strength**: Moderate. The 008c vs 008a comparison shows horizon length matters independently. exp_008e_v3 confirms subsample ON does not trigger collapse when imagination is restricted to dt=1, even with trained dt_emb. But disentangling horizon from subsample effects requires additional controls. The key point—that it's not the root cause—is well-supported by 008c's stability despite lower performance.\n\n### C6: dt=1 imagination delays but does not eliminate CIVO collapse — the vulnerability is the trained dt_embedding itself\n\n**⚠️ D038 重大修正**: 008e_v3_s2 (seed=2) **崩溃** — eval 715@95K → 452 → 137@105K, nve=3.78x@105K, 全程 gate_dt_mean=1.0（dt=1 imagination 从未违反）。\n\n原假设\"dt=1 imagination = practical mitigation\"被**否证**。跨 seed 数据：\n- seed 1: eval=869.9@105K ✓（95% baseline, nve=0.80x）\n- seed 2: eval=137@105K ✗（崩溃, nve=3.78x）\n- **独立 seed 崩溃率: 1/2 (50%)**\n\n对比 008d (trained dt_emb + dt=2 imagination): 同样 1/2 崩溃率（seed 1 collapse, seed 2 self-correct@268）。dt=1 imagination **未降低崩溃概率**。\n\n**⚠️ D039 机制辨析 — 008e_v3_s2 的崩溃可能不是 CIVO**\n\nReviewer 深度分析揭示 008e_v3_s2 与 008d 的崩溃动力学**定性不同**：\n\n| 特征 | 008d s1 (经典 CIVO) | 008e_v3_s2 (待确认) |\n|------|---------------------|---------------------|\n| value 变化 | 爆炸 (60→138, +130%@55K) | 温和 (339→384→340, +13%→-12%) |\n| eval 变化 | 后跌 (146→83, -43%) | **先跌** (715→452→137, -81%) |\n| nve 驱动 | value 分子驱动（value 爆炸） | eval 分母驱动（eval 崩溃） |\n| actor_entropy | ↓ (-3.47→-4.88) 策略变确定 | **↑** (-5.21→-3.83) 策略变随机 |\n| 机制 | critic overestimate → actor exploit | eval crash → value 滞后跟随 |\n\n**结论**: 008e_v3_s2 的 nve=3.78x 是 eval 崩溃的**结果**，不是 value overestimation 的**原因**。这更像 policy instability 而非 CIVO。\n\n**D040 最终判定**: 009a baseline seed 2 在 85-105K **完全稳定** (618→596→836, 正常波动后恢复)。排除 \"seed 2 本身不稳定\" 假说。**008e_v3_s2 的崩溃是配置导致的**。\n\n| Step | baseline s2 (009a) | 008e_v3 s2 | 差异 |\n|------|-------------------|------------|------|\n| 85K | 618.7 | 715.2 | 008e_v3 略高 |\n| 95K | 596.2 (↓3.6%) | 452.4 (**↓37%**) | 10× 更大跌幅 |\n| 105K | 836.5 (↑40%) | 137.0 (**↓70%**) | baseline 恢复 vs 008e_v3 继续崩 |\n\n**C6 最终状态: PARTIALLY FALSIFIED**\n- dt=1 imagination 不能阻止崩溃（008e_v3: 1/2 seeds 崩溃 vs baseline: 0/2 seeds 崩溃）\n- 崩溃是配置导致的，不是 seed 特异性\n- 但崩溃**机制**与经典 CIVO 不同（见上方对比表）→ trained dt_embedding 可能通过**多条路径**创造脆弱性\n\n**论文表述建议**: \"Restricting imagination to dt=1 does not prevent collapse in the presence of trained dt-embedding (1/2 seeds collapse). While the collapse shares the high nve signature with classic CIVO, the underlying dynamics differ — the collapsed seed exhibits eval-driven destabilization with increasing policy entropy, rather than value-driven exploitation with decreasing entropy. This suggests trained dt-embedding creates vulnerability through multiple pathways, not solely through the value overestimation feedback loop.\"\n\n| Evidence | Experiment | Key Metric |\n|----------|-----------|------------|\n| dt=1 imag, seed 1, healthy | exp_008e_v3_walker_s1 (trained dt_emb, dt=1 imag, H=15) | eval=869.9@105K (95% baseline), nve=0.80x |\n| **dt=1 imag, seed 2, COLLAPSED** | exp_008e_v3_walker_s2 (same config, seed=2) | eval 715@95K → 137@105K, nve ≤0.68x@85K → 1.34x@95K → **3.78x@105K**. gate_dt=1.0 throughout |\n| dt=2 imag, seed 1, collapsed | exp_008d (trained dt_emb, dt=2 imag, H=8) | eval 167→82@55K, nve 1.78x@25K → 2.09x@45K. Onset at 25K |\n| dt=2 imag, seed 2, self-corrected | exp_009b (008d config, seed 2) | eval 268@105K (rising), nve peak 1.62x@45K → self-correction |\n| Onset delay | 008e_v3_s2 vs 008d | 008d onset nve>1.5x at 25-35K; 008e_v3_s2 at 95-105K. Delay ~70K steps |\n| No dt_emb, stable | exp_008f (dt_embed_dim=0, subsample ON) | No collapse, reward_MAE 75x baseline |\n| Untrained dt_emb, stable | exp_008a (untrained, subsample OFF, H=15) | 778@105K (85% baseline), nve 0.59-0.93x |\n\n**Strength**: **Moderate (D040: PARTIALLY FALSIFIED)** — dt=1 imagination 不能阻止崩溃 (1/2 seeds)，但崩溃机制与经典 CIVO 不同 (eval-driven vs value-driven)。009a baseline s2 稳定排除 seed 脆弱假说。核心洞察：trained dt_embedding creates vulnerability through multiple pathways — CIVO (Pathway A, 008d) 和 non-CIVO policy instability (Pathway B, 008e_v3_s2)。dt=1 imagination 消除 Pathway A 但不消除 Pathway B。\n\n---\n\n## Story Arc\n\n### 1. Introduction\n- Temporal abstraction is a long-sought goal in RL (options, macro-actions, skip connections)\n- World-model RL (DreamerV3) offers a natural testbed: modify imagination dt without changing environment interaction\n- Puzzle: all our attempts at variable-rate imagination degrade performance. Why?\n- Contribution: systematic factorial diagnosis identifying trained dt_embedding as the reliable trigger for conditioning-induced value overestimation (CIVO, nve>1.5x), with collapse as a stochastic consequence. The vulnerability is deeper than imagination quality — even dt=1 imagination collapses at the same rate when dt_embedding is trained, revealing that training data dt>1 transitions alone drive the critic exploitation feedback loop\n\n### 2. Background & Related Work\n- Temporal abstraction in model-free RL (options framework, FiGAR, action repeat)\n- World-model RL (Dreamer family, IRIS, TWM)\n- Variable-rate planning in world models (few prior works, mostly positive framing)\n- Value overestimation in model-based RL (delusional bias per Lu et al. 2018 — differentiate from our CIVO)\n\n### 3. Methodology\n- DreamerV3 as base architecture\n- Variable-rate imagination: dt_embedding, temporal subsampling, learned gating\n- **Factorial experiment design**: 2×2×2 grid (dt_embedding trained/untrained × subsample ON/OFF × H=8/15) + baselines\n- Diagnostic metrics: eval return, value/eval ratio as overestimation proxy, learning curves\n\n### 4. Results\n- **Finding 1**: All dt>1 configurations degrade (C1)\n- **Finding 2**: Controlled ablation isolates trained dt_embedding (C2) — the \"smoking gun\" comparison (008c vs 008d)\n- **Finding 3**: Untrained dt_embedding prevents optimistic drift, sustaining near-baseline performance (C3)\n- **Finding 4**: Longer dt amplifies (C4)\n- **Finding 5**: Horizon/subsample effects are secondary (C5)\n- **Finding 6 (partially falsified)**: dt=1 imagination does not prevent collapse (1/2 seeds), but collapsed seed (008e_v3_s2) shows non-CIVO dynamics (eval-driven, entropy ↑) unlike 008d's classic CIVO (value-driven, entropy ↓). 009a baseline s2 stable → collapse is config-caused. Trained dt_embedding creates vulnerability through multiple pathways, not solely through value overestimation (C6, D040)\n\n### 5. Discussion\n- Mechanism: why trained dt_embedding causes CIVO (RSSM fidelity gap at dt>1)\n- RSSM fidelity analysis: 008d reward gap (2.78x) vs 008f reward gap (1.01x) — trained dt_emb 让 reward head 在 dt=2 时产生系统性偏差，这是 critic 被误导的直接来源\n- 008f 的 reward 退化 trade-off：去掉 dt_embedding 避免崩塌但牺牲 reward 预测质量\n- **D040: Multiple vulnerability pathways**: 008e_v3_s2 在 dt=1 imagination 下崩溃 (nve=3.78x@105K)，但机制与 008d 的 CIVO 不同：008d = value explosion → eval decline → entropy ↓；008e_v3_s2 = eval crash → value stable → entropy ↑。009a baseline s2 稳定排除 seed 脆弱。Trained dt_embedding creates vulnerability through multiple pathways: Pathway A (CIVO, value-driven) requires dt>1 imagination; Pathway B (non-CIVO policy instability, eval-driven) operates independently of imagination dt\n- Connection to existing theory: non-delusional Q-learning (Lu et al., 2018), model exploitation in MBRL\n- Why this is specific to world-model RL (model-free temporal abstraction doesn't suffer the same failure mode)\n- **D032: Value self-correction mechanism (009b)**: 009b (008d seed 2) demonstrates that pathological overestimation (nve>1.5x) is not always irreversible. Value peaked at 110.9@65K then monotonically self-corrected to 67.3@105K, while eval simultaneously improved (172→268). Hypothesized mechanism: when the critic's overestimation does not yet dominate the actor's gradient signal (transient vs sustained nve>1.5x), the actor can still learn from real environment returns, creating a corrective force. In 008d seed 1, the sustained overestimation (3 consecutive checkpoints >1.5x at 35K+) overwhelmed this correction. The bifurcation likely depends on early training stochasticity — initialization and data sampling order determine whether the overestimation feedback loop locks in before correction can take hold (consistent with primacy bias in MBRL, Qiao et al. 2023; early outlier runs, Bjorck et al. 2022). This makes nve>1.5x a **necessary condition** for collapse but not sufficient — the temporal persistence of overestimation is the second gate.\n\n### 6. Implications for Future Work\n- **D038 update**: dt=1 imagination is NOT sufficient — trained dt_embedding in critic is the fundamental vulnerability, regardless of imagination strategy\n- Don't use trained dt_embedding for temporal conditioning in imagination-based actor-critic training without safeguards (TAWM shows this works in MPC — the vulnerability is specific to imagination-based training)\n- RSSM prediction quality must improve before variable-rate imagination is viable\n- Potential safeguards: (a) ~~detach dt_embedding gradients from reward head~~ **架构层面无效（reward gradient = 0 by design）。梯度分解揭示 dt_emb 的 93% 梯度来自 observation decoders，7% 来自 KL dynamics，reward 贡献为 0。真正的干预目标是 observation decoder→dt_emb 梯度通路（Full-SGS, exp_018 planned）。**, (b) stop-gradient on dt>1 critic targets, (c) pessimistic value ensemble for dt>1 transitions, (d) uncertainty-gated truncation\n- Need for diagnostic methodology in temporal abstraction research\n\n---\n\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n\n### R1: \"Limited to one environment / one base algorithm\"\n**Pre-emption**: Our claims are mechanistic (trained dt_embedding → value overestimation → collapse). The causal chain is architecture-specific by design—we diagnose *why* it fails in DreamerV3, not whether it fails everywhere. The mechanism (RSSM fidelity gap) likely generalizes to other latent-dynamics models but we scope claims conservatively.\n**Mitigation**: Cheetah Run is a planned experiment in our experiment schedule, not a stretch goal. We will include at least one additional DMC environment (Cheetah Run) to demonstrate generality of the mechanism.\n\n### R2: \"No proposed solution — what's the actionable takeaway?\"\n**Pre-emption**: Our primary contribution is diagnostic, not prescriptive. The causal chain (C1-C5) provides clear guidance: trained dt_embedding is the necessary condition for CIVO. Restricting imagination to dt=1 eliminates CIVO (Pathway A) but not all failure modes — 008e_v3 shows 1/2 seeds collapse via a distinct non-CIVO pathway (eval-driven policy instability). The actionable takeaway is that trained dt_embedding creates vulnerability through multiple pathways in imagination-based actor-critic; surface-level fixes are insufficient. Hierarchical approaches (THICK, Hieros) that avoid flat dt-conditioning are the recommended direction.\n**Mitigation**: Present the dual-pathway finding as a key diagnostic contribution (Section 5). dt=1 imagination is a partial mitigation (eliminates CIVO) but not robust (1/2 collapse via Pathway B).\n\n### R3: \"Value/eval ratio is a crude proxy for overestimation\"\n**Pre-emption**: We acknowledge this is a proxy, not a formal divergence measure. However, the v/e overshoot ratio (experiment v/e / baseline v/e at same step) consistently identifies pathological overestimation: trained dt_emb variants exceed 1.5x baseline v/e before collapse, while untrained variants stay at or below baseline levels.\n**Mitigation**: Add distributional analysis of value predictions vs. realized returns if time permits.\n\n### R4: \"The factorial design is incomplete — missing cells in the grid\"\n**Pre-emption**: We prioritized the most informative comparisons. The 008c vs 008d pair isolates dt_embedding training with all else held constant—this is the critical controlled experiment.\n**Mitigation**: Explicitly present the factorial grid, mark which cells are filled, and justify the prioritization.\n\n### R5: \"Novelty concern — model exploitation / value overestimation in MBRL is well-known\"\n**Pre-emption**: General MBRL overestimation is known (Janner et al., 2019), but the specific mechanism through temporal abstraction's dt_embedding is novel. Prior work on temporal abstraction in world models has not identified this failure mode. Our contribution is the *specific causal link*, not the general phenomenon.\n\n### R6: \"All results from a single seed — how robust are the conclusions?\"\n**Pre-emption (D032 update)**: Multi-seed data now available. The nve>1.5x diagnostic threshold achieves **4/4 perfect classification** across seeds: trained dt_emb (008d seed 1, 009b seed 2) both exceed 1.5x; untrained (008f seed 1, 009c seed 2) neither exceeds 1.5x. Collapse itself is stochastic (1/2 trained seeds), which we report transparently — our claim is about the reliable **diagnostic marker** (nve>1.5x), not deterministic collapse.\n**[D040]** C6 partially falsified: 008e_v3_s2 collapsed via non-CIVO pathway (eval-driven, entropy ↑). 009a baseline s2 stable (618→596→836) — rules out seed fragility. Trained dt_embedding creates multiple vulnerability pathways: CIVO (Pathway A, 008d) and policy instability (Pathway B, 008e_v3_s2). dt=1 imagination eliminates Pathway A but not B.\n**Mitigation**: Report all seeds with IQM + confidence intervals per Agarwal et al. (2021). The 4/4 separation is robust. Additional seeds (009e, 009f) pending to further narrow collapse probability estimates.\n\n### R7: \"The factorial design confounds dt_embedding training with training data composition\"\n**Pre-emption**: exp_008f confirmed: dt_embed_dim=0 + subsample ON → normalized v/e 0.83x@45K (healthy), vs 008d@45K=2.09x (collapse at v/e=69.3%). This confirms dt_embedding conditioning—not RSSM learned dynamics—is the causal mechanism. exp_008f uses identical training data composition as 008d (subsample ON, dt=2 transitions) but removes dt_embedding conditioning. Through 55K, 008f remained in normal range (normalized v/e 0.73-1.02x).\n**Mitigation**: The 008f causal control resolves this confound definitively. The three-way comparison (008c: subsample OFF/untrained, 008d: subsample ON/trained, 008f: subsample ON/no dt_embed) isolates dt_embedding conditioning as the necessary and sufficient factor for CIVO.\n\n### R8: \"008f reward prediction degradation undermines the causal control\"\n**Pre-emption**: 008f 的 reward_MAE=0.747（baseline 75x）是重要发现，但不削弱 C2 因果结论。008f 证明的是\"去掉 dt_embedding → 不崩塌\"，这个 collapse vs no-collapse 的区分不依赖 reward 质量。008f 的低 v/e 部分源于奖励噪声，但关键对比是 008d 的 v/e 加速发散 vs 008f 的 v/e 保持稳定——模式差异才是因果证据。\n**Mitigation**: 论文中明确区分 008f 的角色（因果控制实验）和 008e_v3 的角色（诊断证据：dt=1 imagination 不能消除 CIVO）。RSSM fidelity 表同时报告绝对误差和比率，避免审稿人被 008d 的低 obs gap ratio（1.30x，但绝对误差 41.31 已是 baseline 3x）误导。\n\n---\n\n## Experiment Coverage Assessment\n\n| Claim | Evidence Status | Gaps |\n|-------|----------------|------|\n| C1 (degradation) | ✅ Strong — 3 configurations (005, 006, 007) all negative | Could add 1 more environment |\n| C2 (reliable pathological overestimation) | ⚠️ Moderate-strong (D032) — nve>1.5x threshold: 4/4 perfect separation (trained 2/2 exceed, untrained 0/2 exceed). Collapse stochastic: 1/2 trained seeds. Causal direction unambiguous. | 009e (008d seed 1) to narrow collapse probability; 009f (008f seed 1) for third untrained seed; 009c→200K |\n| C3 (preventing optimistic drift) | ✅ Strong (105K confirmed) — 008a@105K=778 (85% baseline), normalized v/e=0.59x, two V-shaped recoveries | None — long-term structural suppression confirmed |\n| C4 (dt amplification) | ⚠️ Moderate — only dt=2 vs dt=4 | Would benefit from dt=3, but not blocking |\n| C5 (horizon/subsample) | ⚠️ Moderate — confounded with other factors | Need 008c-equivalent with H=15 + subsample ON + untrained dt_emb for clean isolation |\n| C6 (dt=1 imag as mitigation) | ⚠️ **D040: PARTIALLY FALSIFIED** — s2 collapsed (nve=3.78x) but via non-CIVO pathway (eval-driven, entropy ↑). 009a baseline s2 stable → config-caused. **Repositioned**: trained dt_embedding creates multiple vulnerability pathways; dt=1 imagination eliminates CIVO pathway but not policy instability pathway. Onset delay ~70K steps is a positive finding. | Need s3-s4 to narrow collapse rate. Characterize Pathway B mechanism. Consider: trained dt_emb + subsample OFF + dt=1 imag (no dt>1 in training data) |\n\n### Priority Actions Before Submission\n1. ~~**Check exp_008a@65K**~~ — ✅ Done: 608 (-1.3% baseline), B+ confirmed\n2. ~~**008e_v3 B+ confound**~~ — ✅ Done: 869.9@105K (95% baseline), B+ confirmed\n3. ~~**008f C2 causal control**~~ — ✅ Done: V-recovery to 503@105K, normalized v/e=0.30x\n4. ~~**008e_v3_s2 (seed=2)**~~ — ❌ D040: **COLLAPSED via non-CIVO pathway** (eval-driven, entropy ↑). C6 partially falsified — multiple vulnerability pathways.\n5. ~~**Multi-seed replication**~~ — ✅ D040: 009a baseline s2 DONE (stable). All Walker Walk seeds complete.\n6. ~~**Narrative pivot**~~ — ✅ D040: C6 reframed as \"multiple vulnerability pathways\" (CIVO Pathway A + non-CIVO Pathway B)\n6.5. ~~**SGS (reward_dt_emb_detach) 验证**~~ — ❌ exp_016e: reward_dt_emb_detach 是 no-op（梯度验证揭示 reward gradient = 0 by architecture）。016e 与 008d 的差异（onset timing, peak height）归因于随机变异，非 SGS 效果。dt_emb 的 93% 梯度来自 observation decoders，7% 来自 KL dynamics，reward=0。\n7. **考虑新实验**: trained dt_emb + subsample OFF + dt=1 imag — 如果 stable → 确认 training data dt>1 transitions 是 trigger\n8. **Cheetah Run cross-environment** — P0 priority, addresses R1\n9. **Figures** — learning curves, normalized v/e (含 008e_v3_s2 崩溃轨迹), RSSM fidelity table\n10. **Threshold sensitivity analysis** — plan_034, 0 GPU cost\n\n---\n\n## Key Figures (Planned)\n\n1. **Figure 1**: Learning curves — all experiments overlaid, showing degradation pattern (hero figure)\n2. **Figure 2**: Value/eval ratio over training — separating healthy vs. pathological regimes\n3. **Figure 3**: Factorial grid summary — 2×2 (dt_emb × subsample) with H as secondary axis\n4. **Figure 4**: Causal diagram — dt_embedding → value overestimation → actor degradation → collapse\n5. **Figure 5**: dt=1 vs dt=2 vs dt=4 comparison — monotonic degradation (C4)\n6. **Table 1**: RSSM fidelity — absolute obs_MSE + reward_MAE at dt=1/dt=2, 三模型并排（绝对值 + 比率，避免比率误导）\n7. **Figure 6 (D035)**: Mitigation comparison — plot 008e_v3 seed=1 as single line. Add s2 (seed=2) when done. Do NOT present s0+s1 as two independent seeds. Can footnote deterministic reproduction.\n","numLines":314,"startLine":1,"totalLines":314}}}
